# Changelog

All notable changes to A House Divided will be documented in this file.

---

# Beta 2

## [0.3.6] - 2026-06-27

### Added

- **Structural market rework — capital tier v1 (`marketSystemMode >= "capital"`).** Sectors now own productive **capacity** (output units/day): seeded with 10% headroom at first exposure so flipping the mode on changes nothing, grown by the growth slider (which becomes _investment_), eroded by depreciation (~12% over 5 game years). Capacity gates realized revenue exactly like geological capacity gates extraction — a sector that stops investing slowly loses the ability to produce. New **Capital & Unit Economics** panel surfaces the design loop: capacity vs potential output, utilization (color-coded), depreciation, and an _emergent_ per-unit P&L (market price − labour − inputs − capital charge, with breakeven price in the tooltip) — computed live, never stored. `capitalStock` persists as state; `capitalUtilization` as telemetry.
- **Structural market rework — posted-price market clearing (`marketSystemMode >= "clearing"`).** "Lower your margin to sell more" is now literally true: sectors post a **pricing posture** (−20%…+20% vs market; CEOs via a new Pricing panel + `POST …/sectors/[sectorId]/pricing`; unset sectors auto-position — skim shortages, undercut gluts). Each turn a clearing pre-pass fills every commodity's lagged demand cheapest-first (pro-rata within a posture tier, scaled by corporate sectors' share of aggregate supply); a sector's realized revenue is scaled by `soldFraction × (1+posture) × price leg`, which **subsumes** Tier-1 realization rather than stacking on it. Sub-1 factors fade in over the shared 240-turn ramp; premium upside applies immediately. Telemetry persisted (`clearingFactor`, `soldFraction`, `effectivePosture`) and surfaced in the panel ("N% sold last turn", clearing multiplier, tooltips).
- **Review fixes (automated Kimi review of #2601):** per-row realization guard now rejects non-positive prices (NaN impossible) and reuses the shared `priceRealizationFactor` helper instead of duplicating the clamp; market-mode enum/order/TLDRs centralized in client-safe `src/lib/market/modes.ts` (admin panel, Zod route schema, and engine all derive from it); the Market admin panel surfaces fetch failures ("Unknown" badge + disabled buttons) instead of silently rendering "Off"; `commodityFlows` gets a lazy `{commodity: 1, turn: -1}` index at the write site; tooltip number formatting.
- **Structural market rework — D1 throughput coupling (`marketSystemMode >= "clearing"`).** Every sector's realized output is now limited by its scarcest available input (Leontief): per input, availability = `min(1, marketSupply/marketDemand)` on lagged global balances; the minimum across inputs throttles `hourlyRevenue`, floored at 0.5 and faded in per sector over the capacity haircut's 240-turn ramp so supply chains can adapt. This generalizes the extraction capacity haircut to the whole economy — a steel shortage now physically limits car output instead of only nicking margins. Sector detail shows per-input "% available (input-limited)" lines and an "Input throughput" footer naming the binding input, with tooltips; `throughputFactor`/`throughputBindingInput` persisted for display. The corp phase now threads a `MarketContext` (mirroring `LabourContext`) instead of a lone boolean.
- **Structural market rework — shadow inventory on the flow ledger (`marketSystemMode >= "ledger"`).** Storable commodities now accumulate a global stockpile each price turn (`stock += supply − demand`, floored at 0) with per-commodity carry/spoilage (food 4%/turn, pharma 1%, bulk ores ~0.1%; services and era-appropriate un-storables like energy hold no stock). Stock, days-of-cover, and spoilage are recorded on `commodityFlows` and shown in the commodity page's Market Flows strip (cover color-coded: <6 turns red, >60 amber). Shadow-only by design — stock does not feed prices until the "clearing" tier, so "ledger" remains observability-only.
- **Structural market rework — player + admin UI surfaces for Tiers 1–2.** Sector detail (Commodity Flows panel): each supply row shows "Realizes ×N @ M× base" when price realization is on, and a new "Price realization" footer row shows the sector's projected multiplier with a tooltip explaining the premium/discount mechanic and last turn's applied value. Commodity detail: a "Market Flows" strip (cleared / unmet demand / unsold, with explanatory tooltips) appears when the flow ledger is on. Admin → Economy gets a "Market" sub-tab mirroring the Labour panel: graduated mode buttons with per-tier descriptions, unimplemented tiers disabled and marked "soon", and a sandbox-first warning. All surfaces disappear entirely when `marketSystemMode` is off.
- **Structural market rework Tier 2 (D0) — shadow commodity flow ledger (`marketSystemMode >= "ledger"`).** The commodity-price phase now writes one `commodityFlows` doc per commodity per turn: units supplied/demanded, what would clear (`min(S,D)`), unmet demand, and unsold surplus — globally and per country, at applied prices. Pure observability, zero behaviour change: it shadows the flows the abstract model already implies, giving the later inventory and throughput-coupling phases their substrate and a divergence baseline. Upserted by `{commodity, turn}` (cron-retry safe), pruned past 240 turns like `commodityPriceHistory`. Builder is pure and unit-tested (`src/lib/market/flowLedger.ts`).
- **Structural market rework Tier 1 — price-realized revenue, behind a new graduated `marketSystemMode` flag (audit t806, "Nothing Wants to Sell").** The re-audit at turn 806 confirmed the engine's structural gap: market price never touches a sector's income (commodity conditions only arrive as capped additive margin points), so no actor has any reason to produce into a shortage or stop overproducing into a glut. Tier 1 closes the aggregate loop: when `marketSystemMode >= "realization"`, each sector's _realized_ revenue is scaled by the lagged market price of its output mix — `computePriceRealization` (`src/lib/market/priceRealization.ts`) weights `clamp((prevPrice/base)^0.5, 0.7, 1.5)` by the sector's strategy supply rates, applied as one more multiplier on `hourlyRevenue` exactly like the extraction capacity haircut (stored revenue base untouched, no compounding). At t806 prices: iron miners realize ~+30%, entertainment ~−18%. Prices are lagged one turn (`lookups.priceRatioByCommodity` from the prior turn's `commodityPrices`) and damped+clamped to prevent cobweb oscillation. Admin toggle: `GET/PATCH /api/admin/config/market` (modes `off → realization → ledger → clearing → capital`, mirroring `labourSystemMode`; higher tiers are scaffolding for Fixes 3/2/4 — see `docs/plans/2026-07-03-market-structural-plan.md`). Default `off` = zero economic change. Per-sector `priceRealization` persisted for display/telemetry only.

### Fixed

- **Education spend-response was saturated near its ceiling at every realistic budget level, so education bills barely moved `highSchoolGradRate`/`testPerformance`/etc (ticket #826 item 14; part of a coordinated 6-category spend-response recalibration).** `EDU_SPEND_HALF_SAT` (`src/lib/metricEngine/registry/education.ts`) was a toy value of `1`, against real per-capita education spend that runs $0-$6,515 (US federal FY2023 baseline $806/capita; theoretical max stacking all five `budgetCategory: "education"` bills' top-funding options) — meaning `fundingResponse` returned ~99.9% of its max at any spend a player could realistically reach, confirmed live in production (WA `testPerformance` simBaseline 95.21). Recalibrated to `1300` (near the flagship federal-education-funding bill's own $1,303 max cost), so response now runs ~0% at $0, ~38% at today's baseline, ~50% after just that one bill, and ~83% at the full stacked max — real, perceptible slope across the whole range instead of near-total saturation.
- **Healthcare policy bills barely moved any healthcare outcome metric no matter how much was spent (ticket #826 item 14 follow-up, part of a coordinated 6-category spend-response recalibration).** `HEALTH_SPEND_HALF_SAT` in `src/lib/metricEngine/registry/healthcare.ts` was a toy-scale placeholder (1.5) against REAL per-capita healthcare spend (typically thousands of dollars), so `fundingResponse()` sat pinned above 99.9% of its max at every realistic budget posture (confirmed live: `physicianRate` simBaseline stuck at 5.49-5.50/1k in WA/NY/TX/CA/MS alike, regardless of each state's actual spend). Recalibrated to 4000 — the midpoint of the $0-$9,484/capita range achievable via every healthcare-budgetCategory bill (`src/lib/seeds/reference/legislationTypes.ts`) — so response now varies meaningfully (~59% at the realistic ~$5,798/capita baseline, ~70% at max) as spend changes, instead of being frozen near the ceiling.
- **Infrastructure spending had almost no effect on infrastructure metrics because the diminishing-returns
  response curve was saturated near its ceiling at any realistic budget (ticket #826 item 14 follow-up,
  spend-response recalibration — infrastructure).** `INFRA_SPEND_HALF_SAT`
  (`src/lib/metricEngine/registry/infrastructure.ts`) was a toy value of `2`, against real US per-capita
  infrastructure spend in the hundreds-to-low-thousands (empirically confirmed live in production:
  `roadCondition` simBaseline ~94.8, `waterQuality`/`broadbandAccess` simBaseline ~~99.7-99.9 across states
  with an 8x population range — i.e. funding bills barely moved the needle). Recalibrated to `1200`, near
  the empirically-derived real US baseline (~~$1,238/capita from enacted federal infrastructure bills,
  GDP-cost-scale-adjusted), so response now spans ~51% at baseline to ~81% at the realistic achievable max
  (~$5,066/capita) — a genuine ~30-point spread a player's infrastructure bills can move.
- **Environment spending bills barely moved their target metrics because the spend-response
  curve was saturated near its ceiling across the entire realistic per-capita range (ticket #826
  item 14 follow-up, coordinated recalibration across education/healthcare/infrastructure/
  publicSafety/environment/social).** `ENV_SPEND_HALF_SAT` in
  `src/lib/metricEngine/registry/environment.ts` was `1.5` against REAL per-capita environment
  spend of ~$108 (2019-default enacted `us_clean_energy` + `us_conservation` positions) up to
  ~$743 at the ceiling (all three US environment bills maxed, per `withPerCapitaCosts` in
  `src/lib/seeds/reference/legislationTypes.ts`) — pinning `envResp()` to ~98.6%-99.8% of max
  everywhere in the achievable range (confirmed live: `airQuality` simBaseline 69-99.8 and
  `carbonEmissions` simBaseline 5.3-7.1 across WA/NY/TX/CA/MS). Raised to `200` (~35% response at
  the realistic baseline, ~79% at the ceiling), restoring real slope so a policy bill actually
  moves the metric.
- **Public-safety spending (police, prisons, criminal justice bills) barely moved any public-safety
  metric no matter how much a player spent (ticket #826 item 14 follow-up, spend-response
  recalibration pass).** `publicSafety.ts`'s funding-response curve (`PS_SPEND_HALF_SAT`) was tuned
  to a toy half-saturation value of `1`, while real per-capita spend achievable via the four
  publicSafety-tagged bills (federal law-enforcement/criminal-justice + prison rehab, state policing
  - state prison rehab) ranges from $418 to $1935/capita — meaning every realistic spend level
    already sat at ~99.9% of the curve's maximum response (confirmed live: NY/CA
    `policePerCapita.simBaseline` at 4.36 out of a ~4.5 max), so a bill moving spend anywhere within
    that real range had almost no effect on `policePerCapita`, `crimeRate`, or the metrics that flow
    from them. `PS_SPEND_HALF_SAT` is now `900` (the geometric mean of the real range), giving ~32%
    response at the cheapest real spend level, ~48% at a moderate/center spend, and ~68% at the
    priciest — genuine slope across the whole playable range instead of a flat near-100% line.
- **Social-policy bills (Social Security, welfare) barely moved child poverty, income inequality,
  recidivism, pension stability, military readiness, or the national poverty rate, regardless of how
  much a bill spent (ticket #826 item 14 follow-up — spend-response recalibration, social
  category).** All six of those metrics read the same shared `{spending: "social"}` funding-response
  channel (`src/lib/metricEngine/registry/social.ts` x2, `publicSafety.ts` recidivismRate,
  `governance.ts` rentenStabilitaet + militaryReadiness, `economic.ts` povertyRate), whose
  half-saturation constant (`1.5`) was calibrated against toy per-capita values while real spend is in
  the thousands of dollars — the real US federal baseline (`$900B` Social Security / 333M population,
  `src/lib/seeds/reference/budgets.ts`) is ~$2,702.70/capita, and the only US lever
  (`us_social_security`) spans $0-$5,303/capita — so the response sat at ~99.94-99.97% (essentially
  maxed) across the entire real range a player could reach. The constant (now the shared
  `SOCIAL_SPEND_HALF_SAT` export) is raised to `2700` (≈ the real federal per-capita baseline), so the
  unlegislated default now sits at ~50% response and the real bill range spans ~0-66% — social/welfare
  legislation now has a meaningful, visible effect on these six outcomes.
- **Region labels on the US map landed in the ocean or in the wrong state (ticket #0861).**
  `featureCentroid` placed each region's label at the flat mean of every vertex across every ring
  of its geometry, with no regard for area or which ring belonged to which landmass. That breaks
  badly for any state with a multi-part or highly irregular coastline — Michigan's two peninsulas,
  Hawaii's separate islands, Louisiana's and Washington's bayous/inlets, Maryland/Florida/New York's
  complex shorelines — since a small offshore or secondary part's handful of corner points pulled the
  averaged point out into open water between the parts, regardless of how much bigger the main
  landmass was. Labels now use the proper area-weighted centroid of the SINGLE LARGEST ring in the
  geometry (the state's main body for a `MultiPolygon`, ignoring small islands/exclaves for label
  placement purposes), instead of averaging every ring's points together.
  (`src/components/maps/RegionalGeoMap.tsx`)

- **Two education-funding laws silently double-counted their effect on engine-derived readouts
  like HS graduation rate (ticket #826 item 14, partial fix).** `us_state_education_funding` (US
  states) and `cn_provincial_education` (CN provinces) each still carried a direct weighted push on
  `highSchoolGradRate`/`testPerformance`/`workforceSkill`/etc. even though those metrics are now
  engine-derived from the education spending channel — the same double-count class an earlier P2a
  sweep (§4.7) removed from their federal/national siblings, but these two slipped through because
  they rely on the `budgetCategory` fallback (from `policyDomain`) rather than an explicit field,
  which the original sweep guard didn't check. Both laws now carry only their root `educationSpending`
  effect, matching `us_federal_education_funding`; the sweep guard test now also checks that
  fallback so this class of bug can't silently reappear. **Not a complete fix** — the spend-response
  curve driving these readouts is separately saturated at real production spending levels
  (near-zero slope over a state bill's entire possible range), which needs a game-balance decision,
  not a mechanical patch; that recalibration is tracked separately.
  (`src/lib/seeds/reference/legislationTypes.ts`, `src/lib/seeds/cn/cnLegislationTypes.ts`,
  `src/lib/seeds/reference/educationSpendingSweep.test.ts`)

- **A registry node's internal EMA baseline could drift outside its own declared bounds and get
  stuck there forever, eventually producing logically-impossible metric states (ticket #826 item
  12, e.g. `publicSafety.crimeRate` reading 0 while `violentCrimeRate`/`incarcerationRate` — which
  should be subsets of it — read far higher).** `evalNode`'s coexistence primitive
  (`src/lib/metricEngine/coexistence.ts`) clamped the final blended `value` to `node.bounds` but
  never clamped the internal `newSimBaseline` the next turn's policy delta is computed against, so
  a baseline that drifted out of range (verified live in production: `crimeRate` simBaseline of
  -1676 against bounds `[0, 15000]`; `publicSafetyConfidence` simBaseline of ~144 against bounds
  `[0, 100]`) could never self-correct. `newSimBaseline` is now clamped to `node.bounds` the same
  way `value` already is, applying to every registry node in the engine.

- **Election-debate challenge prompts could persist and still be actionable after the election they
  belonged to had already ended (ticket #826 item 11).** `DebateSession` documents created by
  `processDebateChallenges` only checked the election's status at creation time; once created, the
  session lived independently for up to its own 12h real-time strategy-pick deadline, regardless of
  what happened to the election in that window. Since turns process hourly in production, that
  window easily spans a primary resolving, a candidate withdrawing, or the general election itself
  resolving — leaving a stale "Debate challenge" card with a live countdown and a working "Commit
  strategy" button on the Actions page for a race that no longer exists. `primaryResolution.ts` (when
  a primary loser is eliminated) and every election-finalization site in `generalResolution.ts` now
  call the new `voidDebateSessionsForElection` to immediately flip any still-`awaitingStrategies`
  session for that election to `expired` (no favorability/XP effects applied, since the outcome no
  longer corresponds to a live race). (`src/lib/debate/debateSessionLifecycle.ts`,
  `src/lib/db/types/debateSession.ts`, `src/lib/turn/primaryResolution.ts`,
  `src/lib/turn/election/generalResolution.ts`)

- **State organization build throttle was wall-clock based, causing the "already built this turn" check
  to last roughly one-and-a-half turns instead of one (ticket #870).** The route gated the atomic
  upsert on `updatedAt < now - 60 minutes`, so a build early in an hour-long turn locked the player
  out until the same wall-clock minute in the following turn. The cutoff is now the current turn
  boundary from `gameState.lastTurnProcessed`, giving the intended "at most +1 per state per turn"
  behavior. (`src/app/api/political-operations/state-org/build/route.ts`)

- **`seedLegislationTypes` reseeding was destructive and admin-hostile (investigated for ticket
  #0860's "UK can't legislate agriculture/technology" report).** The seeder always dropped and
  reinserted the entire `legislationTypes` collection, and only ever ran from the admin reset/setup
  flow. Live-DB verification against production found the UK agriculture/technology entries the
  ticket referenced are already present and correctly served — this wasn't the cause of that
  specific report — but the destructive pattern is a real latent risk: any future reference entry
  added between resets would silently never reach already-running worlds, and every reseed silently
  wiped any admin-authored custom law type (`source: "admin"`) with no warning. The seeder now
  upserts by `_id` (safe to run against a live world at any time to sync in new reference entries)
  and explicitly preserves admin-authored types instead of deleting them.
  (`src/lib/admin/seed/seedLegislationTypes.ts`)

- **State party chair permanently stuck "not vacant" after the chair retired or left the party
  (ticket #0860).** Retiring or leaving a party cleared _national_ party leadership (chair/vice
  chair/treasurer) but never cleared the equivalent _state_-level seats in `statePartyOrg` — so a
  departed state chair left a stale `chairId` behind, and the leadership route's vacancy check kept
  rejecting every new appointment with "State chair position is not vacant" even though nobody
  actually held the seat anymore. Both `retireCharacter.ts` and the party-leave route now clear
  `statePartyOrg.chairId`/`viceChairId`/`treasurerId` (in addition to the `campaignerId` clear that
  already existed) for every state row the departing character held a seat in.
  (`src/lib/retireCharacter.ts`, `src/app/api/country/[code]/parties/[id]/leave/route.ts`)

- **Corporation Tech tab (ticket #0869) — old, inert decades weren't visually distinguished from
  the current/previous decade that still applies effects.** Only the current and immediately
  previous decade's tech bonuses are actually active (`getSectorTechEffectsForYear`); older decades
  are permanent baseline history with no ongoing effect, but the tab showed them identically to
  live decades, so a corp that had committed a lane there looked like it could still switch tracks.
  History cards for any decade older than "current − 1" now show a blurred "Historical decade — no
  longer providing bonuses" overlay. Permanently-unlocked production methods (which persist regardless
  of decade age) are unaffected and continue to surface in the always-visible "Production Methods"
  overview card.
  (`src/components/corporation/TechTab.tsx`)

- **Index fund redemption queue (ticket #0857) — discarded status lost partial-payout visibility.**
  Investigation confirmed the reported cash never actually went missing — the redemption fully paid
  out over the following ~16 hours via the hourly cron, and the player had misread the fund's
  fund-wide, all-holders "Unit redemption" income-statement line as their own personal payout
  receipt. Along the way found a real defect: the redeem route computed the correct queue-entry
  status (`"partial"` when some units were paid immediately before the rest queued) but discarded
  it, hardcoding `status: "queued"` at insert time — so a partially-paid entry was indistinguishable
  from an untouched one, and the cron's own payout loop had the same gap. Both write paths now use
  the already-computed status. Also clarified the fund Income Statement subtitle to state it's a
  fund-wide, all-holders total, not a personal transaction record, to prevent this confusion recurring.
  (`src/app/api/investment-funds/[slug]/redeem/route.ts`, `src/lib/indexFunds/fundRedemptionQueue.ts`,
  `src/components/indexFunds/FundFinancialsTab.tsx`)

- **Presidential primary endorsements (ticket #0868) — stored candidate id, not character id.**
  The `playerEndorsements` collection was keyed to the candidate's `characterId`, while the
  presidential primary standings page joins on the candidate row `_id` (`candidateId`). This
  made the "Endorsed" column always read as zero and the Switch button never recognize the
  viewer's active endorsement. `POST /api/elections/[id]/endorse` now writes
  `candidateId: candidate._id`, and every other consumer of `playerEndorsements.candidateId`
  was updated to join on the row id too: campaign action-per-turn endorsement counts
  (`campaignQueries.ts`, `campaignTurn.ts`), the general-election endorsement badge/Switch
  button (`GeneralElectionPanel.tsx`, `GeneralElectionNoTallyPanel.tsx`,
  `PresidentialCandidateTable.tsx`), the enriched-candidate endorsement list
  (`candidateEnrichment.ts`, also used by the Discord `/race` command), character-rename
  propagation (`propagateNameChange.ts`), and `retireCharacter.ts` cleanup.
  (`src/app/api/elections/[id]/endorse/route.ts`, `src/lib/retireCharacter.ts`,
  `src/lib/campaigns/queries/campaignQueries.ts`, `src/lib/turn/campaignTurn.ts`,
  `src/lib/elections/candidateEnrichment.ts`, `src/lib/propagateNameChange.ts`)

- **Global leaderboard (ticket #0863) — nation median income wasn't converted to the
  display currency.** Unlike campaign wealth / corp market cap, `medianIncome` was
  never normalized to internal units server-side, and the client never passed a
  currency code when rendering country-scope metrics — so the leaderboard showed each
  nation's raw local-currency figure regardless of the currency toggle (e.g. Japan's
  ¥ median income appearing far larger than Nigeria's ₦ one with no relation to real
  purchasing power). `medianIncome` is now converted via the same `toInternalAmount`
  path as other cross-country monetary metrics, and `CountryLeaderboardEntry` carries
  a `nativeCurrencyCode` so the client can complete the conversion to whatever
  currency the viewer has selected.
  (`src/lib/world/globalLeaderboard.ts`, `src/lib/world/globalLeaderboardTypes.ts`,
  `src/app/world/leaderboard/LeaderboardClient.tsx`)

- **Private sale offers invisible to the seller (ticket #0859).** A private-share listing read
  on the Shares tab filtered active listings by wall-clock `expiresAt`, while the offer-submit
  guard, the listing-reservation query, and the turn processor all use turn-based `expiresAtTurn`
  (which freezes on pause). When real time outran the turn cadence (slow turns or a paused game),
  a listing could be wall-clock-expired but turn-alive: buyers could still place offers — and the
  seller got the "new offer" notification — but the listing was hidden from the seller's panel
  ("No offers yet"), so the offer could never be accepted. The read now uses the same turn-first
  filter (`shareListings.ts`), and character listings now stamp `expiresAtTurn` like corp listings
  so all paths agree (`createShareListing.ts`).

- **Org imbalance (ticket #0762) — catchup now additive, not multiplicative.** A trailing
  party with less PS was getting `psLeverage(0.5) × catchup(1.5) = 0.75` — worse than no
  catchup at all. The catch-up bonus is now added to rawLeverage (`0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0`) so
  the underdog reaches neutral rather than being penalised for falling behind.
  (`buildOrgGain.ts`, `BUILD_ORG_PS_EFFECTIVE_CEILING = 2.0`; new max tighter than old
  `1.5 × 1.5 = 2.25`.)

- **Org imbalance (ticket #0762) — national PS blend fraction halved (0.1 → 0.05).** A
  4.4× national PS gap (e.g. 252 vs 57) was adding +25.3 vs +5.7 to each party's blended PS
  in every state, permanently pinning psLeverage at its floor/ceiling. At 0.05 the contribution
  halves, leaving room for the ratio to fall between the clamps in normal play. Combined with
  the additive catchup fix, the per-click org gain ratio drops from 2× to ~1.32×.

- **Org imbalance (ticket #0762) — national PS recovery on national-scoped org builds.**
  Spending national PS to build org now refunds 20% back to the national pool (capped at
  `NATIONAL_PS_CAP`), so treasury-poor parties can recover national PS through active play
  rather than pure investment. Effective net cost: 0.8 PS per national Build Org click.

- **Admin ps-spend-scope tests added** to lock in the role-based eligibility fix from
  commit `59978b832` — three new test cases cover admin with no role, admin as national
  chair, and admin as dual chair.

### Added

- **Bond-default restructuring + closing the "close the tab" loophole.** A defaulted
  corporation previously faced only a 96-turn credit penalty with no forced settlement,
  so a CEO could default, close the tab, wait out the penalty, keep every sector, and
  leave bondholders holding 10¢ paper indefinitely. New **Restructure** option in the
  defaulted-bond crisis modal liquidates the _minimum, highest-value_ sectors needed to
  repay defaulted bondholders **in full** — at an 85% orderly-sale recovery rate
  (`RESTRUCTURE_SECTOR_SALVAGE_FRACTION`, vs the 20% dissolution fire-sale) — then cures
  the bonds, keeping the corp alive on its remaining sectors and cash; only offered when
  the value is actually there (otherwise the CEO must dissolve & settle). The turn tick
  now **auto-restructures** any corp still defaulted from a prior turn when feasible, so
  walking away no longer dodges payment. Adds `POST /api/corporations/[id]/bond-default/restructure`,
  a restructure preview on the crisis `GET`, a `corporation_restructured` wire event, and
  new libs `src/lib/bonds/restructure.ts` + `executeCorporationBondRestructure.ts`.

- **Admin homepage-preview of the Cold-War-1979 landing redesign.** New admin-only
  route `/admin/homepage-preview` (gated by `getAuthAdmin`) renders a bordered,
  theme-sweepable preview of the in-development 1979-era landing so the redesign can be
  reviewed without touching the live `/`. A signed-out / signed-in toggle swaps the whole
  nav shell (`PublicMarketingNav` ↔ real `NavbarWrapper`) and flips the CTAs; a sticky
  parallax hero holds a larger, Iron-Curtain-zoomed globe with bloc coloring
  (NATO / Comintern / Non-Aligned / Disabled), atmosphere, great-circle arcs, hotspots,
  and an ease-to-center on click; an opaque content drawer rises over the fixed hero; and
  an asymmetric magazine bento replaces the uniform card grids with frosted
  Wikimedia-on-CDN hover images. All new globe / `MapSVGContent` overlay props default
  OFF, so `/world` and the broadsheet render unchanged. Adds pure helpers
  `src/components/landing/globeEnhancements.ts` (arcs / hotspots / starfield) and
  `blocColors.ts` (1979 bloc map + token-only colors), a `CDN_WORLD_GEO_URL` /
  `cdnLanding` CDN helper, an R2 upload script + provenance manifest for the eight card
  images (`scripts/upload-landing-images-to-r2.mjs`), and the preview client at
  `src/app/admin/homepage-preview/`. The hover images resolve once the manifest is
  uploaded to the R2 CDN; until then the tiles degrade gracefully to the frosted overlay.

- **Presidential primary delegate map mode (suggestion #0087).** The party primary page
  map now has a `Leader` / `Delegates` toggle. `Delegates` mode labels each state with its
  total delegate count and shows a per-candidate delegate breakdown in the tooltip, using the
  same canonical projection that feeds the standings table. This lets players see at a glance
  where the big delegate prizes are without clicking every state detail page.
  (`src/components/PrimaryElectoralMap.tsx`, `src/app/president/primary/[partyId]/PrimaryMapWithLinks.tsx`,
  `src/app/president/primary/[partyId]/page.tsx`, `src/components/PrimaryElectoralMap.test.tsx`)

### Security

- **Removed committed database credentials.** `scripts/seed-sandbox.ts` and
  `scripts/bootstrap-safe.ts` no longer carry live MongoDB connection strings — they
  read `MONGODB_URI` from the environment and fail fast when it is unset. (The
  previously-exposed Atlas and Railway credentials must be rotated.)
- **Closed an SVG-upload stored-XSS vector.** Party-logo uploads no longer accept
  `image/svg+xml`, and the local uploads route serves any already-stored SVG with
  `Content-Disposition: attachment` plus a locked-down per-response CSP so it cannot
  execute as a top-level document. Added a global report-only `Content-Security-Policy`.
- **Blocked SSRF in the public image proxy.** User-supplied image URLs are validated
  against a private/reserved-IP block list (cloud metadata, RFC1918, loopback) after
  DNS resolution, and redirects are refused so they cannot escape the check. New
  `src/lib/security/ssrfGuard.ts` with a 27-case regression test.
- **Required admin auth on two admin GET endpoints** (`admin/config/init`,
  `admin/officials/init`) that returned config / elected-official data with no auth.
- **Logout now revokes the session server-side.** Logging out stamps `authRevokedAt`,
  so any token issued before logout is rejected immediately — a token captured before
  logout can no longer be used until cookie expiry.
- **Rate-limited universal search.** `search/universal` runs case-insensitive regex
  across many collections; it now enforces a per-user/IP limit (30/min) and ignores
  queries shorter than two characters, closing a cheap DoS-amplification vector.

### Fixed

- **Foreign-currency bond values no longer display wildly inflated.** Bond holdings on
  the corp Bonds tab and holder values on the bond detail page were computed from
  `BOND_UNIT_FACE_VALUE × marketPrice` in the bond's own `currencyCode` and returned
  without converting to anchor units — JPY bonds showed ~100× too high, GBP a smaller
  systematic error. Both now normalize through `corpCapitalToAnchor`.
- **Abandoning a CEO no longer orphans the corp's bond debt.** Deleting a CEO character
  only vacated the seat, leaving any outstanding bonds unpayable: the corp became a
  permanent zombie (dissolve and takeover both refuse while bonds exist) and its held
  shares were dumped to public float instead of settling creditors. CEO-led corps that
  still owe bondholders now run the full bond-default dissolution waterfall on
  abandonment (bondholders paid first, remainder incl. cross-equity distributed in-kind
  to shareholders, sectors restored to Unowned, corp deleted); corps with no outstanding
  bonds keep the lightweight vacate path, and national/IMF corps are never auto-dissolved.
- **Made silent non-atomic money flows visible.** A boot-time check alerts (Sentry, in
  production) when MongoDB lacks transaction support, and `runWithOptionalTransaction`
  warns the first time it falls back to non-transactional writes — so settlement running
  without atomicity on a standalone Mongo is no longer invisible.

### Performance

- **Added order-book indexes** for `shareOrders`, `shareListings`, and `currencyOrders`,
  keyed from the live query predicates — share and forex trades no longer scan these
  collections as the order books grow.

### Internal

- **Architecture audit now blocks hardcoded DB credentials** in `src/` and `scripts/`
  (`npm run architecture:audit`) to prevent re-introducing committed secrets.

## [0.3.5] - 2026-06-23

### Added

- **Redistricting display overhaul + one-click Auto-Map.** District cards now lead
  with a **horizontal stacked composition bar** (left / grey / right, counts inline when
  the segment is wide enough, otherwise on hover) and a prominent **net-lean indicator**
  next to the district name; the old 4×4 grid is preserved behind a "Show grid" toggle.
  The card grid gains a **sort control** (most competitive, lean left→right, lean
  right→left) and a new **statewide composition summary** (`DistrictCompositionSummary`)
  — districts bucketed into Safe Left / Lean Left / Competitive / Lean Right / Safe Right
  (thresholds exported from `cardView.ts`), with counts, percentages, a seat projection,
  and the efficiency gap vs its cap. The map editor adds an **Auto-Map toolbar** with six
  deterministic strategies (`Competitive`, `Fair`, `Lean Left/Right`, `Max Left/Right`)
  that generate a conserved, legal map in one click — each backs off to the nearest legal
  map when the caps bind ("Adjusted to meet compactness/fairness rules"), plus
  **Undo** and **Reset to current map**, a before/after composition preview, and a
  2-column paint grid on mobile. New: `src/lib/redistricting/autoMap.ts`,
  `DistrictCompositionSummary.tsx`, `DistrictLeanBar.tsx`.

- **Organization emblems.** UN, EU, and NATO now show their real flags in the hero seal
  (referenced from Wikimedia Commons, like the app's state flags and executive seals).
  Player-created orgs can upload their own logo during creation (PNG/JPEG/WebP/GIF,
  ≤ 2 MB, downscaled to a 256² square) via `POST /api/upload/org-logo`; the URL is stored
  on the org and rendered in its seal, falling back to the accent-tinted glyph when absent.
  (SVG is intentionally not accepted — it would be served same-origin and is an XSS vector.)

- **International Organizations IA — index + per-org pages.** The single tabbed
  page is replaced by an **overview/index** (`/world/international-organizations`)
  — an org directory grouped by category, your-delegations panel, the
  found-new-org form, and a **cross-org resolutions feed** (aggregated client-side
  from the world view, no new API) — plus **deep-linkable per-org routes**
  (`/[orgId]/overview`, `/[orgId]/flagship`) under a shared layout. orgId in URLs
  is lowercase, resolved case-insensitively (built-ins `eu`/`nato`/`un`; custom =
  the lowercased short name). The masthead's sub-tabs are now routed links; the
  old `InternationalOrganizationsClient`/`MyMembershipsTab`/`PlayerOrgsTab` shell
  is retired.

- **International Organizations world map.** The index now opens with a flat
  (equal-area) world map: an org selector shades the selected organization's
  member countries in its accent colour (member / non-member legend), and
  clicking a country opens a card listing the organizations it belongs to, each
  linked to its page. The ISO-numeric→CountryId mapping is extracted to a shared
  `src/lib/constants/countryIso.ts` helper (re-exported from `commodity-map` for
  back-compat); pure `orgMembersByCountry` / `isCountryInOrg` drive the shading.

- **Organization funds are denominated in the founding country's currency.** An
  org's pooled treasury (`organizationFunds`) now stores `balanceLocal` +
  `currencyCountryId` (the first founding member — EU → euro via Germany, NATO/UN
  → USD, custom orgs → their founder's currency) instead of a flat USD balance.
  Dues in, aid out, the fund provision, and agency-funding draws all convert
  to/from this currency via each country's `usdExchangeRate` (`convertLocal`,
  `resolveOrgFundCurrencyCountry`); aid amounts are entered in the fund's
  currency. The masthead, dues, aid, agency, and flagship surfaces display the
  fund's currency symbol. Built-ins are numerically unchanged (DE/US/UK sit at
  rate 1.0); custom orgs founded by non-parity currencies store that currency.

- **International Organization membership bills (Foreign Policy).** A new
  `international_organization` bill provision (the first sub-typed provision)
  routes org membership actions through the national legislature on a simple
  majority, with three sub-types — **join**, **fund**, **leave**:
  - **Join** now runs two gates in parallel: applying from the org page raises a
    domestic **Join bill** (majority) alongside the existing **member vote**
    (unanimous). Membership is granted only if BOTH pass; if either finishes as a
    failure, a coordination arbiter (`resolveJoinApplication`) cancels the still-
    pending counterpart with a recorded reason (member vote ↔ linked bill are
    cross-linked via `domesticBillId`/`membershipProposalId`, polled each turn).
  - **Fund** (members only): a member raises an appropriation bill to send a
    chosen amount of its treasury to the org's pooled fund. On enactment the
    member treasury is debited (member currency) and the fund credited in the
    fund's currency (`convertLocal`, `creditOrganizationFund`) at no FX cost;
    voids if the country left the org before enactment. Spends into debt if the
    treasury can't cover it.
  - **Leave** is consolidated onto the provision (`subType: "leave"`): the
    withdrawal removal logic was extracted into a shared `removeOrganizationMembership`
    used by both the new provision and the legacy `internationalAction` path; the
    separate FTA-withdrawal path is unchanged, and in-flight legacy leave bills
    still resolve. `billRequiresExecutiveAction` recognizes the provision so
    org-leave bills keep auto-resolving.

- **International Organizations redesign + diplomatic-action budget** — `/world/international-organizations`
  rebuilt onto a per-org dossier masthead (seal, derived metric strip, acting chip) with **Overview**
  and **Flagship** sub-tabs; org selection stays in the page tab bar (no in-masthead switcher). New
  per-country **diplomatic-action budget** (`DIPLOMATIC_ACTIONS_PER_TURN = 4`, lazy turn-refresh) gates
  org-level initiating actions — FTA proposal, membership application, leadership nomination — while
  voting stays free (`propose-leave` is excluded; it already carries its own legislature-bill cost).
  Backbone generalizes org legislation into a typed **resolution** model (`free_trade_agreement` kept;
  `sanctions` / `directive` / `joint_statement` / `aid_package` modelled with passage-rule + turn
  dispatch ready — effects land in Phase 2). Derived, USD-normalized GDP-share metrics (contribution %,
  influence index, bloc standing, notional budget) power the member table and metric strip; new per-org
  identity/palette registry (built-ins fixed, customs deterministically derived). Visuals built on
  semantic tokens + project fonts so all themes keep working. Every org now carries a shared
  **category** (political / economic / security / development), fixed at creation, that determines its
  resolution powers, drives a category-based **Flagship** template (built-ins are instances; customs
  get a matching one), and sets the masthead group label — built-ins preset (UN=political,
  NATO=security, EU=economic), customs choose it in the create form. **Sanctions resolutions**
  (Phase 2): economic/security orgs can table a per-commodity sanctions resolution against a target
  country; on majority passage every member enacts an org-origin embargo (`TradeEmbargo.origin`
  "organization") on that commodity. Resolution voting is now type-aware (FTA → named parties; all
  other types → majority of members). The **UN permanent-member veto** is live (a founding member's
  "no" blocks a UN resolution). Proposal types are gated by `canTableResolutionType` (category powers
  plus implemented effects). Sanctions **auto-expire** after `SANCTIONS_DURATION_TURNS`. **Aid
  packages** (political/economic/development): a member tables an aid package (recipient member +
  USD amount); on majority passage it disburses from the **org fund** to the recipient's treasury
  (USD-converted via `usdExchangeRate`) — failing if the fund is short — and grants the recipient a
  one-time, log-scaled, capped **GDP-growth boost** (the metric engine smooths it back).
  **Org fund + dues:** each org has a pooled treasury (`organizationFunds`) capitalized by per-turn
  member dues (a GDP-scaled assessment, USD-normalized); the dues rate is **member-voted** via a new
  `set_dues` resolution (any category) and stored per-org (default `DEFAULT_ORG_DUES_RATE_ANNUAL`,
  capped at `MAX_ORG_DUES_RATE_ANNUAL`). The masthead shows the live fund balance + dues rate.
  **Directives** (economic orgs): a member tables a standing bloc directive from a curated catalog
  (`DIRECTIVE_CATALOG`); on majority passage it applies a bounded, server-controlled metric nudge to
  **every member state** for `DIRECTIVE_DURATION_TURNS` (then auto-expires), fed through the per-state
  metric turn driver's `targetNudges` (the same seam economic-model synergies use, so the metric
  settles smoothly and relaxes when the directive lifts). The catalog targets curated, node-backed
  metrics — productivity growth, cost of living, income inequality, social cohesion, carbon emissions —
  with directional, balance-bounded deltas. **Joint statements** (political / security orgs): a member
  tables an endorsement or condemnation of any subject country; on majority passage it applies a
  bounded, time-limited government-approval effect to that country (endorse +2, condemn −3) for
  `JOINT_STATEMENT_DURATION_TURNS`, folded into the per-turn approval snapshot recompute (the same
  mechanism as the governor State-of-the-State bump, via `getActiveOrgStatementModifiersByCountry`), so
  it persists across turns and lifts cleanly when the statement lapses. At the UN, a permanent member's
  "no" still vetoes. This completes the Phase 2A resolution set (FTA, sanctions, aid, dues, directive,
  joint statement). **Security flagship — alert posture + defense pledge** (Phase 2B): a security
  alliance's members vote (a `set_posture` resolution) to move the bloc between **Reduced / Standard /
  Heightened / Article 5**; each posture applies a bounded, benefit-with-a-cost metric effect to every
  member while in force (higher alert lifts `militaryReadiness` but drags `civilLiberties`, and Article
  5 adds an economic-growth drag), fed through the metric turn driver's `targetNudges` like directives
  (standard = parity). The alliance Flagship now shows the live posture and each member's **real
  defense spending as a % of GDP** against the 2% pledge target (met / below), computed from the
  federal defense outlay ÷ national GDP. The **Economic (single-market)** and **Development**
  flagships are now live dashboards too: the market flagship lists the org's directives in force (with
  lift countdowns) and active free-trade agreements; the development flagship shows the pooled fund
  balance and the aid packages financed from it (the "project portfolio"), replacing the earlier
  illustrative placeholders. **Political flagship — agency funding** (Phase 2B): a political org's
  members vote (a `fund_agency` resolution) to stand up a global programme from a curated catalog
  (`AGENCY_CATALOG` — humanitarian relief, development, climate, cultural/education); on passage a
  fixed cost is drawn from the pooled fund (fails gracefully if short) and, while funded, the
  programme applies a bounded, node-backed member-wide metric benefit for `AGENCY_FUNDING_DURATION_TURNS`
  via the metric turn driver's `targetNudges` (then lapses). The assembly Flagship lists funded
  programmes with a lapse countdown and the pooled-fund balance. This completes all four Phase 2B
  category flagships (security alert posture, economic directives/FTAs, development portfolio,
  political agency funding).

- **Crisis interaction system admin toggle** — the `crisisInteractionEnabled` flag can now be flipped
  from the admin UI instead of only by editing the `gameState` document directly. New
  `POST /api/admin/crises/toggle` route (`requireAdmin`, mirrors the PREE
  `/api/admin/events/pree/toggle` pattern, stamps `crisisInteractionEnabledBy` /
  `crisisInteractionEnabledAt`), surfaced as an **Interactions: On/Off** button in the admin Crises
  tab (`CrisesManager.tsx`). When off, only the legacy manual crisis creator is available; when on,
  template-based creation, decision trees, collective contributions, and ambient interaction cards
  (the full crisis interaction system) become available.
- **US House districted redistricting (built, flag-gated off)** — a complete replacement for the statewide "one general election magically splits into seats" model, behind the new `redistrictingEnabled` gameState flag (admin-toggleable on the dashboard; off by default, so the live game is unchanged). When on, each US state's House delegation renders as **district cards** of 16 voter squares colored by **ideological pool** — left/right/grey aggregated from _every_ party's registration and lean (multiparty, no hardcoded Dem/Rep), seeded from Cook PVI (modern) or a deterministic spread (1991/at-large), with the statewide pool totals conserved as a fixed square budget. House elections resolve **per district**: the statewide tally is shrunk to `1/N` and shifted by each district's square-derived lean (`LEAN_STRENGTH`), so a packed district is a safe seat; primaries advance the **top 3 per party**, with survivors assigned to districts by primary share. A governing **trifecta** (governor + state-senate majority) can **redraw the map** at census via a paint-and-conserve editor with a live legality preview, gated by three new state laws — **Redistricting Authority** (who may draw + commission auto-neutralize), **Compactness** (per-district deviation + packed caps), and **Electoral Fairness** (efficiency-gap ceiling, plus a few-point baseline-approval drag while a live map is unfair) — all riding the existing legislature flow. **Campaign Here** lets a party spend political strength to boost a single district up to +7.5% (candidate spender 2× an ally), decaying 0.5%/turn. Census reapportionment regenerates changed-seat and commission maps. Admin-toggleable from the dashboard (`POST /api/admin/redistricting/toggle`). Fully test-covered (79 unit tests); zero behavior change while the flag is off. Not yet enabled in any world.

### Changed

- **Redistricting compactness deviation tiers widened.** The per-district deviation
  caps (how far a district's lean may stray from the state mean) are now Strict 4 /
  Moderate 8 / Loose 12 (were 3 / 6 / 10). The old caps held a leaning state's pickups
  to thin, competitive-band wins even on a "Max" redraw (a +2 ceiling on a mean −3.8
  state), so no visible lean/safe districts ever appeared; at Moderate a redraw can now
  express a real, bounded gerrymander while the efficiency gap stays the primary
  guardrail. Key file: `src/lib/redistricting/caps.ts`.

- **Redistricting fairness-law tiers loosened so gerrymanders actually bite.** The
  statewide efficiency-gap ceilings are now Strict 0.10 / Moderate 0.20 / Loose 0.35
  (were 0.07 / 0.12 / 0.20). The raw efficiency gap penalizes lopsided outcomes even
  when they reflect a state's natural lean, so the old tight ceilings prevented even
  leaning states from electing to their lean — a "Max" redraw barely moved a seat.
  With the new tiers, at Loose a leaning state sweeps and a balanced one swings to 4-1;
  the default Moderate tier produces solid (≈3-1 on a balanced state) maps; Strict still
  acts as a real anti-gerrymander guardrail. Deviation/compactness caps are unchanged
  (they were never the binding constraint). Key file: `src/lib/redistricting/caps.ts`.

### Fixed

- **Redistricting no longer crashes the editor for strongly-leaning, low-grey states.**
  `buildDefaultSquares`' overflow repair reduced a district's squares by tilt _weight_
  rather than by which pool actually overflowed, so a heavily one-sided budget with
  little grey (e.g. a deep-red/blue state in an older era) produced invalid districts
  like `{left:-15, right:31}`. That negative count then threw a `RangeError` in
  `buildSquareCells` (`new Array(-15)`) and took down the whole redistrict editor with a
  generic error page. The repair now reduces the larger (always-positive) pool, and
  `buildSquareCells` clamps defensively so a malformed square can never crash the render.
  Surfaced by the lean-from-`organization` fix, which made budgets skewed enough to hit
  it. Key files: `src/lib/redistricting/defaultMap.ts`, `src/lib/redistricting/cardView.ts`.

- **Admin redistrict regenerate is now idempotent** — it upserts district docs by their
  deterministic `_id` instead of delete-then-insert, so a stale row or a double-clicked
  trigger can't collide on the unique `(countryId, stateId, index)` index and 500.
  Key file: `src/lib/redistricting/regenerate.ts`.

- **Seeded districts now reflect each state's actual partisan lean** — the district
  builder read `statePartyOrg.registration`, which is only populated by the (separately
  invoked) registration-lane seed and is unset when core seeding builds the district
  docs. Every US state therefore read 0 left/right at seed time and collapsed to the
  neutral grey default (e.g. a perfectly balanced 25/25/30 budget regardless of the
  state). It now falls back to `organization` — the party-org presence that
  `generateStatePartyOrg` always seeds with the era's presidential-margin lean baked in
  (Democratic bonus in blue states, Republican in red) — so districts start with the
  real state lean. Re-seeding or an admin redistrict re-trigger regenerates existing
  states. Key file: `src/lib/redistricting/buildStateDocs.ts`.

- **Auto-Map directional strategies are stronger and never invert** — "Max Left" and
  "Max Right" could produce mirror-inverted results (Max Right yielding more left
  districts than Max Left) and occasionally illegal maps. The directional strategies now
  draw both sides from one shared candidate pool and pick opposite extremes, which
  guarantees Max Right is always at least as right-skewed as Max Left, and every strategy
  only returns a legal map (best-effort solely when the budget+laws admit none). `realize`
  also allocates grey unevenly to match each target district's lean parity, so efficient
  thin-margin gerrymanders (which an even-grey split rounded away into 0-ties) are found —
  e.g. a balanced 25/25 five-seat state now reaches a legal 3-1 (margin-2) map instead of
  stalling at 3-2. Verified across a 757-case budget/law-setting sweep: 0 skew inversions,
  0 illegal maps where a legal one exists, conservation always exact. Key file:
  `src/lib/redistricting/autoMap.ts`.

- **Districts no longer seed as all-grey when a state has no party registration** — on
  seeds without `statePartyOrg` rows (e.g. Texas 1991), `aggregatePoolPercents` fell
  through to `grey: 100`, so the whole state's 16·n square budget was independents (the
  reported "470 grey, ~2 red, no blue" Texas map). The builder now detects a missing
  two-party signal (`left + right < MIN_TWO_PARTY_PERCENT`) and derives the split from the
  state's average cookPVI — positive PVI tilts right, negative left, absent/zero yields a
  neutral ≈ 5L/6G/5R — via the new pure `derivePoolPercentsFromPvi`. As a backstop,
  `computeSquareBudget` now guarantees at least one left and one right square (borrowing
  from grey) whenever the budget has room, so rounding can never leave a state
  monochromatic-grey. Key files: `src/lib/redistricting/buildStateDocs.ts`,
  `src/lib/redistricting/pools.ts`, `src/lib/redistricting/budget.ts`.

- **Global crises now take a response from every leader, not just the first** — a global (`scope: "global"`) crisis with a `choice` decision (e.g. the Energy Crisis "What is your government's response?") is meant to ask _each_ nation's head of state for their own answer. The interaction engine instead resolved the whole interaction on the first leader's pick — locking every other leader out — and applied that one option's effects to the entire globe. Global choice nodes are now **multi-responder**: each affected country's head of state may respond once, the chosen option's effects are applied **scoped to that responder's own country** (new `applyEffectsForCountry`), and the interaction stays open on its node collecting responses (`leaderResponses`) instead of resolving on the first decision. A leader whose country already responded can no longer re-submit (engine + `/api/crises/[id]/interaction` + Actions-page `canInteract` all enforce it); auto-resolve and crisis-end no longer re-apply a global default over countries that already self-served. Country- and region-scoped crises are unchanged (still single-responder). Key files: `src/lib/crises/interactionEngine.ts`, `src/lib/turn/crisisTurn.ts`, `src/lib/db/types/crisis.ts`.

- **Crisis detail panel shows how each leader responded** — the Crisis Response panel now renders a compact **Leadership Responses** list for multi-responder crises: one row per nation (flag + country name · leader · chosen option, with the option's effects collapsed into a hover summary) so a roomful of responders stays readable. Leaders see their own choice reflected ("Your nation has responded…") and the options hide once they've answered. Key file: `src/app/world/crises/[id]/CrisisInteractionPanel.tsx`.

- **Debate resolution hardened against double-apply and missed simultaneous submits** — `resolveDebateSessionDoc` now **claims** a session with a conditional `awaitingStrategies → resolved` write before applying favorability/Debate XP, so the two independent sweep triggers (per-turn driver + realtime cron) and the submit path can no longer double-apply effects when they overlap. `submitDebateStrategies` re-reads the session after writing its side's picks, so two players submitting at once resolve immediately instead of waiting up to 12h for the deadline sweep. Key file: `src/lib/debate/debateSessionLifecycle.ts`.

- **Primary debates no longer pair you against a rival-party candidate** — the per-turn debate driver (`processDebateChallenges`) grouped active candidacies by election only and picked any other candidate as the opponent. During a primary every party's contenders share one election and are all `active`, so a player could be challenged to debate someone from another party's primary (e.g. a Democrat asked to debate Pat Buchanan) — a debate they aren't actually in. The driver now loads each election's phase and, while the primary is open, restricts opponents to the challenger's own party; the general phase stays intentionally cross-party (challenger vs the other nominee). Added an in-memory `isInPrimaryPhase` helper (a general-only race with no primary boundary never reads as "in primary"). Key files: `src/lib/debate/processDebateChallenges.ts`, `src/lib/elections/electionDeadlineFilters.ts`.

- **JP Regional Council elections restored (parties can slate again)** — the JP prefectural-assembly (Regional Council) chamber stopped spawning elections after the `update/registration-drive-revert` merge silently clobbered the original activation commit (`15a919c6e`). With no `regionalCouncil` elections being created for JP regions, national and regional parties had no Regional Council races to slate candidates onto. Restored `ensureJPRegionalCouncilElections` (mirrors each region's live Shugiin timing/cycle/year, falling back to the Shugiin canonical cycle), its registration in `COUNTRY_ELECTION_PHASES.JP`, the JP `regionalCouncil` officeType (sub-national, 0.85 party-strength weight, admin-appointable), the real `subNationalChamber.seats` total (2679), and the spawner + office-type tests. Self-heals on deploy with no migration — the first turn spawns the missing elections off the already-live Shugiin races. Key files: `src/lib/turn/perpetualElections.ts`, `src/lib/turn/countryPhases.ts`, `src/lib/constants/countries.ts`.

- **National ministerial orders no longer fail validation** — issuing a National-scope ministerial/cabinet order (e.g. the JP Environment Minister's "Clean Energy Acceleration") returned `400 targetRegionId: Invalid input: expected string, received null`. National orders have no region, so the client sends `targetRegionId: null`, but the route schema was `z.string().optional()`, which accepts `undefined` but rejects `null`. The schema is now `z.string().nullable().optional()` for `targetRegionId`/`regionId`, and a trailing-`null` is normalized to `undefined` so the effect stores an absent `regionId` rather than `null`. Added an up-front guard: a regional-scope order issued without a target region now returns `400 "Select a target region"` instead of spending a ministerial action on an effect the turn engine silently drops (it only applies regional effects that carry a `regionId`). Key file: `src/app/api/country/[code]/executive/cabinet/[positionId]/order/route.ts`.

## [0.3.4] - 2026-06-20

### Added

- **Custom (flavor) bills** — every propose-legislation flow (US Congress, each country's national legislature, and state/regional legislatures) now offers a **Custom** category. A custom bill carries no provisions and has zero mechanical effect; the player supplies only a Title and Summary for roleplay/flavor. It moves through the normal legislative lifecycle (opens for voting, can pass/fail, "enacts" to a no-op) and costs the standard 10 action points and 0 national political influence. Selecting "Custom" hides the provision editors in all three propose modals. Server-side, `category:"custom"` forces `provisions:[]` in `proposeNationalBill`, the US congress route, and `proposeStateBill`, so a client cannot smuggle real effects under the custom label; `proposeBillSchema` permits an empty provisions array only for custom. Custom is intentionally not offered via the JP Cabinet or governor-queue channels. No migration.

- **Tariffs surfaced on the World Trade Ledger's Restrictions tab** — `/world/trade` → Restrictions now lists active tariffs alongside embargoes, grouped by the imposing nation with a readable scope summary (economy-wide / sector / origin-country / corporation) and rate. Backed by a new read-only `GET /api/world/trade/tariffs` route that returns every active tariff (`rate > 0`) across all nations, resolving corporation-scoped targets to names. Read-only by design — the `tariffs` collection is reconciled from signed bills each turn (`buildCorporationLookups`), so this surface reads it directly rather than re-reconciling per page load. `RestrictionsView` loads embargoes and tariffs in parallel (full-panel error only if both fail), with "Embargoes"/"Tariffs" section headers and an updated count, subtitle, and empty state.

- **Nigeria (NG) seed bundle** — Nigeria is now seedable. New `src/lib/seeds/ng/` bundle modeled on Brazil: 6 geopolitical zones (North-West/East/Central, South-West/South/East) with seat counts scaled to NASS totals (House 360, Senate 109), 5 parties (APC, PDP, LP, NNPP, APGA), 8 voter archetypes, a Layer-1 census model (`src/lib/seeds/international/ng.ts`), zone metrics + baselines, a government-formation doc, a sovereign-issuer corp, and an NG national budget config. Wired into `bootstrapGameWorld`, `/api/admin/seed` (`NG_TARGETS`), the admin Universal Seeder (Scope: Nigeria), `ensureNGElections` in `perpetualElections`, and `metricPresets` / `defaultPartyTiers` / `SupportedBudgetCountryId` / `basePolicies` / `basePolicies1991`. 2019-default only (1991 falls back to 2019); data is approximate and tunable. Activate via Admin → System → Universal Seeder → Nigeria → Reset on → Run Full Seed (inserts 6 `states`; `seedUnownedSectors` etc. then create ~102 NG unowned sectors). Known follow-up: `ensureNGElections` reuses the 2-year House cycle anchor (NG terms are 4 years).

- **RPG character stats system** — characters now have six allocatable stats (Charisma, Intelligence, Leadership, Business Acumen, Statecraft, Resilience) gated behind the `rpgStatsEnabled` flag. Stats shift outcome probabilities on PREE events (shown as a roll-delta badge on the event card). Profile page shows a Stats panel with mechanic-specific tooltips, current bonus %, and stat class (Average, Above Average, etc.). Training actions include explanations of what each stat improves in practice.

- **One free stat reallocation** — after their initial allocation, each character may respec their stat sheet exactly once, anytime, for free. The own-profile Stats panel shows a "Reallocate (1 free)" button (only when the flag is on, stats are allocated, and the free change is unused) that reopens the 28-point allocator. Confirming does a full reset: it rewrites `stats`, clears earned growth (`statXp`), and resets the Debate decay anchor. Backed by a new `POST /api/character/reallocate-stats` route with an atomic one-per-character guard (`statsReallocationUsed` on the character doc) and exposed via `statsAllocated` / `statsReallocationUsed` on `/api/auth/me`.

- **Crisis interaction system (player-facing, now being enabled)** — crises are no longer admin-only set pieces: players resolve them through multi-step decision trees with collective contributions and ambient interaction cards. The `crisisInteractionEnabled` flag is now flippable from the admin Crises tab (`CrisesManager.tsx`) via `POST /api/admin/crises/toggle` (`requireAdmin`, mirrors the PREE toggle, stamps `crisisInteractionEnabledBy` / `crisisInteractionEnabledAt`); when on, template-based creation, decision trees, collective contributions, and the player `CrisisInteractionPanel` become available. The panel previews each option's signed, color-coded effect chips before you choose, shows a live deadline countdown (turns rose under an hour), and surfaces the resolved terminal node's `outcomeEffects` as a "Net effect, applied at turn resolve" row.

- **Minority shareholder pressure events** — four new PREE events fire when a CEO holds >65% (or >80%) of their public corporation's shares: a strategy-switch demand (flavor reflects the corp's actual current and demanded strategies), an expansion demand into a minority shareholder's home state (named after the specific shareholder), an ESG extraction-strategy objection (names the actual current and alternative extraction methods), and a boardroom ultimatum threatening a proxy campaign at >80% concentration. All four are gated behind new `ceoConcentrated` / `ceoVeryConcentrated` eligibility tags, mitigated by `businessAcumen`, and start as `draft` pending admin approval. Added payload interpolation (`{token}` replacement) in event copy so dynamic flavor tokens resolve at offer time.

- **Automatic regional natural disasters** — a new turn phase (`autoDisasters`) spawns natural-disaster crises on a per-region schedule without admin input. Disasters target states in the affected region, apply a transient margin penalty to corporations operating there (decaying each turn), and are tagged with `autoGenerated` for audit. Gated behind `autoDisastersEnabled` feature flag (off by default). Admin dashboard shows a toggle.

- **Election debate subsystem** — characters in elections can issue and accept debate challenges. Debate outcomes score based on stats and feed into election support. Includes a three-phase engine (challenge, session, scoring).

- **Unified inbox** — the `/notifications` page is rebuilt as a single inbox merging system notifications and player mail. A pure mapper layer (`src/lib/inbox/`) reduces both a `Notification` and a grouped `PlayerMail` thread to one `InboxItem`, deriving a 6-category mapping (`categoryOf`), an action-required predicate (`isActionRequired`), and a shared source deep-link resolver (extracted from `NotificationCard`). List-rail + reading-pane shell (`InboxClient`) with All/Notifications/Mail segments, "This turn / Earlier" grouping, mail thread + composer, archive/snooze, and a responsive single-column mobile layout. Per-item archive/snooze added to the notifications API (`archivedAt` / `snoozedUntil`, new PATCH `action`s). The navbar bell gains a priority push-up + soft pulse (`ahdHalo`) when action items exist, and a `PriorityLane` card surfaces the active PREE event for inline select→commit resolution. Foundation mappers are unit-tested.

- **Index funds place premium standing bids when float is dry** — when public float can't cover an
  underweight constituent's deficit, the fund now places a fund-owned order-book buy order at a 2%
  premium (`fundBidPolicy.ts`), escrowed from fund cash. It captures float as it appears and adds
  buy-side order flow. One open bid per (fund, corp); bids are cancelled when the corp leaves the
  basket or after 24 turns. Required making `ShareOrder.characterId` optional and teaching the turn
  matcher to settle fund-owned fills (`placeFundShareBuyOrder`/`cancelFundShareOrder`).
- **Admin bulk deployable-cash injection for index funds** — `POST /api/admin/investment-funds/deploy-cash-all`
  and a "Deploy Cash to All Funds" button inject a flat amount of deployable buying power into every
  active fund (distinct from the existing top-to-100%-backing action).

- **Insider concentration penalty on share price** — public corporations where the CEO holds more
  than 65% of total shares now receive a share price discount that scales quadratically from 0% at
  65% ownership to −30% at 100% ownership (`insiderConcentrationMultiplier` in
  `sharePriceFormula.ts`). Private corporations are fully exempt. Applied in both the per-turn price
  computation (`sectorCalculations.ts`) and the post-bond recompute pass
  (`recomputeSharePrices.ts`).

- **Insider concentration credit rating downgrade** — when CEO ownership of a public corp exceeds
  65%, the credit rating is downgraded one notch (e.g. A → BBB). Does not stack with the bond
  default credit penalty (if `bondDefaultCreditPenaltyActive` already floors to CCC, the
  concentration notch is not applied). Implemented via `insiderConcentrationPenalty` option in
  `calculateCreditScore()` (`bonds.ts`) and wired through `computeCorporateCreditAtTurn()`
  (`corporateCredit.ts`).

- **Concentration penalty tooltips** — MarketOverviewPanel shows a "−N% conc." badge next to the
  share price when the penalty is active; BondsTab shows the credit rating tooltip explaining the
  one-notch downgrade. Both show the CEO's exact ownership percentage.

- **Dual-class supershare pie chart** — when a corporation has adopted dual-class supershares,
  MarketOverviewPanel now renders two side-by-side pie charts: one for economic ownership (shares
  only) and one for voting power (supershare-weighted). Driven by `shareholderVotingPower()` and
  `totalVotingPower()` from `superShares.ts`.

- **Passed proposal stays visible** — shareholder proposals that reach their required threshold now
  display a "PASSED" status badge and remain on-screen until the next page load. Previously they
  disappeared immediately on resolution. Fix in `CorporationVoteCard.tsx` (only
  `failed`/`cancelled` trigger parent removal).

- **`ceoCharacterId` on corporation detail** — the `/api/corporation/[id]` detail response now
  includes `ceoCharacterId` (the character ObjectId as a string) for character-type CEOs; null for
  NPC/NPP/imperial CEOs.

- **Bulk "Inject Capital — All Funds to 100%" admin action** — new
  `POST /api/admin/investment-funds/inject-capital-all` route (`requireAdmin`) tops every index fund
  up to a target backing ratio (default 100%) by injecting anchor-currency cash without minting
  units, recomputing NAV and backing per fund and logging each injection plus a bulk admin-log
  entry. Funds already funded, or whose NAV has collapsed to ~0 (no quoted liability — these need
  manual unit repair), are skipped. Surfaced as a header button in the index-funds admin panel.

- **Iteration-aware wiki office-holder history** — generated office wiki pages (Head of Government,
  US Vice President, cabinet, and congressional leadership) now group the holder history by game
  iteration (Alpha → Beta → Iteration, then number ascending, oldest section first) and render tenure
  as in-game **Week N, YYYY** instead of raw turn numbers, alongside a real-world **Dates** column
  (e.g. "June 16, 2026 - Present"). A new global `gameState.iterationHistory`
  registry drives which iteration sections always render (seeded `Alpha 1, Beta 1, Beta 2` by the
  backfill migration and appended on every reset / "Set Now"); office-history records are stamped
  with their iteration via `recordCountryEvent` and a shared `freezeOfficeHistoryIterations` helper
  run at each `resetGameWorld`. Admins can curate past-iteration holders inline on the office page
  (in-game Week + Year and/or real-world start/end dates — at least one start required) via a new
  `manualOfficeHistory` collection and admin-gated
  `POST/PATCH/DELETE /api/wiki/office-history` routes. One-time backfill script
  `scripts/debug/backfill-office-history-iterations.mjs` stamps existing records and seeds the
  registry.

### Changed

- **Free-trade agreements are an economic instrument again.** FTA is no longer a baseline
  power granted to every category — only **economic** and **development** orgs can table one.
  Security alliances (e.g. NATO) carry only collective posture + joint statements, and a
  political forum (e.g. the UN) keeps agency funding / joint statements / aid — neither
  proposes FTAs anymore. Security orgs also lose sanctions (an economic coercion tool).
  The Overview hides the FTA and sanctions panels wherever the category lacks the power.
- **Reverted active Registration Drive action; buffed passive Reg drift** — the
  `registration-drive` route + `RegistrationDrivePanel` (region→party Overview and State Politics
  tab) and its constants/types (`REGISTRATION_DRIVE_*`, `calcRegistrationDriveGain`,
  `RegistrationDriveFactors` / `RegistrationFactorBreakdown` / `RegistrationFactorsExplainer`) are
  gone. Reg is back to growing only via passive Org→Reg drift (`PASSIVE_REG_DRIFT_RATE`), bumped
  `0.04 → 0.06` per turn (~1.44 pp/day toward each party's `Org − country lag` target, ~50% faster
  than pre-revert) so the lost active path doesn't slow committed parties to a crawl. Org-building
  remains the primary lever and Reg follows behind it on its own.

- **RPG stat growth/decay reworked to "use it or lose it"** — `applyTurnDrift` (`statDrift.ts`) no
  longer subtracts idle decay unconditionally. A skill stat that was engaged that turn (XP > 0) now
  grows with **no** decay, so active play never nets a loss on the stat it trained; only neglected
  stats slip toward the floor at `IDLE_DECAY_RATE`. This fixes Business Acumen, which previously
  net-decayed (−0.02/turn) even while actively held — its single per-turn CEO tick could never beat
  the flat decay. Energy keeps a dedicated rule (`ENERGY_MAINTENANCE_XP`): because it accrues XP on
  every action, it only avoids decay when the turn's activity clears a maintenance threshold (~2
  actions), so it's no longer a free ratchet.

- **Business Acumen training scales with corp performance** — the per-turn CEO Business-Acumen XP
  increment is now multiplied by the corp's realized profit margin this turn
  (`businessAcumenGrowthMultiplier`: 0.5× when unprofitable → 2.0× at >25% margin), and a CEO running
  several corps is credited for their most profitable one. `processSectors` now returns
  `profitMarginByCorpId` (net income before dividends ÷ revenue per corp).

- **Business Acumen effect changed from revenue boost to growth-cost lever** — BA no longer multiplies
  per-turn sector revenue. Instead `calculateDailyGrowthCost` takes the CEO's BA stat and applies (a)
  a flat growth-cost discount (`acumenGrowthCostMultiplier`, ~±13.5% across the stat band) and (b) a
  dampened prime-rate term (`acumenRateSensitivity`, ~±27%), so a skilled CEO grows more cheaply and
  feels less of a high prime rate — the benefit compounds when rates are high. The per-corp lookup
  was repurposed from a revenue multiplier to the raw stat (`ceoBusinessAcumenMultByCorpId` →
  `ceoBusinessAcumenByCorpId`). Player-facing growth-cost previews (`setSectorGrowth`,
  `bulkSetSectorOperations`) read the CEO's BA so displayed cost matches the turn-engine charge.

- **Debate progression eased and tied to debating** — Debate Prep now costs 1 AP (was 2) at a 15%
  success rate (was 10%), and Debate decays −1 per 72h (was 48h). Debate is now also trained by
  _debating_: every resolved election debate gives a character participant a practice roll to +1
  (winner 30%, loser 15%) via `rollDebatePractice`, applied in `resolveDebateSessionDoc`.

- **Corporation Financials tab redesign ("tear sheet")** — the corporation Financials tab is
  rebuilt from a flat full-width ledger into an analyst-style layout. A summary band leads with the
  headline figures (net income, net margin, price/book, retained — or total assets / book value /
  price/book / share price on the balance-sheet view) and folds the Income/Balance and Hourly/Annual
  toggles into a single toolbar. On desktop the income statement now sits beside an at-a-glance rail
  ("where revenue goes" allocation bar, cost breakdown, net-margin gauge + credit rating), filling
  the previously empty gutter; it stacks on mobile. Negative subtotals render in accounting style
  (`($950K)` not `-$950K`), the confusing `+` prefix on indented rows is gone, and the trailing
  Trade Restrictions / Active Subsidies empty states are compact side-by-side cards. New pieces:
  `financials/financialsModel.ts` (pure, unit-tested), `FinancialsVisuals.tsx`, `SummaryBand.tsx`,
  `GlanceRail.tsx` — all built from existing semantic tokens and market primitives.

- **Bond issuance interface — slider** — the corporation Debt tab's Issue Bond form (`BondsTab.tsx`)
  now drives the face value with the shared `Slider` (operating in ₳ anchor units), bounded by the
  per-issuance cap (25% of annual revenue, floored at $100M) and the minimum issuance, with the
  coupon rate surfaced per maturity option. Replaces the raw numeric entry.

- **Bond-coupon income now discounted in share-price valuation (interest-rate risk)** — a corporation
  that funds its earnings with bond coupons is treated as riskier than one earning the same from
  operations. In the fundamental formula (`sharePriceFormula.ts`), the bond-coupon slice of
  normalized earnings is now capitalized at `BOND_INCOME_SHARE_PRICE_DISCOUNT = 0.75×` the rate of
  operating profit, and bond holdings in the tangible-book floor get the same 0.75× haircut
  (mark-to-market / rate risk on principal). On top of that, an over-reliance penalty applies a
  **graduated** multiplier to the earnings-derived components when coupons exceed 75% of net income,
  ramping with no cliff from `1.0×` at the `BOND_INCOME_RELIANCE_THRESHOLD` (75%) down to
  `BOND_INCOME_MAX_RELIANCE_PENALTY` (`0.5×`) at 100% reliance — so a fully bond-reliant corp gets
  `0.75 × 0.5 = 0.375×` of the valuation that income previously produced. New
  `bondCouponEarningsAnchor` input + `bondRelianceValuationPenalty` helper; wired into both the
  corp-turn placeholder and the post-bond-turn recompute (`sectorCalculations.ts`,
  `recomputeSharePrices.ts`). Operations-driven corps are unaffected. Closes the loop where holding
  large (often government-subsidized) bond positions inflated the largest corporations' share prices.

- **Crisis Response panel display polish** — the player-facing crisis decision panel
  (`CrisisInteractionPanel`) now previews each option's signed, color-coded effect chips before you
  choose (mirroring the crises-list `EffectPills`), shows a live deadline countdown chip in the panel
  header that turns rose under an hour, surfaces the resolved terminal node's `outcomeEffects` as a
  "Net effect, applied at turn resolve" chip row (previously dropped), and adds a severity accent bar
  plus lift-on-hover option cards. Display-only; no schema or API changes.

- **Legible crisis resolutions** — when a crisis resolves, the outcome is now surfaced as a human-readable
  message and a narrative history entry instead of silently updating state metrics. Resolved crises carry
  an `outcomeLabel` and an `outcomeDescription` drawn from the winning decision node, persisted to the
  `crisisDecisionHistory` collection and surfaced in the crisis detail panel.

- **Crisis aid via legislature** — countries can now pledge international aid to a crisis as an emergency
  appropriation bill. The pledge flow (`aidPledge.ts`) deducts the amount from the sender's treasury
  immediately (surplus-first, remainder as debt) via the shared `spendFromTreasury` / `creditTreasury`
  fiscal helpers, applies provisional recovery effects to the recipient crisis, then creates a federal
  appropriation bill (`category: "foreign policy"`, `provisions: []`, `crisisAidId`) gated behind the
  `crisisAidBillsEnabled` feature flag (off by default). Bill resolution finalizes or claws back
  (`aidFinalize.ts`): a passed bill stands; a failed bill reverses provisional effects, refunds the treasury,
  and levies a temporary government-approval penalty (`AID_FAILED_PENALTY`) for `AID_PENALTY_TURNS` turns.
  The `processCrisisAidResolutions` and `reverseCrisisAidPenalties` turn phases sweep pending commitments
  each turn. The `crisisAidCommitments` collection carries three indexes added via migration
  `2026-06-21-crisis-aid-indexes`: unique `{crisisId, senderCountryId}` (double-pledge race guard),
  unique `{billId}`, and `{status, approvalPenaltyExpiresTurn}` (penalty sweep).

- **Shared `spendFromTreasury` / `creditTreasury` fiscal helpers** — `src/lib/budget/treasurySpend.ts`
  exports two atomic primitives: `spendFromTreasury` (drains surplus before converting overage to debt,
  returns a spend breakdown: `fromSurplus`, `addedToDebt`) and `creditTreasury` (repays debt principal
  before restoring surplus). Both accept an optional `resyncDerived` flag to recompute derived fiscal
  fields in place. Intended as the canonical write path for any system that moves money in/out of the
  national treasury — replaces ad-hoc `$inc` patterns across crisis aid, event rewards, and foreign
  transfers.

- **Cabinet ministerial actions refill at midnight Eastern Time (S#51)** — replaced the rolling
  +1-per-24-turns regen with a daily refill to cap (2) on each `America/New_York` calendar day.
  `processMinisterialOrders` stamps `lastMinisterialActionResetDay` and resets the pool; new
  appointments seed via `initialMinisterialActionFields` (`src/lib/time/dailyReset.ts`,
  `src/lib/cabinet/ministerialActionPool.ts`). Cabinet office UI shows "Resets daily at midnight
  Eastern Time." Appointment and tier-setting cooldowns unchanged.

### Fixed

- **International-organization dues were assessed ~1,000,000× too small.** The turn charge
  computed dues from `states.gdp` (stored in local-currency **millions**) but debited member
  treasuries — and credited the org fund — in **absolute** currency units, so a member paid a
  few hundred dollars a year instead of its real assessment and the pooled fund never grew.
  Dues now scale GDP to absolute (`GDP_MILLIONS_TO_USD`) before charging, matching the displayed
  "$X/yr" figure; this also fixes the fund-balance tile, which previously read ~1e6 too low once
  any dues accrued. (Live fund balances were all 0, so no data migration is needed.)

- **Debate challenges were dead-on-arrival for the player who didn't happen to be online at the turn
  tick** — when a debate session was created, each human candidate was snapshotted against a 15-minute
  "online" window (`lastActivity`): anyone not active in those 15 minutes was built as `mode:"ai"`
  with strategies pre-picked for them, so `/api/debates/active` reported `canSubmit:false` and the
  Actions-page debate card showed disabled options under "Waiting on the other candidate" — even
  though the debate stays open for 12 hours and the player was plainly online reading the card. Since
  the vast majority of challenges fire while the player isn't mid-click, this locked most players out
  of ever choosing their strategies. `buildCharacterParticipant` now always builds the interactive
  (`pvp`) side with no pre-picked strategies; the existing 12-hour deadline sweep
  (`sweepExpiredDebates` → `finalizeStrategies`) still AI-fills any side that doesn't respond in time,
  so genuine no-shows are unchanged. Removed the now-dead online-gate plumbing (`isOnline`,
  `DEBATE_ONLINE_WINDOW_MS`, per-user `lastActivity` lookup). No migration — any in-flight
  pre-resolved sessions self-heal at their deadline (≤12h). The Actions-page card also reflects a
  submitted choice on return (refresh before the deadline): the chosen strategies render with a green
  `success` highlight and a "✓ You selected" badge — and the instruction line switches to "You've
  locked in your strategy" — instead of a generic disabled list, wiring the route's already-returned
  `submittedStrategies` into `DebateCard`.

- **Debates are now a single-pick mechanic, rebalanced so your strongest stat decides** — the picker
  let players select up to three strategies and summed each side's scores, but the AI/NPP opponent
  also picked up to three, so the menu's "1–3 / spread across three" framing both invited the player
  to dilute their pick and (more importantly) let the highest-base archetype dominate. The card is now
  single-select (`toggle` replaces the prior pick; clicking it again clears it), `pickAiStrategies`
  commits the AI to its single best-fit archetype, `finalizeStrategies` caps a submitted side to its
  lead pick, and the route schema is `length(1)`. To keep the one pick meaningful, the three base
  rewards are equalized (10/7/9 → 8/8/8) and Tout's record factor now reaches full strength
  (`min(1,raw)*1.3 − 0.3`, range −0.3…1.0) so a statecraft-strong incumbent's best pick is Tout rather
  than Attack — each archetype's risk/reward now lives in its roll band, not a base-reward gap. Copy
  updated across `DebateCard`, the `rpg-stats` wiki page, and the debate type/scoring docs. No
  migration.

- **Approval-scaled incumbency for governor elections** — the incumbency persuasion driver was
  approval-blind: an unpopular incumbent governor got the same lift as a popular one. For a regional
  executive's own re-election it now scales with the sitting governor's stored approval — a shield up
  to +10pp above 50% (capping at 60%), and a drag down to −15pp below 50% (capping at 35%), pivoting
  on `BASE_APPROVAL`. The presidential race is excluded (its engine already folds approval into a
  `strengthMultiplier`, so the driver would double-count). Legislatures, primaries, and races with no
  resolved approval keep the prior seat-share behavior. New `approvalAdjustedIncumbencyBudget` +
  `isOwnRegionalExecutiveRace`; reuses the gov-coattail approval resolver (which deliberately skips
  the own race). No migration.

- **Index funds never converged to their target weights** — the per-turn engine only ever _bought_
  from public float (sized by target weight, never by drift), so overweight names grew unbounded and
  underweight names with thin float never filled. The cron now runs a two-sided drift rebalance
  (`planFundTargetRebalance` → sell overweight back to float, then buy underweight), so holdings
  track the basket each turn.
- **Index funds froze permanently when a constituent delisted** — a delisted/privatised constituent
  auto-paused the fund with `constituent_delisted`, and the whole cron skips non-active funds, so it
  never recovered. The fund now drops the dead holding and rebalances into the surviving basket
  instead of pausing. Added `scripts/heal-paused-index-funds.ts` to reactivate funds already stuck.
- **Partial limit-buy fills double-refunded the unfilled escrow** — on a partial fill the matcher
  refunded the entire unfilled remainder's escrow immediately _and_ left it reserved on the open
  order, so a later cancel (or further fill) refunded it again — mintable by placing a premium buy
  larger than available float, then cancelling. Refund is now only the filled shares' below-limit
  savings, with the unfilled escrow reserved share-proportionally. Affected the character/corp order
  book as well as the new fund path; full fills are unchanged.
- **Corporation Charts price history stopped short of the early game** — the `/history` endpoint
  capped at the most recent 500 turns. The Charts tab now requests `?full=1`, which returns the full
  history downsampled to ~500 evenly-spaced points (always keeping the newest), so the long-range
  view reaches game start. The recent-500 default is unchanged for the masthead sparkline.
- **Opening a second shareholder vote crashed with a 500** — `corporationVotes` has a unique partial
  index on `corporationId` (where `status = "open"`), so only one open vote per corporation is
  allowed. The existing-vote guard in `openCorporationVote` queried with a `type` filter, so it only
  blocked a second vote of the _same_ type; a different-type vote (e.g. an open `ticker_change`)
  slipped through the guard and the insert threw a duplicate-key error that surfaced as an Internal
  Server Error. Guard now queries without the `type` filter — any open vote blocks a new one, with a
  message distinguishing same-type ("already in progress") from cross-type ("resolve it before
  opening a new one").
- **Cutting spending could paradoxically increase the deficit (budget cost-scale cliff)** — absolute
  per-capita / per-USD spending-law costs were multiplied by a country scale that jumped ~3.4× at
  game-year 2000 (e.g. UK 0.31 → 1.05) and was frozen to each law's enactment year. Re-legislating a
  category in 2000+ therefore cost far more than the pre-2000 default it replaced, so a nominal "cut"
  (e.g. NHS funding 1758 → 977 per-capita) raised effective spending and widened the deficit. Replaced
  the two-table step function in `src/lib/budget/costs.ts` with a per-country GDP-per-capita ramp
  (`COST_SCALE_ANCHORS` + `getGdpIndexedCostScale`) between the 1991 and 2020/2023 seed anchors,
  applied at the current economy and uniformly to all active laws — so identical policy costs the same
  regardless of when it was passed, the scale self-corrects to the in-game economy, and cuts reduce
  spending. National GDP-per-capita is threaded through `calculateFederalSpending`,
  `calculateStateSpending`, budget validation, and the policy-record/heal routes; an
  `anchorConsistency` test pins the anchors to the seed configs. One-time live refresh:
  `scripts/debug/heal-budget-cost-scale.mjs`.
- **Sector subsidies were charged to the treasury at ~13× the benefit corporations received** — the
  budget cost was 100% of subsidized sector revenue (`SUBSIDY_COST_MULTIPLIER = 1.0`) while corps only
  gained a +7.5pp margin (~7.5% of revenue). `SUBSIDY_COST_MULTIPLIER` is now
  `(SUBSIDY_MARGIN_BONUS / 100) * SUBSIDY_DEADWEIGHT_FACTOR` (= 0.105 — parity plus a 40% deadweight
  premium), tied to the margin bonus so cost and benefit stay in lockstep. Corps' benefit is
  unchanged; only the over-stated budget cost is corrected.
- **Leaving a party to become independent wrongly armed the 24-hour party-switch cooldown** — the
  `leave` route stamped `lastPartySwitchAt: now` on every departure, but that field is the anchor for
  the 24-hour join cooldown (enforced on the `join` route via `getPartySwitchCooldown`). A player who
  had been in a party for days and quit to independent was therefore locked out of joining _any_
  party for a fresh 24 hours, even though the cooldown is meant to rate-limit party _switching_, not
  becoming independent (the `purge` route already deliberately clears the anchor for the same reason).
  The `leave` route no longer writes `lastPartySwitchAt`: it leaves the prior value (from the last
  actual join) untouched, so the cooldown is incurred only by joining a party while still preventing a
  quick join→leave→rejoin hop from dodging it. For that hop-prevention to hold, every real join must
  stamp the anchor, so charter ratification (`ratifyCharter`, the founders-join step) now stamps
  `lastPartySwitchAt` alongside `partyJoinedAt`, matching the `join` route. +`leave` and `ratifyCharter`
  tests; `lastPartySwitchAt` doc comment corrected. Live data healed for the 3 current independents
  still carrying a stale anchor.
- **Index fund admin "Manage" / pause / resume / delist returned "Fund not found"** — the admin
  detail (`GET`) and status (`PATCH`) routes resolved funds with `getFundBySlug`, but the admin
  panel sends the fund `_id`, not the slug, so every lookup 404'd. Both now use
  `resolveFundBySlugOrId` (slug **or** ObjectId). The admin panel also surfaces detail-fetch errors
  instead of silently rendering an empty fund shell.

### Platform

- **Wiki subdomain** — `wiki.ahousedividedgame.com` now serves the existing wiki section via a
  Next.js middleware rewrite (`middleware.ts`). Requests arriving on the `wiki.` hostname are
  internally rewritten to `/wiki/*`; the app, DB connection, and auth cookie (already scoped to
  `.ahousedividedgame.com`) are unchanged. All 25 static `href="/wiki/..."` links inside
  `src/app/wiki/` have the `/wiki` prefix stripped so they resolve correctly on the subdomain.
  Canonical URLs across all wiki pages (`[slug]/page.tsx`, `category`, `elections`, `elections/[id]`,
  `elections/browse`, `paths`, `roadmap`, `my`, `new`) now emit
  `https://wiki.ahousedividedgame.com/<slug>` via `getWikiCanonicalUrl` / `wikiPublicPageMetadata` /
  `getWikiSiteUrl` — no SEO split between main domain and subdomain. Requires Railway custom domain +
  DNS CNAME to activate (infrastructure step, no deploy change).

## [0.3.3] - 2026-06-14

### Changed

- **Referendum ground game rebalanced** — campaign actions now move the Yes share in line with their
  stated values. Whole-electorate persuasion (e.g. broadcast ads) shifts the result by its card
  figure instead of a fraction of it; get-out-the-vote / mobilization actions are now effective and
  turn out _your own_ supporters (a Yes drive and a No drive pull opposite ways); repeated effort on
  one voter group tapers off smoothly instead of stopping dead at a cap; and both sides campaigning
  at equal volume cancels out fairly. Internally, per-cohort campaign effects accumulate as raw units
  and saturate (tanh) at read time, and card swing labels now reflect the real effect. +balance test.

- **Maps follow region transfers** — the country and world maps now render regions by their LIVE
  country ownership (`states.countryId`) instead of a fixed per-country list, so a region that
  changes countries (e.g. Northern Ireland reunifying with Ireland) automatically leaves its old
  country's map, appears on its new one (auto-framed), and recolours on the world map. A shared
  British-Isles geometry keyed by region code + an ownership resolver drive UK + IE; the pattern
  generalises to future transfers/secessions. +tests.

- **Referendum reunification transfer reworked** — a region transfer no longer relocates national
  party docs (which gutted the UK by moving GB-wide parties to Ireland); it now evacuates the
  region's politics — NPP politicians relocate to London with any company they run (free), players
  go Independent, and the region's party orgs / officeholders / seats dissolve for the new country to
  re-seed. Consent bills show a "Referendum Passed" chip; the transfer masthead gets a split IE/UK
  flag emblem. +tests.

### Added

- **Northern Ireland reunification actuation (Phase 1)** — a passed NI reunification referendum opens
  a 24-turn conversion window and tables TWO concurrent bills — a Westminster Commons bill (the UK
  releases NI) and a Dáil bill (Ireland admits NI) — both of which the respective MPs/TDs vote on.
  The region transfers only if BOTH bills pass within the window (admins may still resolve early or
  block); if either bill fails — or the window closes without both passing — the reunification is
  cancelled and the North may try again immediately. On success,
  Northern Ireland moves out of the United Kingdom and into the Republic of Ireland. Sitting NI MPs carry over as TDs; the parties remap (Sinn Féin merges into
  Ireland's, the unionist parties and SDLP/Alliance become Irish parties); NI becomes an Irish
  PR-STV region with a population-share Dáil allocation (the Dáil grows accordingly); every
  NI-resident character becomes an Irish citizen; and both countries' national figures recompute.
  All region-scoped data (party orgs, metrics, demographics, budgets, registration, governor office
  state, …) re-scopes atomically, verified by an end-to-end conservation test. +tests.

- **UK independence/reunification referendum framework (Phase 0)** — Scotland, Wales, and Northern
  Ireland can now pursue leaving the United Kingdom through a player-driven referendum process. The
  region's First Minister requests a referendum once independence/reunification desire reaches 60
  (Devolution tab); the Prime Minister grants or declines it; a granted referendum opens a campaign
  window where the pro-independence and unionist parties spend Political Strength to move the Yes share
  before the vote resolves. A new `referendumLifecycle` turn phase advances the whole process, and
  passing/failing results are recorded to country history. Map-changing actuation (standing up
  Scotland/Wales as countries, transferring Northern Ireland to Ireland) is not yet enabled — a
  passed referendum awaits admin confirmation. +tests.

- **Registration Drive party action** — a new PS-spending state action (`registration-drive`
  route + `/preview`, `RegistrationDrivePanel` on the region→party Overview and State Politics tab)
  that converts Independent/Unregistered voters into a party's Reg%. Gain diminishes toward a
  ceiling (`REGISTRATION_DRIVE_CEILING = 85`) and tapers as the convertible pool dries up; draws
  `unregistered` then `independent` preserving the Σ = 100 pool invariant
  (`calcRegistrationDriveGain`). The active counterpart to passive Org→Reg drift — lets committed
  parties carry Reg into the band where the election curves bite.
- **Major / Minor party tiers** — parties now carry a `tier` driving a Major/Minor badge and the
  national PS cap (`parties/partyTier.ts`, `partyTierTurn` turn phase). Minor cap = `100 + 10 ×
regions at ≥20% Org` (hysteresis: earned at ≥20%, lost below 10%); Major = the full national cap.
  Graduation at ≥20% Org in ≥⌈regions/3⌉ regions; demotion after a 240-turn warning when <10% Org
  in ≥⌈2·regions/3⌉ regions (cancelled by re-graduating). Custom parties start Minor; default
  parties seed Major/Minor per country/era (`seeds/defaultPartyTiers.ts`). One-party-state ruling
  parties are pinned Major. National Party page shows an at-risk demotion-warning countdown.
- **Inter-country trade balance & World Trade Ledger** — a real bilateral trade subsystem on top of
  the existing per-country commodity supply/demand. Each turn an affinity-weighted gravity engine
  (`src/lib/trade/`: `affinity.ts`, `clearing.ts` asymmetric-IPF) clears every commodity's surpluses
  against deficits into directed country→country flows, persisted as one `tradeFlowSnapshot` document
  per turn from `commodityPriceTurn`. Trade then **dampens prices** whole-market: each country's
  effective supply/demand eases by `k` (=0.5, live-tuned) of the trade it did, before pricing — so
  imports relieve shortages across prices, sector margins, inflation, and stored S/D
  (`convergence.ts`). Exporting sectors earn a capped **export-reward margin premium** for producing
  goods their country actually ships into foreign deficits (`exportPremium.ts`, surfaced in the corp
  margin diagnostic). Flows are shaped by policy: FTA coverage and shared international-org bloc
  membership raise affinity, importer tariffs drag inbound flows (mapped per commodity→sector,
  FTA-exempt), and embargoes block or cap specific flows (`tradeAffinity.ts`, `tariffDrag.ts`).
  Embargoes come in two forms — temporary (trade-minister `requireTradeMinister` + the
  `/api/world/trade/embargoes` impose/lift API) and durable via legislation (`embargo` /
  `end_embargo` bill provisions reconciled into records, `reconcileEmbargoes.ts`). Surfaced on the
  new `/world/trade` "World Trade Ledger" page — five views (By Nation, By Commodity, Bilateral,
  Trade Matrix, World Map) reading the real snapshot, all in the viewer's display currency.

- **Embargo bill builder (Propose Legislation)** — the **Trade** category now offers a
  Tariff/Embargo restriction-type toggle in both the US Congress and the country (UK/DE/JP/IE/CN)
  bill-proposal modals. The embargo builder (`EmbargoProvisionEditor`, `embargoProvisionTypes.ts`)
  lets a sponsor impose or lift a durable embargo per target country, commodity (or all), direction
  (export/import/both), and block-or-cap mode. A trade bill carries tariffs **or** embargoes (not
  both); embargo provisions are validated and stored by both backends (`validateBillProvisions`,
  `proposeNationalBill`, and the congress route) and materialize as legislation-origin embargo
  records via the turn's `reconcileSignedEmbargoBills` once signed — `applyLegislationEffect` no-ops
  the provision at enactment so the tariff path never misapplies it. Bill detail and the legislature
  bill list render a readable provision summary (`formatEmbargoProvisionLabel`, shared by
  `billEnrichment` and `nationalBillQueries`) — e.g. "Cap Steel & Metals imported from Germany at
  5,000 units" with a supporting sentence — instead of the raw `commodity → country`.

- **Trade-minister embargo panel (cabinet office)** — the trade-policy cabinet seat
  (`TRADE_MINISTER_POSITION_BY_COUNTRY`: US Commerce, UK Business & Trade, DE Economic Affairs, JP
  METI, CN Commerce, IE Enterprise, Trade & Employment) now carries a "Trade Embargoes" panel on its
  office page
  (`TradeEmbargoPanel`). It lists active embargoes touching the country (imposed by it / on it),
  lets the holder impose a temporary embargo (target · commodity · direction · block/cap · duration)
  and lift their own ministerial ones, all via `/api/world/trade/embargoes`. Legislated embargoes
  appear read-only ("repeal via bill"). Authority is enforced server-side by `requireTradeMinister`
  (seat holder, admin, else head of government).
  Limits keep the cabinet lever deliberate (constants in `trade/constants.ts`): each impose **costs 1
  cabinet action** (refunded if rejected; not charged for admin/head-of-gov overrides), **max 96
  turns** duration, **at most 2 active per cabinet member**, and a **168-turn per-target cooldown**
  measured from enactment that survives an early lift (tracked in a new `embargoCooldowns`
  collection) — so durable or repeated restriction must go through legislation.

- **Trade Restrictions ledger view** — a new "Restrictions" tab on `/world/trade` (`RestrictionsView`)
  lists every active embargo worldwide, grouped by the nation imposing it, with the readable summary
  (`formatEmbargoProvisionLabel`), origin (Ministerial / By law), expiry, and a Block/Cap badge. A
  **Pending in legislation** section also shows in-flight embargo bills (not yet enacted) with their
  source→target, summary, sponsoring bill, and legislative status. The `GET /api/world/trade/embargoes`
  endpoint now returns a `pending` array (non-terminal bills carrying embargo/end_embargo provisions)
  alongside active embargoes. This completes the trade influence-lever surface.

- **United States per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — completes the set: all 51 US regions
  (50 states + DC) get authored per-era values for their 45 country-relevant new overhaul ROOT
  metrics, composed from a national baseline + a regional archetype (tech coast / New England &
  Mid-Atlantic / Rust Belt / South / Plains / Mountain West) + per-state outlier overrides (CA, NY,
  TX, FL, WV, MS, DC, HI, AK, UT, …). 2019 carries the tech-coast-vs-Deep-South divide; 1991 is the
  post-Cold-War "peace dividend" year amid the 1990-91 recession and the early-90s crime peak — a
  larger manufacturing base, Cold-War-peak military, near-zero renewables, higher uninsuredness, and
  much lower public debt (~60% vs ~107%). Same overlay mechanism as the others (overlaid in
  `seedStateMetrics` and the `runCoreSeed` baseline pass). +tests.

  With the US done, **all seven countries** (IE, DE, JP, BR, CN, UK, US) now have hand-authored,
  per-region, per-era presets for both reset dates. Live 2019 worlds need a (held, dry-run-first)
  backfill migration to pick up the authored values.

- **United Kingdom per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — the UK's 48 country-relevant new
  overhaul ROOT metrics — the largest set, and the only one that natively uses `nhsWaitingTime`,
  `gcseAttainment` and `bbcTrust` — are authored per region for both eras. As with Japan, 2019
  preserves the genuine per-region values the UK seed already authors (property, GCSE attainment,
  NHS waits, BBC trust, rough sleeping, devolution satisfaction) and authors only the formula-derived
  remainder. 1991 captures John Major's first year amid the early-90s recession: a larger
  manufacturing base (deindustrialisation came later), much lower public debt (~30% vs ~98%),
  pre-devolution governance (no Scottish/Welsh/NI legislatures), longer NHS waits, and a dominant
  pre-internet BBC. Same overlay mechanism as the others. +tests.

- **China per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — China's 45 country-relevant new overhaul
  ROOT metrics (IE's set plus `nuclearSafety`) are hand-authored per macro-region for both eras,
  composing with the existing CN-specific era adjustments (social credit, hukou, party discipline).
  2019 carries the coastal-vs-interior gap (Huadong/Huanan R&D, manufacturing, and tier-1 property
  pressure vs the Xibei/Dongbei periphery); 1991 captures early Deng-era reform after Tiananmen —
  overwhelmingly rural and poor, danwei-allocated housing with no property market (a floor-level
  index), near-zero R&D/robotics, coal-only energy, very low public debt, and a strong Mao-era
  female-workforce legacy that has since eroded. Same overlay mechanism as the others. +tests.

- **Brazil per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — Brazil's 45 country-relevant new overhaul
  ROOT metrics (IE's set plus `nuclearSafety`, for the Angra reactors) are hand-authored per
  macro-region for both eras. 2019 carries the Norte/Nordeste-vs-Sudeste/Sul development gap
  (R&D, property, education, child poverty); 1991 captures the hyperinflation years before the Real
  Plan — Collor's recession (contracting productivity), a four-year-old democracy, a closed
  import-substitution economy, a very young population, a nascent SUS, and lower public debt than
  today. Same overlay mechanism as the others. +tests.

- **Japan per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — Japan's 45 country-relevant new overhaul
  ROOT metrics (IE's set plus `nuclearSafety`) are authored per region for both eras. The 2019
  bundle preserves the genuine per-region values Japan's seed already hand-authored (robotics,
  gender equality, elder care, disaster prep, …) and authors only the metrics the seed left to the
  uniform formula. 1991 captures the asset-bubble peak: astronomical Tokyo/Osaka land and commercial
  values and peak "Japan Inc." manufacturing dominance (both _higher_ than 2019), brutal bubble-era
  overwork and exam pressure, pre-Fukushima nuclear confidence, almost no renewables, and low public
  debt (~60% vs ~255% today). Same overlay mechanism as the others. +tests.

- **Germany per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — Germany's 46 country-relevant new
  overhaul ROOT metrics (IE's set plus `coDeterminationQuality` and `nuclearSafety`) are now
  hand-authored per Land for both eras using West/East archetype blocks. 1991 captures the
  immediate post-reunification year (Treuhand-era East collapse in output/R&D/manufacturing,
  Soviet-design nuclear legacy, debt/GDP ~40% before the reunification-cost ramp, no Energiewende);
  2019 carries real West/East spread (southern industrial R&D and robotics well above the former-GDR
  periphery, but the East leading on wind-energy transition). Same overlay mechanism as Ireland
  (`stateMetrics` + `stateBaselines`). +tests.

- **Ireland per-era new-metric presets (1991 + 2019)** — Ireland's 44 country-relevant new
  overhaul ROOT metrics now have **hand-authored, per-region values for both eras**, replacing the
  formula-derived defaults. A 1991 IE world seeds era-correct figures (pre-Celtic-Tiger R&D, energy
  transition, robotics near floor; debt/GDP ~95%; lower gender equality/civil liberties; higher
  state-media control); 2019 gets real regional spread (Dublin/Cork R&D and housing pressure well
  above the rural periphery — the old formula understated Ireland's housing crisis). Values overlay
  both `stateMetrics` and `stateBaselines` (the decay targets), so the authoring doesn't erode over
  turns. New `ieMetricPresets` bundle (single source of truth for these metrics) + generic
  `getRegionMetricPresets` selector + shared overlay helpers (DE/JP/BR/CN/UK/US to follow, one
  country at a time). +tests (coverage, bounds, era-direction, regional spread, selector, seeder
  metrics + baselines). Live 2019 worlds need a backfill migration (held) to match the authored
  values.

- **Position Editor reaches production readiness** — the admin demographics tool now persists a full
  global Layer-1 model per country+era (positions, baseline turnout rates, and archetype composition
  weights + civic multipliers) via the "Apply to seed" flow. A country-aware map and state editor
  support the US, UK, Germany, Japan, Ireland, Brazil, and China; an in-UI ON/OFF toggle controls
  the `demographicsLayer1PositionsEnabled` flag without needing an API call. The seeded override
  store is consumed by `runCoreSeed`, international seeders, and the demographics reseed path.
  +tests (persistence, seed threading, country-aware routing, toggle).

- **Crisis interaction system completion pass** — the generalized crisis decision tree is now
  functional end-to-end behind the `crisisInteractionEnabled` flag. Inflation is a first-class
  effect target that bumps `federalBudget.economicFactors.inflationRate`; terminal nodes apply
  outcomes, emit the `crisis_outcome` wire event, and resolve the interaction; collective-fund
  crises surface to national leaders worldwide; role-gated decisions use a shared role resolver so
  cabinet steps are completable; admin template UI is gated through the API instead of a
  server-only client import. +tests.

- **Historically grounded Layer-1 models for international countries** — added calibrated
  per-era Layer-1 demographic models for the United Kingdom, Germany, Japan, Ireland, Brazil, and
  China, plus a country registry and international-seed gating so reseeds only run where a model
  exists. The UK also gained its own dedicated Layer-1 model and regional-demographics builder.

- **UK regional council seat consistency + election handling** — `ensureUKRegionalCouncilElections`
  now self-heals stale `stateSenateSeats` values (e.g. 1991 seed drift) against the canonical
  `UK_REGIONAL_COUNCIL_SEATS` table before scheduling elections, so legislature pages and election
  generation agree on regional council chamber sizes. Seat-allocation tests and perpetual-election
  handling updated accordingly.

### Fixed

- **Presidential primary standings clipped the Endorse button on mobile** — the standings table on
  `/president/primary/[partyId]` sat directly inside a rounded `overflow-hidden` wrapper, so on narrow
  viewports the 11-column table was clipped with no way to scroll to the rightmost columns — including
  the per-candidate **Endorse** action. The table is now wrapped in a horizontal-scroll container
  (`overflow-x-auto`) with a `min-w` floor, matching the stock-market table pattern, so it fills wide
  screens and scrolls to reach Endorse on mobile. Display only; no logic change.

- **Stock splits silently confiscated index-fund shares** — the CEO stock-split / reverse-split
  route rebuilt the cap table from a proportional reallocation that only recognized character,
  imperial, and corporation holders. `fundId` shareholders returned no holder kind and were skipped,
  so every index fund's position was erased on each split and its weight redistributed to the
  remaining holders + public float — a free expropriation any CEO could trigger with a trivial
  split. No invariant tripped (the register stayed balanced) and the drop-guard was equally
  fund-blind. The fund's internal `holdings` ledger was untouched, so its NAV still counted shares it
  no longer owned. Splits are now fund-aware end to end: fund holders are preserved and rescaled like
  every other holder (and protected from rounding to zero), the drop-guard and the structure-change
  audit recognize funds, and the route mirrors each fund's rescaled cap-table position back into its
  holdings ledger (shares + cost basis) so NAV stays accurate. +tests. _(A separate write-down heal
  reconciles funds already affected on live; nationalization / bond-default dissolution remain
  fund-blind in shareholder payout and are tracked separately.)_

- **Nationalizing a corporation that owns shares in other corporations destroyed those shares** —
  when a corporation holding equity positions in other corporations was nationalized, its portfolio
  was silently erased during dissolution: the `nationalizeWholeCorp` engine paid out the seized
  corporation's own shareholders but never transferred or liquidated the shares it held in other
  corporations. A whole-corp taking of BYD that owned 100 shares in Baowu would pay BYD's shareholders
  but destroy the Baowu position without compensation or transfer. The engine now scans for corporations
  where the seized corporation holds shares and transfers those positions to the National Corporation
  at market value before dissolving the shell. The National Corporation receives the shares and is
  credited with their liquidation value in its home currency. +tests. _(A backfill script and admin
  healing tool are available to heal prior affected nationalizations on live worlds.)_

- **Profiles and politician pages stuck on a loading skeleton for some players** (Bug #0795) — the
  ad service worker served from `/sw.js` (`public/sw.js`) imported a remote ad script and installed
  a `fetch` handler that intercepted navigation/RSC requests. When that remote was blocked or
  unreachable, the handler broke page loads — the shell + skeleton rendered but content never swapped
  in, while the console showed a name-resolution failure and a syntax error from the injected script.
  It only affected browsers that had the worker registered, so it reproduced inconsistently. `sw.js`
  is now a self-unregistering kill-switch: it clears caches, unregisters itself, and reloads open
  tabs once (loop-safe) so affected browsers heal and run service-worker-free thereafter. +test.

- **Index funds could destroy shares from a corporation's register** — the share-debit helpers
  (`debitSharesFromFund` and its corporation/imperial siblings) forwarded the Mongo transaction
  session only to their zero-cleanup `$pull`, not to the primary debit write. When an index-fund
  cross-fund rebalance (or redemption-liquidity sale) ran inside a transaction and MongoDB retried
  the transaction body on a transient write conflict, the out-of-transaction debit committed on the
  first attempt and ran **again** on the retry, while the in-transaction credit rolled back and
  re-applied once — double-debiting the selling fund and erasing shares from the issuer's cap table
  (one live corporation drifted −3,351 shares, which blocked its stock split with "share register
  does not match total outstanding"). All debit helpers now bind both the primary write and the
  cleanup `$pull` to the caller's session, so a retry rolls the debit back atomically. +tests.

- **Economic models are national-only** — states/regions no longer have or display their own economic
  model (e.g. New York showing a "Military-Industrial Complex" distinct from the nation). A country has
  one national model that governs all its regions: the classification phase writes only the national
  doc, and the effect channels (corp alignment, sector-GDP concentration, synergy nudges) all read that
  national model for every region. The region page now shows the national model on its Economy tab
  (above the sectors board) instead of a per-region card. Live region docs had their stale `economicModel`
  cleared. The model card is also hardened to hide (not crash) on an unexpected payload.

- **Negative "spending" on the budget page** — two legislation types modeled their deregulation/cut
  options as _negative_ per-capita cost, which summed into the budget's per-category spending as a
  negative line (e.g. New York showing "Economic: −$81.0M" after enacting Streamline Licensing).
  Rebased both cost tables to be non-negative while keeping the left→right gradient: State Business
  Regulation Act `[100, 60, 0, −15, −30] → [100, 60, 20, 8, 0]` and federal Medicaid
  `[1300, 900, 0, −150, −300] → [1300, 900, 250, 100, 0]`. Deregulation/cap options are now the
  cheapest (floored at 0), never a phantom budget credit — their fiscal upside flows through the
  growth/`smallBusinessFormation` metric effects instead. +regression test (no option may carry a
  negative `annualCostPerCapita`). Fresh resets pick up the new tables; existing live enacted laws
  keep their snapshotted cost until re-enacted or healed.

- **Runaway world population from international migration** — the new cohort demographic flow consumed
  each region's `migrationRate` as a literal annual net-migration %, added it to the national headcount
  with no global renormalization, and let economic pull amplify it — so a world where most regions had
  positive migration inflated by ~5.5%/yr (live: +2M people/turn). Three fixes: (1) a **per-region cap**
  of ±1.5%/yr on net international migration (applied after economic pull, so a GDP boom can't push past
  the ceiling); (2) a **global bound** — the modeled bloc nets at most ~0.3%/yr of immigration from the
  unmodeled rest-of-world (positive nets scale down if exceeded; outflows and a net-emigrating bloc are
  untouched); (3) **retuned the authored `migrationRate` seed values** to realistic figures (CN 4.2→0,
  IE 8.5–12→0.3–0.8, DE 3.2–5.5→0.4–0.6, BR 1.8→0.1; US/UK/JP were already realistic). Natural increase
  (births − deaths, ~0.7%/yr) was correct and is unchanged. +tests.

- **German Animal Welfare Act metric effects corrected** — its `foodSecurity` primary was positive,
  implying strict animal welfare _raises_ agricultural self-sufficiency; flipped to negative, since
  welfare rules constrain intensive-livestock output and raise costs (and the bill already models a
  rural-farming burden, which the food-security gain contradicted). Also rescaled its oversized
  secondary weights (`ruralRevitalization`/`carbonEmissions` ±1.5, `socialCohesion` 1.0) into the
  normal range (−0.5 / −0.5 / 0.3) so they no longer outweigh the primary.

- **US Transportation Act budget effect was backwards** — `us_transportation` weighted
  `budgetBalance` positively, so its big spend/expand (left) options were shown improving the
  federal surplus; switched to a negative weight so transit/road _investment_ now worsens the
  balance and the privatise/eliminate (right) options improve it, matching every sibling transport
  and infrastructure bill. (Found via a broad sweep of weighted metric-effect directions; the other
  cross-country sign differences checked out as intentional, e.g. Ireland's indigenous-SME strategy
  trading off MNC-inflated headline GDP, and IE defence options being ordered cut-first vs the US.)

- **North Sea energy bill no longer claims a one-sided power-grid effect** — `uk_north_sea_energy`
  (oil/gas vs offshore-wind licensing) listed `powerGridReliability` as a left-favoring weighted
  target, so the propose preview painted "Power Grid ↑" for the green options and "↓" for drilling.
  Grid reliability genuinely cuts both ways for an energy-_source_ bill (dispatchable gas firms the
  grid; intermittent wind can stress it), so the directional grid effect was dropped from this type.
  Grid-_investment_ bills (`uk_energy_grid`, the broadband/utilities infrastructure acts) keep it,
  where the direction is clear. The bill's `gdpGrowth` effect was also flipped (negative weight):
  North Sea oil & gas is a major revenue driver, so pro-extraction options now lift near-term GDP
  and banning licensing costs it, rather than the reverse.

- **Archetype approvals on tax (and other right→left-ordered) bills were reversed** — the
  shift-based archetype-approval calculation read a policy's left/right movement from the option's
  array index, assuming index 0 = most-left. But tax brackets are authored ascending by rate
  (index 0 = 0% / most market-friendly → highest rate = most-left at the top index), so every
  tax bill flipped its approvals: libertarians and small business "approved" a 50% income tax while
  unions and public-sector workers "opposed" it. The shift direction now follows the option's
  curated economic+social position score instead of the array index, which is robust to option
  ordering. A programmatic sweep found **57 of 368 legislation types** affected (all 42 tax/levy
  types across all six countries, plus ~15 CN/DE/JP types ordered right→left); the underlying
  per-archetype affinity tables were already correct. Fixes both the propose-bill preview and the
  real approval swings applied at enactment. +tests.

- **Corporation public API exposes credit-rating components** — `/api/public/v1/corporations/*`
  responses now include the `creditRating.components` breakdown (debtToEquity, interestCoverage,
  profitability, liquidity) when `creditRatingComponents` is present on the corporation record.
- **Index fund leaderboard and rebalancing** — fund leaderboards sort by the displayed effective
  balance; cross-fund rebalancing is cadence-gated and cumulative buys are capped to prevent
  runaway churn.

### Changed

- **Contest action weakened** — Contest no longer inherits the Build Org catch-up bonus, base PS
  cost raised 2 → 3 (`CONTEST_BASE_PS_COST`), and its effect now tapers against low-Org targets
  (`CONTEST_LOW_ORG_TAPER = 10`) so a party can't be cheaply ground to 0. The revived
  unmanned-default capture shield (`DEFENSE_UNMANNED_CAPTURE_MULTIPLIER = 0.5`) halves Contest
  against a chair-less default party (`parties/unmannedDefenseShield.ts`).
- **Org enters elections on a diminishing-returns (sqrt) curve** — `orgVoteWeight =
normalizedOrgShare ^ 0.5` replaces the linear share in general elections and the poll preview,
  so a 3:1 Org lead yields ~63/37 of weight instead of 75/25. Order and the 0-Org gate preserved
  (`electionEngine/electionFormulaFactors.ts`).
- **Presidential election now uses the swing-flow engine** — the same §7.3.2 model as every other
  general election (`distributeVotesBySwingFlow`), so Reg flows through the persuasion/peel model
  and the personal-reach floor applies (a supported 0-Org candidate is no longer hard-zeroed).
  Real-world balance diagnostics still pass unchanged.
- **Registration% can climb higher and matters more** — the passive Reg target is decoupled from
  `Org − lag` (now targets Org; `REG_LAG_BELOW_ORG_PCT_BY_COUNTRY` emptied) and the governed-state
  Reg cap raised 60 → 75 (`HOME_FIELD_REG_CAP`), opening the high-resistance band the curves reward.
  Reg also now applies as a baseline nominal-share tilt (`regResistanceMultiplier`, 1.0–1.3×) in the
  swing-flow `appealWeight` and the poll preview, so entrenched registration helps a party hold its
  vote across all general elections (president + down-ballot) even without active persuasion pressure,
  on top of the existing persuasion-resistance layer.
- **Retired the legacy party-Org soft scalars** — `partyOrgScalar` / `partyOrgScalarPresidential`
  removed; general elections and polls use the normalized Org share consistently, and primaries
  apply a neutral 1× (intra-party Org cancels). Polls now model Org the same way the election does.
- **Position Editor UI redesign + clearer surfacing** — the admin position editor was rebuilt onto
  the shared design system: every hardcoded `var(--surface-*)` / raw red-green / `rgba()` value was
  migrated to semantic tokens so all 11 themes render correctly, the map view gained a proper header,
  labelled toolbar controls, and themed empty/warning states, and the state editor now uses shared
  `Button`s plus an in-app `Modal` confirm and `Toast` (replacing `window.confirm`/`alert` and a
  full-page reload on reset). The entry point under World → Demographics is now a card that pairs the
  `demographicsLayer1PositionsEnabled` gate toggle with the editor launch, instead of a bare text
  link and a context-free toggle on the reseed row. No behavior, derivation, or API changes.

- **Static images served from the R2 CDN to cut Railway egress** — the heavy `public/` assets
  (the Lincoln Memorial hero on login/register/create-character, and the site logo used in the
  navbar and Open Graph / Twitter cards) are now uploaded to Cloudflare R2 and referenced via
  `cdn.ahousedividedgame.com` instead of being re-encoded and served by the Railway container
  through `/_next/image`. The hero dropped 610 KB → 288 KB (WebP) and the logo 177 KB → 16 KB,
  and both now serve from Cloudflare's edge at zero origin egress. New constants in
  `src/lib/images/staticCdnAssets.ts`; CDN URLs are kept out of `next.config` `remotePatterns`
  and marked `unoptimized` so the optimizer can never proxy (and re-bill) them. Country map
  geometry JSON is likewise served from the CDN. Re-publish via
  `scripts/upload-static-to-r2.mjs`. Unreferenced `usmap.svg` and `cabinet-seals/` assets removed.

## [0.3.2] - 2026-06-09

### Added

- **Economic Models (P7b — effect channels).** The economic identity now grants real bonuses, applied
  at **full strength the moment a named model is active** (binary on/off — intensity is a fit/confidence
  readout, not an effect dial) and reading the LAGGED (previous-turn) model so the effect↔classification
  cycle stays a one-turn lag: (1) §6.1 **sector GDP concentration** — aligned sectors carry more weight
  in a region's revenue-weighted growth (primary +25%, secondary +10%), so a specialized economy leans
  into both the upside and the fragility; (2) §6.2 **corp margins** — primary-sector corps +8%, secondary
  +3%, off-model −2%, with a Favored/Disfavored badge on the corporation page; (3) §6.3 **metric
  synergies** — a held model nudges its synergy metrics' targets (full `maxNudge`, smoothed through the
  engine, not hard-set; node-target metrics today); (4) §6.4 **spending efficiency** — spend in the
  model's signature categories goes +12.5% further. All four are **parity-neutral only when no named
  model is held (Mixed/absent)** (golden-master-safe). The identity card is now also on region pages.
  +tests (effect helpers, binary activation, golden-master parity, the smoothed target-nudge channel).

- **Economic Models — historically-grounded per-era seed identities.** A fresh game now cold-starts
  each nation's economic model from a start-date-specific default (1991 vs 2019), chosen for real-world
  fit: US Military-Industrial (1991) → Tech-Innovation (2019); UK Financialized (both); DE Industrial
  Powerhouse (1991) → Social-Market (2019); JP Industrial Powerhouse (both); IE Agrarian (1991) →
  Tech-Innovation (2019); BR Agrarian (1991) → Resource/Extraction (2019); CN Agrarian (1991) →
  Industrial Powerhouse (2019). CN starts from its sector-supported identity rather than a forced
  State-Capitalist label — that identity EMERGES via the ≥67% state-ownership lever if the government
  nationalizes the economy (fresh games seed all sectors unowned).
  `seedEconomicModel` is now era-keyed in country config; the classification phase resolves it from
  `gameState.startingYear`. (Emergent classification still moves a country off its seed over time.)

- **Economic Models — State-Capitalist activation lever.** A command economy is now defined by STATE
  OWNERSHIP rather than its energy-sector mix: when National Corporations own ≥ 67% of ALL a country's
  (or region's) sectors — corporate-owned and unowned alike — that share drives the **State-Capitalist**
  affinity (overriding the
  weak generic signal), so heavy nationalization activates the identity. Below the threshold it falls
  back to the generic signal. The archetype's sectors still govern its effect channels when active.
  (`STATE_CAPITALIST_OWNERSHIP_THRESHOLD`, computed per region + country in the classification phase.)

- **Economic Models (P7a — classification engine + identity).** Every country and region now develops
  an emergent **economic identity** (Military-Industrial, Tech-Innovation, Financialized, Industrial
  Powerhouse, Resource/Extraction, Agrarian, Service/Consumer, Social-Market, State-Capitalist, or
  Mixed) computed each turn from a blend of sector mix (0.4, graded weighted-share), spending mix
  (0.4), and active flagship laws (0.2). Scores drift slowly (0.9 inertia) and the named model only
  flips after a challenger holds a ≥5-pt lead for a sustained 48 turns (hysteresis with grace decay,
  not hard reset). Per-country seed identities (US→Military-Industrial, CN→State-Capitalist, etc.)
  cold-start a fresh game. A new `economicModel` turn phase classifies all countries + regions
  (national = Σ regional sector revenue, SSOT) and writes the identity onto `stateMetrics`; a
  "National Economic Model" card surfaces it on the country page (name, intensity meter + qualitative
  band, signature sectors, the live "what's driving this" split, and any challenger's progress).
  **Descriptive only — zero balance impact.** The four effect channels (sector GDP concentration,
  corp bonuses, metric synergies, spending efficiency) + closed-loop damping are P7b. Excluded from
  approval (an engine intermediate). +59 tests (registry coverage, signals, hysteresis+decay, the
  phase, open-loop stability, presenter, card).

- **Dynamic fiscal-growth factors — wages & trade grow on the real economy.** The static
  `wageGrowth` (3%) and `tradeGrowth` (2%) tax-base seeds are now engine-derived metrics.
  `economic.wageGrowth` = the annualized real growth of `medianIncome` (clamped) plus a slice of
  lagged inflation (`WAGE_INFLATION_PASSTHROUGH`, calibrated so hyperinflation still compounds the
  income-tax base); `economic.tradeGrowth` = a world-trade baseline minus tariff/foreign-corporate-
  tax wedges plus FTA partners, economic-bloc membership, currency competitiveness, and
  manufacturing strength (an emergent trade-Laffer curve). A new `fiscalTradeInputs` provider feeds
  both. Both are player-visible metric cards (with thresholds) but excluded from approval (engine
  intermediates — `gdpGrowth`/`medianIncome` carry the term). `tradeGrowth` also tilts the cyclical
  GDP signal via a lagged net-exports term. The factors are ANNUAL display rates **applied per
  turn**: a new `fiscalBaseGrowth` turn phase compounds the federal + state tax bases by a
  `1/TURNS_PER_YEAR` slice each turn (mirroring how `state.gdp` already compounds) and recomputes
  federal revenue, so the treasury tracks the live economy instead of jumping once a fiscal year;
  `fiscalYear` no longer grows the bases (it would double-count). +calibration tests (BR-1991
  hyperinflation, trade-Laffer turn-over, wage↔inflation stability).

- **📊 Budget-sync — live fiscal-ratio metrics.** `debtToGdp`, `budgetBalance` (now % of GDP),
  and `schuldenbremseHeadroom` now mirror the real `federalBudget` ratios each turn via a new
  `fiscalRatios` provider + `fiscalMirror` (exact, no smoothing; they leave the coexistence
  regime — the value IS the ratio, persist-gate scoped). Schuldenbremse is scoped to 2019-era
  presets (de-seeded from the 1991 preset — debt brake = 2009). A `policyEffects` chokepoint
  (`MIRROR_CONTROLLED_METRIC_IDS`) makes the three computed metrics read-only to the policy
  layer — a law moves the budget only through its real tax/spending channel, which the mirror
  reads, so direct law targets on them no longer double-count (§4.7). Removed the anachronistic
  `futureIrelandFundBalance` metric (FIF est. 2024, after both start dates) and retired the
  `HealDebtToGdp` admin tool/route (the static drift it patched no longer exists).

- **P6d — the two-axis electorate-weighted approval CUTOVER (the overhaul's finale).** State
  approval is no longer ideology-blind. Each demographic group weights a metric's approval
  contribution by how much _its_ ideology cares (`metricAxisAffinity.ts`: a per-metric
  `{econ, social} ∈ [−1,1]` table — gdpGrowth/economicFreedom/regulatoryBurden/debtToGdp econ-right,
  poverty/inequality/joblessness econ-left, nationalPride/militaryReadiness/stateMediaControl/crime
  authoritarian, civilLiberties/pressFreedom libertarian, everything else universal), and the
  state's approval is the population-weighted blend of its groups. The weight is
  `1 + k·(econLeanNorm·econAff + socialLeanNorm·socialAff)` floored at 0; **k=1.0** so a maximally
  aligned group double-weights a metric and a maximally opposed group ignores it. A conservative
  electorate now rewards growth/freedom/order/pride; a progressive one services/equality; a
  libertarian one civil liberties — **no single agenda is best everywhere** (the deepest fix for the
  "left-wing meta"). The six P6a axis metrics are un-excluded atomically and finally score approval.
  **Four blocking gates, all green:** (1) golden master — k=0 reproduces the ideology-blind average
  exactly; (2) live-shift — every representative state (incl. a polarized one) moves ≤5 pts vs
  pre-cutover (the cutover opens a ~3pt conservative-vs-progressive gap on a polarized state); (3)
  downstream parity — no discrete decision keys off government approval (OPS collapse uses
  ruling-party confidence, NPP reads none, elections/coattails consume it as a continuous swing), so
  the bounded shift can't flip a discrete outcome; (4) the P4 approval feedback loop re-proven damped
  with the weighting active. Threaded EVERYWHERE approval is computed from metrics — the per-turn
  snapshot, both metric API routes, AND the gameplay drivers that recompute it on the fly:
  `getStateApprovalForElection` (so the electorate weighting decides election vote swing — the full
  "who you govern" payoff, with an election-reach gate: identical metrics + opposite electorates ⇒
  different election approval), UK independence-desire drift, governor coattail magnitude, and the
  map/approval display surfaces. A shared `loadElectorateGroups`/`weightingFor` helper keys groups by
  `${countryId}:${stateId}` to avoid cross-country state-ID collisions. k and the affinities are
  first-pass (the headline balance knobs). **F-8 rollout caveat:** the payoff is fullest where laws are axis-differentiated —
  it excludes JP until its both-law audit fully lands and BR entirely (no legislation); the live
  axis-metric backfill (still local) is needed before the six metrics bite on live data.
- **P6c — law axis-tag corrections + right-option metric symmetry (Prong C).** Three pieces. (1)
  Axis-tag fixes: defense spending (US/UK + Trident) economic→both (fiscal AND
  authoritarian/security), senate filibuster rules both→social (pure procedure), workforce
  development ×2 both→economic (skills/labor). (2) JP both-law audit: five genuinely-social JP laws
  re-tagged off the economic axis (policing, criminal justice, regional policing, gender equality,
  digital governance — civil-liberties matters whose budget is incidental). (3) Symmetry sweep: a
  new guard (`policySymmetry.test.ts`) asserts every regulatory law's RIGHT options improve a
  natural metric for their domain, not merely reduce a left-favored one — tax cuts lift small
  business formation (via the shared `taxRateOptions` helper, all tax brackets), deregulation lifts
  economic freedom, immigration limits ease housing-cost pressure, tough-on-crime lowers crime,
  state-media/nationalist laws lift national pride. Funding laws are exempt by construction (a
  spending cut already helps the budget, a buildup already helps its channel outcome — the tradeoff
  is structural, and re-pointing a channel readout would double-count it per §4.7). 36 regulatory
  laws across all five countries gained a right-improving weighted target. First-pass magnitudes,
  flagged for the balance pass. This is the content half of the "no universally optimal agenda"
  fix; the electorate-weighting cutover (P6d) is what makes the axis tags bite.
- **P6b — country social-axis position + drift.** Each country now carries a runtime social-axis
  position on the −5 (libertarian) … +5 (authoritarian) scale shared with demographic leans and
  legislation-option scores, seeded from a per-country baseline (`CountryConfig.socialAxisBaseline`:
  CN +3.5 authoritarian; US/UK/IE −1.5 libertarian-leaning; DE/JP/BR mid). A new sequential turn
  phase (`socialAxisDrift`, after bill enactment) EMA-pulls the position toward the social stance of
  national laws enacted that turn — but only laws whose type is socially differentiated (some option
  carries a non-zero social score), so economic-only legislation never drags the axis. A centrist
  option of a genuinely social law does moderate it. Net per-turn drift is capped at ±0.5 and the
  position is clamped to ±5; a watermark turn prevents double-counting. The field self-heals onto
  countryState docs that predate it (no migration). Read surface: the National Policy page header
  shows a National Outlook strip pairing this social label with an economic lean aggregated from the
  page's law records. This is the auth↔lib compass machinery; the P6d cutover is what makes it
  approval-relevant. First-pass drift constants flagged for the global balance pass.
- **P6a — the two-axis compass metrics (authored EXCLUDED from approval).** Six new metrics give every
  quadrant a scoreboard: civilLiberties (libertarian anchor ← pressFreedom − stateMediaControl − mass
  incarceration − social credit, all LIVE chains), nationalPride (authoritarian ← militaryReadiness +
  growth), militaryReadiness (universal defense-channel capacity; **bundeswehrReadiness reconciled into
  its DE tracker** — no double-author), stateMediaControl (root), economicFreedom (econ-right ←
  regulatoryBurden + smallBusinessFormation), regulatoryBurden (root). ALL SIX sit in
  APPROVAL_EXCLUDED_METRICS at birth (introducing them would shift every region's approval via the
  sum/count denominator) and enter the basket atomically at the P6d cutover — a live gate asserts ZERO
  approval contribution even at extreme values. Uniform seed derivations + a dry-run live backfill
  script (prepared, NOT run). **Law homes:** new Recruitment/National Service Acts in all six countries
  (the service model is conscription STRUCTURE the defense channel can't express — pinned §4.7 keeps;
  readiness/pride vs civilLiberties trade), new IE/JP Media & Press Freedom Acts (the missing
  pressFreedom/stateMediaControl wiring homes), uk_surveillance_privacy re-targeted onto civilLiberties,
  and stateMediaControl secondaries reconciled onto the existing US/CN state-media laws (BBC funding
  deliberately skipped — publicly funded ≠ state-controlled). Sims: defense buildup → readiness → pride
  with the DE tracker in lockstep; a security-state turn erodes liberties through the live policing →
  incarceration chain; deregulation lifts economic freedom. (F-8 rollout caveat: the meta-fix payoff
  arrives at P6d, and excludes JP until its both-law audit and BR entirely.)

- **P5 — the living country indices.** The defense spending channel (7th category) plus 7 country-flavor
  nodes in `registry/governance.ts`: devolutionSatisfaction (tracks the live publicTrust, uniform-aligned;
  devolution acts via coexistence) → independenceDesire (SCT/WLS/NIR seed deltas survive via parity;
  devolution buy-in defuses it, economic grievance feeds it), IE unityReferendumSupport (hardship +
  cohesion driven), DE rentenStabilitaet (**pension stability vs the live P1 dependency ratio** + the
  social channel's pension spend), DE bundeswehrReadiness (defense-channel capacity stock),
  roboticsAdoption (manufacturing + R&D roots, uniform-aligned), IE directProvisionLoad (live
  migrationRate vs social capacity). Sims: the corruption-scandal → trust → devolution → independence
  chain runs end-to-end through live edges; aging erodes pensions with funding cushioning; defense
  buildup builds readiness; migration surges strain direct provision. §4.7: de_bundeswehr_funding
  re-pointed to its fiscal dimension (readiness is channel-derived); 9 trust/devolution/robotics
  pass-through secondaries stripped (jp/cn research acts re-pointed to the R&D root); pinned keeps for
  automation-policy (de_robotics_ai, cn_ai_strategy → roboticsAdoption) and asylum-system structure
  (ie_immigration_asylum → directProvisionLoad). Legislation alignment: the two P6.2b mis-tags fixed
  (cn_internet_governance, de_constitutional_protection positioning both→social), tax-bracket economic
  positioning verified (explicit per-bracket values — no fix needed), and a socialCreditCoverage
  consistency guard (exactly the 2 known @1.0 primaries, all weights sane). DEFERRED per design: JP law
  re-tag → P6 (the "predominantly both" premise fails — JP is 117 economic / 53 social / 53 both; axis
  tags are inert until Prong A), BR build-out → own project, new Recruitment/media laws → P6 Prong C,
  schuldenbremse/futureIrelandFund 📊 syncs → the pending budget-sync decision. Pure 🔧 roots stay
  policy-driven (partyDiscipline, socialCredit, taiwanStrait, beltAndRoad, euCohesion, coDetermination,
  hukou, …).

- **P4 — the living legitimacy tier (governance + media; the last cross-country tier).** Seven metrics
  ENGINE-DERIVED across two new registries plus provider #2 (`governmentApproval` — one batched read of the
  canonical approval store, sliced per country; the approval phase writes AFTER the engine, so nodes read
  LAST turn's approval — the lag that keeps the loop damped). Media (`registry/mediaInformation.ts`):
  socialMediaSentiment (bipolar ← approval + growth − unemployment, fast inertia), mediaPolarization
  (← inequality + souring mood; **hand-back #2: socialCohesion's polarization read is now a live topo edge**
  — gini → sentiment → polarization → cohesion → education), newsTrust (← pressFreedom root −
  polarization), disinformationRisk, UK-origin bbcTrust (tracks newsTrust, uniform-aligned). Governance
  (`registry/governance.ts`): corruptionIndex (← transparency root + elite-capture inequality),
  publicTrust (← approval − unemployment − corruption + newsTrust, 2dp). **The approval feedback loop is
  sim-proven damped with the REAL approval function in the circuit** (settles at constant inputs, absorbs
  20/75 approval shocks convergently, propagates an unemployment shock without divergence). §4.7
  (root-vs-readout — this tier has no spending channel): 20 ethics/media laws stripped of pass-through
  secondaries (transparency→corruption, pressFreedom→newsTrust/disinformation); de/ie_government_ethics
  primaries re-pointed to the transparency root; cn_anticorruption_campaign keeps corruption (enforcement
  mechanism) and lost its transparency secondary instead. Weak chain hops (→publicTrust/bbcTrust, gain
  ≈0.125) deliberately NOT flagged — distinct trust mechanisms, named for balance. Roots stay:
  pressFreedom, governmentTransparency, all country governance specials (P5), budgetBalance/debtToGdp
  (the flagged sync decision), voterTurnout (election engine).

- **P3d — the living social-outcomes tier (housing + cohesion; completes P3).** Eight metrics are now
  ENGINE-DERIVED in `registry/social.ts`: housingAffordability (redefined — see Fixed — as a 0-100 cost
  PRESSURE index ← costOfLiving + the housingSupplyGrowth root), homelessnessRate (pressure + poverty −
  mentalHealthAccess), socialMobility (schooling − inequality − lagged poverty, generational inertia),
  socialCohesion (− inequality − unemployment − mediaPolarization root; **the hand-back: education's
  highSchoolGradRate seed read resolved into a live same-turn topo edge**, 2dp storage against the
  plateau class), civicParticipation (schooling + cohesion + publicTrust root), UK roughSleeping
  (homelessness/5, uniform-aligned), IE vacantPropertyRate + rentalPressureIndex (housing-pressure
  variants). The medianIncome affordability term is DROPPED (local-currency, the established precedent —
  named). Sims: housing-chain response (supply root relieves pressure→homelessness→roughSleeping +
  IE variants), the cohesion hand-back both ways (social investment lifts schooling; polarization drags
  it), full-cluster convergence. §4.7: the social sweep gained the 8 new readouts + per-law pinned keeps
  (kita/childcare/training pipelines → socialMobility; integration/composition/community services →
  socialCohesion); uk_housing_planning + uk_regional_housing_development re-pointed to the
  housingSupplyGrowth root they actually control.

- **P3c — the living environment tier (carbon from the real sector mix).** Five metrics are now
  ENGINE-DERIVED: carbonEmissions (tons/capita from a CARBON_INTENSITY-weighted revenue mix via the
  sectorRevenueTax provider — extended with `sectorType`, the planned one-provider extension — minus the
  renewables root and the environment spending channel), airQuality (AQI, lower-is-better ← carbon +
  urbanization − renewables; 1dp storage against the rounding-plateau class), energyTransitionProgress
  (renewables+10 + channel, the uniform-seed alignment), UK-origin floodRisk (18 − climateResilience·0.12),
  and IE agriEmissionsShare (agriculture's share of intensity-weighted sector emissions). Roots
  (renewableEnergy, recyclingRate, climateResilience, protectedLand, naturalDisasterPreparedness,
  nuclearSafety) stay policy-driven. **The carbon→air→lifeExpectancy chain is live** (sims prove
  convergence, the spend response, the renewables response, and industrialization-vs-tertiarization mix
  dynamics); lifeExpectancy moved to 2dp storage with the AQI slope strengthened to ~2.5 years across the
  clean↔smog span (0.02 was sub-grain — the plateau class). §4.7 sweep: 10 environment-budget laws — 4
  pass-through secondaries dropped (air/transition/carbon/floodRisk follow the engine), jp_renewable_energy
  re-pointed carbon→renewables root; per-law allowed-readout keep-list (net-zero/climate REGULATORY
  mandates on carbon, nuclear COMPOSITION displacement on carbon, regional SUB-ALLOCATION on airQuality).

- **P3b — the living public-safety tier (closes the poverty↔crime cycle).** All 8 publicSafety metrics are
  now ENGINE-DERIVED on the generic wiring: policePerCapita (capacity stock from the publicSafety spending
  channel), crimeRate (per-100k hub ← LAGGED poverty + LAGGED unemployment + highSchoolGradRate +
  policePerCapita + Gini-100), violentCrimeRate (crime-correlated share), incarcerationRate (lagged crime,
  long inertia — sentencing laws act via coexistence deltas), recidivismRate (incarceration + unemployment −
  the social channel), publicSafetyConfidence, and the UK specials antiSocialBehaviourRate/knifeCrimeRate
  (persist-gate scoped). povertyRate's `{lagged: crimeRate}` edge is live both ways — co-sims prove the
  two-way lagged loop is damped, police investment lowers poverty through the lag, and social investment
  lowers crime through the other lag. povertyRate storage moved to 2dp (the lagged crime term stepped below
  1dp's half-grain — the P2c rounding-plateau class). §4.7 sweep: 8 publicSafety-budget funding laws
  re-pointed/stripped (UK policing/regional-policing + JP policing/regional → community-cohesion mechanisms
  only; JP criminal-justice primary → incarcerationRate); the guard now pins PER-LAW allowed readouts
  (uk_prison_rehabilitation, jp/cn criminal-justice structure, cn_public_security surveillance mandates,
  uk_regional_justice/emergency-services sub-allocation — emergency services lost its crimeRate secondary).

- **P3a — the living income/poverty cluster + two unit repairs.** Six metrics are now ENGINE-DERIVED:
  medianIncome (RELATIVE form — grows its own baseline by productivity + labor-tightness, currency-safe
  across all six economies), costOfLiving (urbanization-set structural drift), incomeInequality (Gini-100),
  povertyRate (the P0 reference node REGISTERED live — unemployment + inequality + lagged crime + the new
  social spending channel), childPoverty (poverty-amplified; feeds the education tier's lagged inputs LIVE),
  foodInsecurity. Two new spending channels: `social` (summing the social/welfare/socialSecurity budget
  keys) and `environment`. UNIT REPAIRS (live bugs): crimeRate/incarcerationRate are per-100k but bounds
  capped at 80 → values were ceiling-clamped live (now [0,15000]/[0,1200]); incomeInequality was stored as
  Gini FRACTIONS against [25,60] bounds → the floor rewrote every value UP to 25 (now unified on the Gini
  INDEX ×100: defs/THRESHOLDS/44 seed sites rescaled + a local dry-run migration script prepared, NOT run).
  The bounds-containment allow-list is now EMPTY. §4.7: a social-spending sweep guard with a
  transfer-incidence keep-list (pension/welfare acts encode WHO transfers reach — sub-population allocation
  the uniform channel cannot express; overlap named for the balance pass). Sim catches fixed: enrollment
  nodes' medianIncome LEVEL terms dropped (perpetual-growth creep + currency-unsafe — named for a
  normalized affordability term); testPerformance stored at 1dp (rounding-plateau). medianIncome-level
  poverty relief likewise named pending currency normalization.

- **P2d — the living TFP loop (completes P2).** `TFP_BASELINE = 1.2` in potential growth is replaced by a
  living basket (`tfpBasket` in `potentialGrowth.ts`): R&D intensity + workforceSkill (P2a hand-off) + the
  infrastructure composite (transportEfficiency/broadband/grid — P2c hand-off) + SATURATING urbanization
  agglomeration (audit-C3 damping: concave + one-turn-lagged inputs), each as a bounded
  deviation-from-reference so an average region's TFP = 1.2 exactly (parity by construction; P1c
  golden-master untouched; clamped [0.2, 2.6]). Education, infrastructure, and R&D policy now drive long-run
  potential growth → gdpGrowth → Okun. `economic.productivityGrowth` is animated from the same formula
  (cold-start parity-preserving derived node). §4.7 sweep: 6 productivityGrowth secondaries dropped where the
  law's primary is already a basket root (rdIntensity/broadbandAccess/apprenticeshipRate pass-throughs);
  4 non-basket-mechanism laws keep theirs (robotics, digital governance, SME, labor standards — named for
  the balance pass). Growth-band sim: realistic TFP spread ±~1.2pp, bounded.

- **P2c — live infrastructure with maintenance-decay.** All 7 infrastructure metrics are now
  ENGINE-DERIVED nodes (`registry/infrastructure.ts`): roadCondition, publicTransit, broadbandAccess,
  waterQuality, powerGridReliability are CAPITAL STOCKS that erode each turn unless infrastructure
  spending holds them up (sim-proven: sustained underfunding visibly degrades them; restored funding
  re-pins them); infrastructureInvestmentGap tracks needed-vs-actual spend; transportEfficiency
  (transit+roads composite) is the P2d TFP hand-off. Two mechanism findings baked in: the decay must run
  on the node's simBASELINE (decaying the stored value is exactly cancelled by the coexistence policy-delta
  preservation), and capital nodes store at 2dp (per-turn decay steps ~0.05 stall on a 0.1 rounding grain).
  §4.7 sweep: 11 metric-specific acts (broadband/rail/transit/utilities/grid/roads) keep their direct
  effects as SUB-ALLOCATION mechanisms; all 7 `infrastructureInvestmentGap` law targets stripped (it is the
  channel's own readout) + a nothing-targets-the-gap guard.

- **P2b — live healthcare outcomes + the mortality loop, real units.** Nine healthcare metrics are now
  ENGINE-DERIVED nodes (`registry/healthcare.ts`): physicianRate (spend-built capacity), affordabilityIndex,
  preventableMortality (per-100k), lifeExpectancy (YEARS), mentalHealthAccess, elderCareQuality, plus
  UK/IE specials (nhsWaitingTime, socialCareQuality, hseWaitingListMonths) — driven by the healthcare
  spending channel + access/coverage roots (uninsuredRate, publicHealthPreparedness, slaintecareProgress) and
  elder DEMAND via `population.dependencyRatio`. The healthcare→demographics loop is closed with real units:
  `healthcareMortalityModifier` consumed lifeExpectancy/preventableMortality as 0-100 indices centered at 50 —
  wrong for years/per-100k (post-S1, per-100k seeds pegged the modifier at the 1.4 max everywhere); it now
  normalizes over each metric's THRESHOLDS span (sim-tested: damped, stable, better healthcare → fewer
  deaths). The engine's generic persist now SKIPS metrics a region never stored (UK-only metrics no longer
  introduced onto US/JP/CN docs, which would have shifted approval — audit-5). §4.7 sweep: 13 healthcare
  funding/coverage laws re-pointed to roots across US/UK/DE/JP; 7 sub-allocation/price-mechanism laws keep
  their direct effects (documented keep-list + guard). uninsuredRate floor widened to 0
  (universal-coverage containment).

- **P2a — budget→metrics spending backbone + live education outcomes.** A once-per-turn
  `spendingProvider` rolls federal + state `spending.byCategory` into per-region per-capita spend and feeds
  the metric engine (`ctx.spending` was `{}` since P0). Seven education metrics are now ENGINE-DERIVED DAG
  nodes (`registry/education.ts`): highSchoolGradRate, testPerformance, literacyRate, collegeEnrollment,
  workforceSkill, gcseAttainment, universityEnrollment — driven by the diminishing-returns education spending
  channel + cross-metric edges (lagged childPoverty, academicPressure/apprenticeshipRate roots). The phase
  now wires non-bespoke registry nodes GENERICALLY from their declared inputs (projection, prev/simBaseline,
  seedCurrent, persistence) — adding a category = registering nodes. Cold-start is parity-preserving by
  construction (first live turn writes back the stored value; approval shift < 0.1pt — gate-tested), so the
  seed governs the LEVEL and the engine governs the RESPONSE to spending/poverty changes. §4.7 sweep:
  14 education-FUNDING laws re-pointed off the now-spend-driven readouts to root drivers (educationSpending,
  rdIntensity, apprenticeshipRate) across US/UK/DE/CN/IE/JP; 5 regulatory/price-mechanism laws keep their
  direct effects (documented keep-list + guard test). Multi-turn sim stability + perf gates green.

- **P1d-3 — region-page surfacing of the GDP/population engine.** The Metrics tab (economic category)
  now shows a GDP decomposition card: GDP level, annualized growth, the potential-vs-cyclical split
  (`gdpGrowth = potential + cyclical`), output gap, and labor force — fed by a new `economyStock` block on
  the region `metrics` route. The Demographics tab gains a `LivePopulationPanel`: live population + growth,
  median age, sex ratio, dependency ratio, realized net migration, a hand-rolled sex-split age pyramid
  (binned from the cohort vector), and a per-region census/reapportionment notice — fed by a new region
  `population` route. The census phase now persists a `gameState.lastCensus` seat-change summary so the
  notice is real, not synthetic. `src/lib/demographics/pyramidBins.ts`,
  `src/lib/metricEngine/gdpDecomposition.ts`, `src/components/metrics/GdpDecompositionCard.tsx`,
  `src/components/demographics/AgePyramid.tsx`, `src/components/demographics/LivePopulationPanel.tsx`,
  `src/app/api/country/[code]/region/[id]/population/route.ts`.
- **Crisis interaction system (behind the `crisisInteractionEnabled` flag)** — generalizes the
  sovereign-default decision model to all crisis types: per-crisis multiple-choice decision trees
  (choice / collective / terminal nodes), ambient crisis cards on the actions page, an interactive
  decision panel on the crisis detail page (`/world/crises/[id]`), role-gated decisions (head of
  state / cabinet / governor), collaborative "collective fund" contributions that draw from the
  national treasury and shorten crisis duration, an admin template/creation panel, and a migration
  for legacy sovereign-default records (`npm run migrate:sovereign-crises`). The completion pass
  made the system functional end-to-end: crisis effects bind to real `stateMetrics` fields (they
  previously `$inc`-ed nonexistent paths and did nothing); **inflation is a first-class effect
  target** that bumps the real `federalBudget.economicFactors.inflationRate` (the per-turn inflation
  recalc then decays the shock through inertia) rather than a metric proxy; terminal nodes apply
  their outcome effects, emit the `crisis_outcome` wire event, and resolve the interaction (they
  previously dead-ended); the `interact` route resolves the interaction from the crisis id and
  derives roles via the same shared helper as the read path (cabinet-gated steps were previously
  un-completable); contributions and budget/approval gates read the real
  `federalBudget.treasuryBalance` / `governmentApproval` instead of a fictional `countries`
  collection; collective-fund crises surface to national leaders worldwide so anyone can contribute,
  not just the affected region; neither the actions-page card nor the admin panel imports a
  server-only DB helper into the client bundle; admin-created interactive crises create their
  interaction document immediately; and the engine tests run on the shared `MockDb` instead of a
  live MongoDB. +tests.

- **Cross-fund rebalancing market + outcome news wire** — the index-fund cron gained a cross-fund
  rebalancing pass: overweight funds can sell constituent shares directly to underweight funds of the
  same anchor currency, moving shares (atomic cap-table `$inc`) and cash in one settlement and logging
  `cross_fund_buy` / `cross_fund_sell` fund transactions plus public trade history. Separately, resolving
  a random event can now emit a system news post (the "outcome news wire") summarizing the chosen option
  and its tier outcome. +tests (value conservation, FX-scoping, wire emission).

- **Country-specific random events (US/UK/JP/DE)** — a PREE country-definition layer plus per-country
  event handlers seed scripted/random events for the United States, United Kingdom, Japan, and Germany,
  each with option sets, default options, and tier outcome tables. A new admin endpoint
  (`POST /api/admin/events/definitions/approve-all`) bulk-approves draft definitions whose options match
  a registered handler and reports any whose option ids / default drifted (so admins re-seed rather than
  publish buttons the resolve route would reject); the Random Events manager surfaces per-option outcomes
  and the news-wire badge.

- **Position Editor + feature-gated Layer-1 position model** — a new admin tool (`/admin/position-editor`,
  linked from World → Demographics) shows the US map shaded by computed state lean for a selected era;
  clicking a state opens a 3-tab editor (Layer 1 Demographics · Archetype Composition Rates · Final
  Composition) with a live, turnout-weighted state-lean preview, per-dimension Σ=100 validation, a
  "feeds N archetypes" coupling cue, auto-save to local storage, and CSV export of mockup seeds. Behind
  it, a gated derivation (`demographicsLayer1PositionsEnabled` on `gameState`, off by default; admin
  toggle at `/api/admin/demographics/layer1-positions/toggle`, surfaced as an in-UI ON/OFF control in
  the World → Demographics manager next to Reseed) makes archetype econ/social positions
  derive from Layer-1 demographic positions (state-share-weighted) instead of the legacy ideology-net
  path. "Apply to seed" now persists the full authored global model for a country+era — positions,
  baseline turnout rates, and per-archetype composition (Layer-1 weights + civic multiplier) — which
  `runCoreSeed` (per era), the international seeders (UK/DE/JP/IE/BR/CN), and the demographics reseed
  (US/2019) all consume; per-state Layer-1 shares remain census-driven (preview-only in the editor).
  When enabled this is an intentional, bounded rebalance. The election engine is untouched; only
  seed-time derivation changes. +tests (directional sanity, civicMultiplier exclusion, engine parity,
  gate, CSV, storage, turnout/composition override threading).

- **Operations subtab refinements** — the corporation CEO "Operations" subtab now groups the
  "By Type" bulk controls under collapsible per-country accordions (the home country expands by
  default), and "By Sector" gains a per-sector growth control with the same projected-cost preview +
  confirm as the bulk controls. The single-sector growth endpoint (`POST /sectors/[id]/growth`)
  gained an optional `preview` flag (returns projected/current/delta cost, writes nothing).

- **Bulk sector growth & production by type** — a CEO can now set a growth target and/or production
  policy across every one of a corporation's (or National Corporation's) holdings of a sector type
  — or corporate-wide — in a single atomic, country-scoped action, instead of editing each state's
  holding one at a time. Growth shows a projected per-turn cost (and delta vs current) before you
  confirm; production applies directly. New `POST /api/corporations/[id]/sectors/bulk` endpoint
  (CEO-only, rate-limited, one summary audit entry) replaces the previous per-holding request loop
  used by the production "By Type / Corporate-Wide" controls. Per-holding editing is unchanged. The
  corporation CEO "Production" subtab is now "Operations" and pairs growth with production, matching
  the National Corporation panel. +tests.

- **Public API rate-limit headers + key rotation** — successful and throttled public API
  (`/api/public/v1/*`) responses now carry `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and
  `X-RateLimit-Reset` (epoch seconds) so integrators can see remaining quota before hitting a 429.
  `checkRateLimit` now reports `limit`/`remaining`/`resetAt` (additive — existing callers unchanged).
  Users can rotate an API key via `POST /api/settings/user-api-keys/[id]/rotate`: a fresh token is
  issued for the same scope/name and the old key keeps working for a 24h grace window before
  auto-revoking, so callers can swap over without downtime. Key validation now honors the
  `revokeAt` grace timestamp. Admins can rotate any key for incident response via
  `POST /api/admin/user-api-keys/[id]/rotate` — ownership is preserved (the admin does not take the
  key) and the new secret is returned once to hand to the owner over a secure channel. +new tests.

- **Public API access logging (instrumentation)** — every authenticated public API
  (`/api/public/v1/*`) request now records a bounded `apiAccessLog` row (path, method, client IP,
  user agent, auth type, owning user/key, status) via a fire-and-forget helper that never blocks or
  breaks a request. Rows TTL-expire after 30 days. This is Phase-1 instrumentation only — it enforces
  nothing — and lays the groundwork for later enumeration / polling abuse detection. The
  `requireHumanSession` / `requireHumanSessionWithCharacter` guards (the ~99 player action routes)
  log too, so internal action traffic is covered alongside the public API. New indexes:
  a unique `tokenHash` index plus a per-user/scope index on `userApiKeys` (the API-key auth hot
  path), and TTL + path/user query indexes on `apiAccessLog`, all under a new `indexesApiAccess`
  seeder target. +new tests.

- **Durable (Mongo-backed) rate limiting for high-value endpoints** — login, registration, API-key
  creation, and the public API (`/api/public/v1/*`) now run a hybrid limiter: the existing in-memory
  check is a zero-latency fast-reject, and a
  MongoDB-backed fixed-window counter (`rateLimitBuckets`, TTL self-cleaning) is the authoritative cap.
  Limits now survive deploys and are shared across replicas, so an attacker can't reset their window by
  waiting for a restart and horizontal scaling no longer multiplies the effective limit. If the durable
  store is unavailable the limiter fails open to the in-memory result, so a storage hiccup never
  hard-blocks legitimate traffic. +new tests.

- **API abuse detection (report-only)** — a new detector scans recent `apiAccessLog` rows and flags
  scraping patterns per actor (account, API key, or IP): sustained high request volume, fixed-interval
  scripted polling (low inter-arrival variance), and sequential-id enumeration across an endpoint
  template. An hourly cron scans and persists results to `apiAbuseScans` (30-day TTL); admins review
  them in the Admin panel under **Players → API Abuse**, which shows the latest scan and can trigger
  a live re-scan via `GET /api/admin/api-abuse` (`?live=1`). It only reports — nothing is throttled
  or blocked — and is tuned to avoid flagging a moderator who simply keeps refreshing. +new tests.

- **Origin validation on human-only action endpoints** — the `requireHumanSession` /
  `requireHumanSessionWithCharacter` guards (used on ~99 state-changing action routes — attacks,
  political operations, endorsements, etc.) now reject cross-origin requests. A request whose
  `Origin`/`Referer` host is neither the request Host nor an allowlisted origin
  (`NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` + the `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` env) gets a 403. Header-less requests are allowed
  (the `sameSite: lax` cookie is the backstop there). Defense-in-depth against CSRF and a deterrent
  to off-site automation; deployment-agnostic via the Host comparison. +new tests.

- **Automation detection flag (Suspicious Activity)** — the suspicious-activity turn phase now flags
  likely autoclicker / scripted play. A new action-type-agnostic detector scans each actor's
  `actionLogs` timeline for a **fixed-phase cadence** signature — a single action type, or a
  cross-action "loop", recurring at the same ±15s offset past the turn boundary for ≥3
  **consecutive** turns (a missed turn breaks the run). Flagged accounts get an `automation_timing`
  flag reading "Potential automation due to
  {reason} for {N} hours by {user}", with the exact flagging actions and timestamps recorded in the
  flag. The shared Admin + Moderator **Suspicious Activity** tab gains an Automation filter (with a
  count) and an expandable action/time evidence list. To make economic abuse visible to the detector,
  the player corp economic endpoints that previously logged nothing (sector attack, unowned split,
  share buy/sell, sector buy/growth) now write `actionLogs` rows via a shared `logEconomicAction`
  helper, and the detector's candidate set was widened to include accounts that acted recently — so
  headless scripts that never load the site are still evaluated. +18 tests.

- **Banned accounts clear out of the Suspicious Activity queue** — banning a user now auto-resolves
  their suspicious-activity flags (archived to the resolved pool, marked reversible); unbanning
  restores only those ban-resolved records to active so the detector re-evaluates the account and
  future flags surface normally (a moderator's permanent dismissals stay resolved). The detector
  also skips currently-banned accounts so no new flags accrue while banned. Deleted-account flags
  remain shown only under the Deleted Accounts filter (now guarded by a test). Also fixed the
  Suspicious Activity tab's Ban button, which posted the wrong field name and silently failed. +7
  tests.

- **Executive mastheads show the real-world head-of-government seal** — the generated emblem on the
  Executive surface is replaced by the country's actual executive seal (US Presidential Seal, UK
  Royal Arms, German federal eagle, Japanese PM emblem, Irish harp, PRC national emblem; BR/NG
  configured too) on an ivory medallion so transparent heraldry stays legible on the dark band.
  Sourced from Wikimedia Commons at the allowlist-safe 330px width, with `next/image` optimization
  bypassed (Wikimedia throttles server-side fetches) and a graceful fall back to the generated
  `NationalSeal` if a remote asset fails. Executive only — National Policy and the Central Bank keep
  their generated marks. +5 tests.

- **Cabinet pages gain Overview / Admin tabs (all countries)** — both cabinet clients (US
  Senate-confirmation and the parliamentary direct-appointment flow) now carry a tab strip under
  the hero: Overview shows the existing page, and a red Admin tab (admins only, matching the
  StatePageTabs convention) exposes seat management backed by the new
  `POST /api/admin/country/[code]/cabinet` route — direct appointment that bypasses the PM/Senate
  flow (replace semantics, syncs both cabinet collections in the parliamentary family, sets
  `currentOffice` + career history, clears cooldowns, and closes any open Senate nomination for the
  seat so the US "awaiting Senate vote" banner can't reference a now-filled seat), removal with
  **no** re-appointment cooldown, and a Reset Cooldown action that clears the 12-hour firing
  cooldown. The route is
  runtime-government-type aware, head-of-government seats stay off-limits (Appoint-Premier flow
  owns those), and NPPs cannot be seated. +9 tests (7 route, 2 RTL tab gating).

- **"Cabinet Office" entry in the Nation dropdown** — cabinet members get a direct link to their
  own office page (`/country/{code}/executive/cabinet/{position}/office`) in the Home Nation
  section of the Nation dropdown (and the mobile Nation section), hidden for everyone else. The
  `client-nav` cabinet lookup now also covers the parliamentary direct-appointment seats
  (UK/DE/JP/IE/CN `ukCabinetMembers`), not just the US Senate-confirmation flow, via a tested
  `resolveCabinetOfficeNavEntry` helper — so the existing "My Office" mobile entry and State
  dropdown link light up for parliamentary ministers too. Replaces the dropdown's old UK-only
  roster fetch.

- **Central Bank pages get the monetary masthead and the rate corridor**
  (`update/country-pages-ui`, Phase 5 — final phase of the country-pages modernization). The
  hand-rolled hero + stats strip becomes the shared institution masthead **keyed off the bank, not
  the country**: $ Federal Reserve / £ Bank of England / ¥ 中国人民银行 / ¥ 日本銀行 — and DE + IE
  both render the € European Central Bank record ("Monetary Authority · Eurosystem") with its own
  EU palette, via `getBankId`. The prime rate is the masthead's headline number; chair + term and a
  gold "FOREX intervention active" chip sit in the band; the fused tile strip carries Inflation
  (with the full breakdown tooltip), Savings Flow, and Reserves (from the balance sheet, in the
  bank's home currency). The Overview gains the **rate corridor**: the prime rate as a stepped line
  over the inflation band across the last 60 turns, with a computed stance verdict beneath
  ("Rate sits +1.15 above inflation — restrictive stance · inflation cooling" — tested
  `corridorVerdict` / `inflationTrendLabel`, never editorial). The standalone inflation card it
  replaces lives on in the strip tile; chair selection/nominations/lobbying, Economic Trends, the
  credit-rating scale, and all tabs (Savings / LoC / Financials / FX Intervention / Admin) are
  unchanged.

- **National Policy becomes the statute book — with a Living Code timeline**
  (`update/country-pages-ui`, Phase 4). The bare-h1 accordion is now the country's code of law: a
  §/法 identity masthead (same treasury grammar) carrying the P0 National Ideology axes in its right
  slot, statute/title counts, and a URL-persisted **Code | Record** toggle. Code view: a sticky
  Titles rail whose rows each carry a per-domain lean mini-tick (from the tested `computeDomainAxes`
  — strictly grouped records, never a hardcoded domain list; axis-less domains show a muted "—", not
  a fake zero) beside the statute book — serif law names, mono provenance sublines joined from
  `enactedLaws` ("enacted 1991 · Jun 4 · per {bill title}"), axis badges, and fiscal chips computed
  with the budget page's exact cost context (collapsing where cost data isn't seeded); the existing
  metric-effect pills and tariff/subsidy treatments carry over. Record view: every enactment plotted
  as a node on the replayed running-average lines (new `policy/record` read route — no snapshot
  collection; labeled "since first recorded law"), with click-to-pin detail cards showing each law's
  exact pull on the average, and the current administration's era shaded when its start is known
  (era history accrues from here on; no fabricated back-history).

- **Politicians page rebuilt as a ranked leaderboard** (`update/country-pages-ui`, Phase 3). The
  card grid becomes a dense ladder list with proper column headers
  (`# / Name / Party / Office / Influence / Favorability`): every row carries an NPI bar scaled to
  the current result set's #1 (gold leader, neutral rest — tested `ladderWidthPct` helper), Office is
  its own column (CN's "President of the PRC" / "NPC Delegate" fit untruncated; below `lg` Party +
  Office fold into the location subline and the bar slides under the name — one row component,
  responsive variants), and players carry a gold PLAYER badge. The four wrapping filter-chip rows
  collapse into one sticky toolbar (Party / Office / Sort dropdowns + a gold Players-only switch),
  killing the page's hardcoded `blue-500/red-500/purple-500` party and toggle classes — party colors
  now come exclusively from party documents via PartyChip. The hero is gone per the
  operational-page density rule; sorting by Campaign Funds swaps the Favorability column for a
  Funds column so the sort stays legible. All filter/sort/search semantics unchanged.

- **Executive pages converge on one config-driven shell** (`update/country-pages-ui`, Phase 2). The
  six hand-rolled executive surfaces (US White House + the UK/DE/JP/IE/CN hubs) now share an
  identity masthead (photo hero fading into the country's brand-gradient band — `institutionIdentity`
  extends the treasury grammar with per-surface glyphs: ★ White House, 政 国务院, HM Downing Street,
  閣 首相官邸…), an **instrument strip** (Term/Plenum Clock · Mandate approval · The Desk/Directives ·
  Cabinet/State Council fill — all naming via a new per-country `executiveSurface` config, including a
  tested "TERM n OF x" badge that renders once a term source exists), and an **Acts of Government
  ledger**: a read-side aggregation route merging signed/vetoed/on-desk bills, national executive
  orders, and cabinet nominations/confirmations into a filterable, date/turn-stamped chronicle with
  per-country chip labels (SIGNED/EX. ORDER ↔ ENACTED/DIRECTIVE). US and CN additionally get the
  **office plaques** treatment (constitutional order, seal chips, dashed vacancies carrying each
  office's selection rule). All existing functionality is retained — White House Address/Orders/
  Endorsements/Admin tabs, sign/veto desk, appointment modals, parliamentary no-confidence and
  formation panels, CN confidence/opposition/NPC composition, Regime Health. No new writes anywhere.

- **Country lander: National Ideology band, grouped Explore directory, and hero registration pill**
  (`update/country-pages-ui`, Phase 1 of the country-pages modernization). The lander
  (`/country/[code]`) now opens with a National Ideology band showing the country's **Economic Axis**
  and **Social Axis** — the equal-weight average of every implemented national law's position on the
  −5…+5 scale, computed by a new tested SSOT helper (`computeNationalAxes`) over the same records the
  National Policy page lists (explicit-zero positions count; laws without a position on an axis are
  excluded from that axis only; tariffs/subsidies never contribute). Each axis renders as a spectrum
  bar (bucket label + exact score, marker at the average) with a live drift sparkline replayed
  retroactively from enacted-law history (`axesTimeline` — no snapshot collection; labeled "since
  first recorded law"), plus a "Recently moved the needle" feed of the last five axis-carrying laws
  with their enactment stamp, axis chips, and the actual pull each had on the running average. A
  dashed **Economic Model** field ("Not yet determined" · PLANNED) reserves the slot for the future
  feature. The icon nav-card grid is replaced by a grouped directory (Politics / Government / Country
  & Economy) whose rows carry live figures (active parties, politicians, live elections, bills before
  the legislature, national law count, region count, prime rate) from a new batched
  `overview-counts` route — every figure degrades per-row to a plain chevron. Registration moved out
  of the stats strip into a glowing status pill over the hero photo. All config-driven — CN renders
  Premier/Head of State tiles, its own legislature/bank names, and its own domain data with no
  country branches.
- **Player random events — catalog expanded from 5 to 13 templates.** Eight new code-first PREE
  handlers with seed definitions: _Tax Audit_ and _Old Friend's Venture_ (all characters), _Memoir
  Offer_ and _Town Hall Ambush_ (politicians), _Polarizing Endorsement_ and _Hot Mic_ (active
  candidates), _Product Defect_ and _Insider Tip_ (CEOs). Each offers four options (including an
  inaction default) over the shared d100 roll with three outcome tiers. Rewards and penalties are
  grounded in existing system scales — personal wealth ±$25K–$500K (vs. ~$3.2M starting wealth for a
  middle-wealth character), favorability ±1–12 on the 0–100 scale, infamy +1–8, campaign support
  ±1–5, and corp sentiment pulses ±1–10. A new seed-catalog parity test asserts every definition's
  option ids, default option, and eligibility tags match its registered handler so copy/code drift
  can't ship.
- **Actions page — "Latest event" outcome card.** After a random event resolves (or times out), the
  event card no longer vanishes: for 48 hours it renders a compact outcome card showing the event,
  the choice made (or the auto-applied default on timeout), the outcome tier, and the applied
  effects. `GET /api/events/active` now returns `lastResolved` alongside `event`; outcome tables
  still never leave the server (only the applied tier's effects are exposed).
- **Admin guardrails for the events feature gate.** Enabling random events while zero templates are
  approved now returns an explicit warning (surfaced on both the Turn Controls toggle and the
  Random Events panel, which also shows a persistent banner in that state) instead of silently never
  firing. The approve route now validates that a definition's option ids and default option match
  the registered code-first handler before approving.

### Fixed

- **Approval scoring inverted sign for negative-average metrics.** `computeStateApprovalBase` /
  `computeApprovalBaseFromAverages` divided the state-vs-national deviation by the _signed_
  national average, so any metric whose national average is negative (now `budgetBalance`, and
  `socialMediaSentiment`) scored backwards — a worse deficit _raised_ approval. The denominator
  now uses `|avg|`, so the deviation's sign tracks `value − avg` regardless of the average's
  sign. No-op for positive-average metrics. Surfaced by the budget-sync P6d gate.
- **Cabinet overview and individual office pages disagreed on who held a seat.** The cabinet
  overview reads the country-scoped `ukCabinetMembers` collection while each minister's office page
  reads the unified `cabinetMembers` collection; appoint/fire dual-write both, but
  `clearCabinetOnTransition` (run on a government/PM change) cleared only **one** per branch — the
  UK branch wiped `ukCabinetMembers` only, the JP/DE/IE/CN branch wiped `cabinetMembers` only. A
  new Taoiseach therefore left Ireland's seats reading filled on the overview but vacant in the
  office (and a new UK PM did the reverse). The transition now clears **both** collections for every
  parliamentary country and unions the resign-notification recipients across both. A read-only heal
  script (`scripts/debug/heal-cab-office-desync.mjs`, dry-run by default) reconciles existing live
  rows by rebuilding `ukCabinetMembers` to match the unified collection. +tests.

- **Ex-ministers kept drawing the cabinet office-action bonus after a government fell.**
  `clearCabinetOnTransition` deleted the cabinet member rows but never reset the holders'
  `character.currentOffice`, so a minister whose government collapsed (or who was replaced wholesale
  on a PM/President change) kept `currentOffice` pointed at the cabinet seat and kept regenerating
  the cabinet AP bonus until reassigned. The transition now restores each cleared holder's
  `currentOffice` to their legislative seat (from `electedOfficials`) or unsets it — mirroring the
  PM-facing fire flow — for both presidential (US) and parliamentary cabinets. Guarded so a holder
  whose office already moved on is left untouched. +tests.

- **Cabinet appointment cooldown is now per-seat and keyed to appointment, not firing.** Previously
  firing a minister put the seat on a 12-turn re-appointment cooldown. Now a Prime Minister can fire
  a minister at any time with no penalty, but each cabinet seat can only be (re)appointed **once
  every 24 turns** — the lock is set when a minister is appointed and persists for the full window
  even if that minister is later fired. +tests.

- **Cabinet ministers lost their office-bonus AP (Bug #0758).** Cabinet appointment overwrites
  `character.currentOffice` with a cabinet office key (`parliamentaryCabinet` / `ukCabinet` /
  `usCabinet`), and the live `gameConfig.officeActionBonus` had no entry for those keys — so per-turn
  action regen resolved the office bonus to `0`, erasing the legislative-seat bonus (e.g. a German
  minister's Bundestag +1). Office AP now resolves through a shared `resolveOfficeActionBonus`
  helper that **stacks** the cabinet bonus on top of the holder's legislative seat (recovered from
  `electedOfficials`, since `currentOffice` is overwritten) — mirroring how the central-bank chair
  bonus stacks. Cabinet membership is read from the unified `cabinetMembers` collection (the SSOT
  across US/UK/parliamentary families). The same resolver now drives all four surfaces — turn regen,
  the StatusBar, and the profile/character pages — fixing a pre-existing drift where the StatusBar
  read the seat from `electedOfficials` while regen and the pages read `currentOffice`. Seed and live
  config gain `usCabinet`/`parliamentaryCabinet`/`ukCabinet` (= 1). Result: DE/UK ministers get
  seat + cabinet (e.g. 2), US cabinet (no seat) gets 1, backbenchers unchanged at 1. +17 tests.
  The Political Standing Actions tooltip now also lists each contributing source on its own labeled
  line (Base / Office (seat) / Cabinet (position) / Central Bank Chair / Party influence) between the
  summary and the "1 turn = …" line, via a tested `getActionBreakdown` helper + new `actionBreakdown`
  prop. +10 tests (helper + RTL).

- **Stock-market exchange picker on mobile clipped its last exchange (SSE).** The mobile exchange
  dropdown is absolutely positioned inside the stock-market hero `<header>`, which is
  `overflow-hidden` to round the hero image — so a dropdown tall enough for every exchange had its
  last entry (Shanghai's SSE, the last active exchange in `COUNTRY_ORDER`) clipped at the card's
  bottom edge. The dropdown now renders through a `document.body` portal (matching `InfoTooltip`),
  anchored to the trigger with fixed positioning, flipping above when there isn't room below and
  capping its height to the viewport so it scrolls instead of clipping. Outside-click/Escape close
  now checks both the trigger and the portaled menu. Desktop pill row unchanged. Added a happy-dom
  RTL test asserting all exchanges (incl. SSE) list and that the menu escapes an `overflow-hidden`
  ancestor.
- **National party Treasurers couldn't see the PS Investment, GOTV, or Suppression controls.** The
  Treasury tab's budget gate (`canManageBudgets`) was chair/VC/admin only, while the
  `/ps-investment`, `/gotv`, and `/suppression` API routes all authorize the treasurer — so the role
  allowed to set those budgets couldn't see the controls or the PS "Total this turn" readout. The
  gate now includes the treasurer (the four treasury permission flags are extracted into a tested
  `resolveTreasuryPermissions` helper); state-party pages already showed the block to treasurers.
  Unchanged: the tax slider stays read-only for treasurers and the Treasury Plan stays
  treasurer+admin-editable.
- **Re-seeding PREE drafts demoted approved templates back to draft.** `seedPreeEventDefinitions`
  `$set` the full seed row — including `status: "draft"` — over existing rows, so clicking "Seed
  PREE drafts" after approving silently removed every template from the offer pool (events stopped
  firing with no error). Seeding now updates display copy only; `status`/`version` are set on insert
  alone, preserving approval and retirement.
- **Players beyond the first 200 characters never received random events.** The PREE turn driver
  loaded characters with a hard `.limit(200)` and no sort, so the same first-inserted 200 characters
  were considered every turn and everyone else was permanently excluded. The driver now streams the
  full player-character collection in 200-character chunks.
- **Timeout resolutions from the turn-phase sweep never notified the player.** The turn driver built
  its owner-lookup map _after_ sweeping, so `player_event_resolved` notifications for expired events
  only went out when the 15-minute cron happened to do the sweep instead. Both paths now share one
  sweep helper that loads instance owners before resolving.
- **Corrupt-donor "return the money" low tier silently did nothing extra.** Its
  `investigationFlag` custom effect had no consumer; replaced with a concrete +4 infamy so the tier
  matches its "investigation opened" label.
- **Disabling random events wrote `null` audit fields.** The toggle now `$unset`s
  `playerRandomEventsEnabledBy/At` instead of `$set`ting `undefined`.

- **Regional executive offices created after the initial world seed had their Office AP frozen at 0.**
  `governorOfficeState` rows — the spendable Office AP balance (`gubernatorialActions`) behind the
  Governor's / Minister-President's Office — were only ever created by `seedOfficeStates`, which runs at
  world bootstrap and admin reset. Ireland was built out _after_ that one-time seed ran, so its eight
  regional governor seats (DUB, KIL, MID, WEX, LIM, COR, GAL, DON) never received a row: the office page
  read `gubernatorialActions ?? 0` and the per-turn `processGovernorAPRegen` only `$inc`s _existing_ rows,
  so AP sat at 0 and never regenerated — and the seats could not act at all, since the spend paths require
  an existing row. Wired the idempotent `seedOfficeStates` into the turn loop as a new `officeStateSeed`
  phase that runs each turn immediately before `governorAPRegen` (and before the governor-office reader
  phases), so any missing regional-executive / national row is created at the action cap and is
  regen-eligible the same turn. Self-heals the existing Ireland gap on the next turn after deploy and
  future-proofs any region or country added after bootstrap; in steady state (all rows present) it performs
  reads only, no writes. Added a turn-registry ordering test.

- **The bill vote module now shows the real pass threshold — including filibusters and supermajorities.**
  The seating-chart hero hard-coded a simple majority (`Math.floor(seats / 2) + 1`) and rendered
  "Ayes · need 51 to pass", which never reflected an invoked filibuster (3/5 cloture) or a two-thirds
  supermajority (nationalization / privatization, JP Shūgiin override) — so a filibustered bill
  confusingly showed both "/51" and a separate "60% needed to pass" line. Extracted a tested
  `seatingThreshold(eligible, requiredPct)` helper, drove `VoteSeatingChart` from it via a new optional
  `requiredPct` prop, and replaced the seat-count caption with a single "{pct}% needed to pass" line
  (51% simple majority, 60% filibustered Senate, 67% supermajority). The national bill page passes a
  `heroRequiredPct` keyed to the chamber on the floor and drops the now-redundant standalone threshold
  line. Added a red "⚑ Filibustered" badge in the page header and relocated the filibuster attribution
  into a prominent red banner at the top of the floor-vote module. Display only — no change to
  pass-rule logic.

- **Washington, D.C. was generating phantom House / Senate / Governor / state-legislature elections.**
  D.C. is excluded from every US seat constant (`HOUSE_SEATS`, `SENATE_CLASSES_BY_STATE`,
  `STATE_SENATE_SEATS`) — it elects no congressional or gubernatorial seats and only casts 3
  presidential electoral votes — but it lives in the `states` collection for economy / demographics /
  presidential purposes. The per-turn `ensurePerpetualElections` and the bootstrap + admin
  officials/elections seeders selected US regions by country alone, then fabricated seats via fallback
  defaults (`SENATE_CLASSES[id] || [1, 2]`, `houseSeats[id] || 1`, unconditional governor / stateSenate),
  spawning a full slate of D.C. races every cycle (plus matching empty D.C. `statePartyOrg` /
  `statePartyElections`). Added a single preset-independent allow-list, `isUsElectoralState()` (the 50
  `HOUSE_SEATS` states), and applied it at every generator: `ensurePerpetualElections`,
  `initializeOfficials`, the `officials/init` and `country/[code]/elections` admin routes,
  `generateStatePartyOrg`, plus a defense-in-depth guard in `ensureStatePartyOrgRow`. The admin routes'
  pre-existing `find({})` (all-country) leak is closed by the same filter. Live data was healed (10
  elections, 4 automated NPP candidacies, 4 `statePartyOrg`, 48 `statePartyElections` removed); the D.C.
  region doc, metrics, and voter-registration pool are retained.
- **NPPs could be slated into a race whose primary had already closed, dropping them straight into the
  general election.** Both the slate-assignment API (`POST .../slate/[electionId]/invitations`) and the
  per-turn slate filer (`fileAcceptedSlateRows`) gated only on `election.status`, which stays `"active"`
  all the way through the general phase — so a candidate recruited after the primary closed was filed
  into the general, bypassing the primary entirely (observed: NPP "Matthew McDonald" filed into the AZ
  U.S. House general ~27 hours after that primary ended). Both paths now also check the primary boundary
  via a shared turn-first `isPrimaryClosed` helper (mirrors the player `enter` route): the API returns a
  409 ("the primary has ended for this race") and the turn filer skips the row while preserving the
  assignment for template carryover. The incumbent-defense and generic party-pool passes were already
  safe — they only ever see still-open primaries.
- **Presidential coattails were wildly overpowered and keyed off the wrong signal.** They were a
  persuasion-driver component worth up to ±0.25 of the swing/peeling budget, sourced from the live
  presidential _race tally_ margin per state. They are now an approval-driven nominal-share multiplier
  using the **same** `COATTAIL_MAX_BONUS` (±9%) / `COATTAIL_APPROVAL_SATURATION` (±25 pts) the governor
  coattail uses, sourced from the **sitting President's** party and stored national approval
  (`governmentApprovals.approvalRating`) and applied nationwide to down-ballot generals (excluding the
  presidential race itself). New `presidentialCoattail.ts` mirrors `govCoattail.ts`; the shared
  magnitude math is extracted to `coattailMagnitude.ts`. The old presidential-margin persuasion driver
  and `presidentialMargin.ts` are removed (persuasion budgets now sum to 0.75). The election persuasion
  card shows a "Presidential Coattails" `%`-tilt row parallel to the gubernatorial one.
- **Release-readiness audit (security).** Campaign-strength detail endpoint
  (`GET /api/campaigns/[id]/campaign-strength/detail`) now requires login — it exposed contributor
  identities and spend totals publicly. Corporations list no longer drops the enabled-country scope when
  filtering by `?stateId=`, and the `?search=` term is regex-escaped (ReDoS / filter bypass).
  Admin cabinet-nomination force-confirm and the acting-appointment removal are now scoped by
  `countryId` so same-named positions in different countries cannot collide. `officials/[id]/resign`
  validates the ObjectId and only counts **active** candidacies when blocking legislative resignation.
  Corporation vote routes 400 (not 500) on malformed vote IDs. `map/overview` validates `countryId`
  against `COUNTRY_CONFIGS`. Admin law-type chamber enum re-synced with `LegislationTypePosition`
  (removed `bundesrat`/`joint`/`chamber`, added `landtag`/`peoplesCongress`).
- **Release-readiness audit (performance).** Post-election partial-25% `characterStateOrg` reset is now
  a single pipeline `updateMany` instead of fetch-all + per-row bulk writes. New seeded indexes: the
  campaign-strength contribution aggregation (`activityLog` by `actionType` + `details.campaignId`) and
  the index-fund collections (`indexFunds.slug` unique, positions by fund/holder, transactions,
  redemption queue, NAV snapshots) — all previously full collection scans.
- **Typecheck was red on `development`.** Fixed an unescaped backtick that truncated the
  election-mechanics wiki seed template, the missing `corporationLogoUrl` on unowned sector rows,
  contingent-panel resolution-mode narrowing in `GeneralElectionPanel`, untyped aggregation results in
  the campaign-strength detail route, and an incomplete `Character` fixture in
  `resignExecutiveOffice.test.ts`. Removed stray root-level scratch query scripts (`audit-*.js`,
  `market-audit*.js`, `query-*.js`) that failed lint. Campaign-strength route tests now mock
  `electionCandidates` for the new active-candidacy management guard.

- **Index fund redeem could pay out without debiting units (double-redeem race).** `debitFundPosition`
  now returns `{ ok, position }` and the redeem route aborts with 400 when the guarded debit matches no
  position (e.g. a concurrent redemption drained it) instead of paying out anyway.
  `src/lib/indexFunds/fundQueries.ts`, `src/app/api/investment-funds/[slug]/redeem/route.ts`.
- **Corporation dissolution dropped index-fund shareholders from in-kind equity distribution.** Funds on
  a dissolving corp's cap table now receive their pro-rata slice of cross-held equity (cap-table credit
  via `creditSharesToFund` plus a mirrored entry in the fund's holdings ledger; non-constituent windfalls
  are liquidated by the next rebalance sweep). `src/lib/corporations/distributeCrossEquityInKind.ts`,
  `src/lib/corporations/inKindEquityDistribution.ts`, `src/lib/indexFunds/fundQueries.ts`.
- **Queued-redemption payouts in the fund cron now use a guarded debit** (`cashAnchor`/`unitSupply`
  `$gte` filters) so racing subscriptions/redemptions can no longer drive fund cash negative.
  `src/lib/indexFunds/fundCron.ts`.
- **Backing-ratio auto-pause was dead code** — the ratio was computed against the freshly derived NAV,
  making it identically 1. It now compares current backing to the previous cycle's quoted liability so a
  sharp backing collapse actually pauses the fund. `src/lib/indexFunds/fundCron.ts`.
- **Fund holders endpoint now requires login.** `GET /api/investment-funds/[slug]/holders` exposes
  per-player positions and queued redemptions; it is now gated by `requireAuth` (the aggregate fund
  list and transactions endpoints remain public). `src/app/api/investment-funds/[slug]/holders/route.ts`.
- **Index fund type errors masked by `ignoreBuildErrors`.** `fundFloatAbsorptionPlan.ts` imported the
  loose `CurrencyCode` from `db/types` instead of the strict union; `fundHoldingsValuation.ts`'s corp row
  type omitted `totalShares`/`publicFloat` required by `resolveShareExecutionPrice`. Type-only; no
  runtime change.

### Added

- **Index funds.** 10 investment funds tracking baskets of corporations by sector/market index (US25, US
  Tech, US Financials, US Energy, UK FTSE 100, Japan Nikkei 225, Germany DAX 40, China SSE 50, Global
  Financials, Global Energy). Each fund absorbs public float from constituent stocks per-turn at
  `Math.floor(totalShares / 100)` cap, rebalances positions, and issues/redeems shares at NAV. Players
  subscribe/redeem via stock market UI. NPP AI also invests via archetype-specific allocation. Engine gated
  by `indexFundsEnabled` feature flag. `src/lib/indexFunds/fundCron.ts`,
  `src/lib/indexFunds/publicFloatAbsorption.ts`, `src/lib/indexFunds/nppInvesting.ts`.

- **Index fund audit logging.** Subscribe, redeem, queued-redemption payouts, and dividend pass-through
  now emit `index_fund_subscribe`, `index_fund_redeem`, and `index_fund_dividend` rows in `financialTxLog`
  (admin Financial Ledger / tx history), with fund slug, ticker, units, NAV, and source metadata. Player
  subscribe/redeem also write `game_action` activity-log rows (`index_fund_subscribe` /
  `index_fund_redeem`) for the admin Activity Log tab. `src/lib/indexFunds/fundTxLog.ts`,
  `src/app/api/investment-funds/[slug]/subscribe/route.ts`,
  `src/app/api/investment-funds/[slug]/redeem/route.ts`, `src/lib/indexFunds/fundCron.ts`,
  `src/lib/indexFunds/dividendPassThrough.ts`.

- **US contingent presidential election.** When no candidate reaches 270 electoral votes (269–269 tie or
  fractured field), resolution now runs a 12th-Amendment contingent ballot: the incoming House elects the
  President from the top three EV finishers (one vote per state delegation; DC excluded; 26 states
  needed) and the Senate elects the Vice President from the top two running mates (51 votes needed).
  Chamber members vote by party match then ideology proximity; tied delegations abstain. Election detail,
  wiki, and Discord result embeds show delegation/senate breakdowns. House/Senate generals resolve before
  president so the contingent ballot uses the incoming Congress. `src/lib/turn/election/contingentElection.ts`,
  `src/lib/turn/election/loadContingentElectionData.ts`, `src/lib/turn/election/presidentResolution.ts`,
  `src/app/elections/[id]/components/ContingentElectionPanel.tsx`.

- **VP resignation from the White House.** Seated Vice Presidents can resign from `/whitehouse` without
  withdrawing from other active candidacies (e.g. a concurrent presidential race). Executive resign vacates
  the singleton VP slot, clears `currentOffice`, and records a `resigned` career event instead of deleting
  the `electedOfficials` row. `src/app/whitehouse/WhiteHouseClient.tsx`,
  `src/app/api/officials/[id]/resign/route.ts`, `src/lib/elections/resignExecutiveOffice.ts`.

- **Presidential suspend & endorse (general election).** Nominees can suspend active campaigning from their
  own `/campaign/[id]` page during the general phase, stay on the ballot, and endorse another active
  nominee. Campaign turn income, upgrades, rallies, travel, canvassing, contributions, and Support
  drips stop; each EV turn transfers 25% of the suspender's campaign strength and 25% of their per-state
  character org investment (`characterStateOrg`, not `statePartyOrg`) to the endorsed ticket in
  `accumulatePresidentVoteTurn`. Suspended nominees forfeit ground-game / VP-home-state EV bonuses;
  endorsed-withdrawal clears transfers via `endorsementTargetWithdrawnAt`. Election table + wiki
  documented. `campaignSuspended` / `endorsedElectionCandidateId` on `electionCandidates`;
  `POST /api/campaigns/[id]/suspend-endorse`; `src/lib/campaigns/suspendEndorseTransfers.ts`,
  `src/lib/campaigns/suspendEndorseLifecycle.ts`, `src/app/campaign/[id]/components/SuspendEndorsePanel.tsx`.

### Changed

- **Metric-engine core (P0 of the metrics overhaul).** The standalone `gdpGrowth` turn phase
  (`updateGdpGrowth`) was ported into a new unified metric engine (`src/lib/metricEngine/`): a declarative
  node registry + topo-sort evaluator + `simBaseline`/`policyDelta` coexistence + per-node bounds &
  circuit-breakers, fed by an external-aggregate provider (FX-normalized sector revenue + tax) and pre-sorted
  once per turn. `economic.gdpGrowth` + `economic.unemploymentRate` are the first two registry nodes; the
  old phase is deleted and the registry phase (`metricEngine`) occupies its slot. **Behavior-preserving** —
  gated by golden-master parity fixtures (incl. the `MAX_POLICY_DELTA` cap, NaN-prev rejection, FX
  normalization, and the legacy `growthRate` fallback). Also adds the spending→metric channel and a proven
  (not-yet-live) `povertyRate` reference node. Foundation for the dynamic GDP/population engine (P1).
  `src/lib/metricEngine/*`, `src/lib/turn/gdpGrowth.ts` (now pure helpers + constants),
  `src/simulation/phases/turnPhaseRegistry.ts`.

- **Demographic legislation wired to the cohort engine (P1b-4 of the metrics overhaul).** Re-pointed or
  removed 14 legislation effects that directly targeted now-derived population readouts
  (`populationGrowth`/`medianAge`/`demographicDecline`) and double-counted the cohort engine's emergent
  output (§4.7 sweep). Each affected law now targets its upstream DRIVER instead — pension / social-security /
  retirement-age acts move `laborParticipation`; immigration / asylum / work-visa / foreign-worker / skilled-
  talent acts move `migrationRate`; family / childcare / reproductive / exam-burden acts move `birthRate`;
  elder-care acts move `lifeExpectancy`; rural-development / hukou acts move `urbanizationRate` — across
  US / UK / DE / IE / JP / CN. Effect signs were verified against the policy-effect engine (e.g. expanding a
  pension lowers `laborParticipation`; childcare raises it; pro-choice lowers `birthRate`). Also
  de-double-counted the approval channel: `populationGrowth` and `medianAge` no longer carry a direct
  approval term (engine-derived stocks whose consequences already reach approval via the economic metrics;
  this also neutralizes secular-aging approval drift), while the policy lever `migrationRate` keeps its term.
  Realized net migration is surfaced as a new `population.realizedMigrationRate` readout written alongside —
  never onto — the policy input (§8.2). A corpus guard test asserts no law targets the derived readout trio.
  `src/lib/seeds/**/legislationTypes.ts`, `src/lib/utils/{governmentApproval,metricScoring}.ts`,
  `src/lib/demographics/phase.ts`.

- **Three new demographic acts (P1b-4).** US **Paid Family Leave & Child Care Act** and UK **Childcare &
  Family Support Act** (both move `birthRate` + `laborParticipation` — childcare lets parents work), and a
  JP **Pension & Retirement Age Act** (retirement-age lever → `laborParticipation`). Each is a 7-option
  left↔right act, registered in the 2020 and 1991 baselines. `src/lib/seeds/reference/legislationTypes.ts`,
  `src/lib/seeds/jp/jpLegislationTypes.ts`, `src/lib/seeds/reference/basePolicies{,1991}.ts`.

- **GDP engine — potential + cyclical (P1c of the metrics overhaul).** `gdpGrowth` is no longer just the
  revenue-weighted sector growth; it is now **`potential + cyclical`**. **Potential growth** (Solow
  `αL·g_L + αK·g_K + TFP`, 0.66/0.34) is driven by the **civilian labor force** (the P1b cohort
  `workingAgePopulation` minus conscripts, × `laborParticipation`) and a new per-region **Solow capital
  stock** (`state.capitalStock`, seeded ≈3×GDP, advanced by investment-from-the-prime-rate minus
  depreciation, converging to a steady-state `K/Y≈3`). The preserved sector engine becomes the **cyclical
  signal** (`economic.sectorGrowth`, keeping its EMA + tax/legislation coexistence); its deviation from
  potential feeds a persisted **output gap** (`state.outputGap`) that closes over time, so `gdpGrowth`
  reverts toward potential — a shrinking/aging workforce drags the trend down, and a sector boom shows up as
  a closing gap rather than permanent excess growth. **Unemployment (Okun)** now keys off the real output
  gap (`gdpGrowth − potential`) instead of a fixed 2%. The **C+I+G+NX demand block was deliberately not
  built** — the supply + sector-cyclical model is sufficient (criterion #4). New surfaced read-only
  diagnostics `economic.{potentialGrowth, sectorGrowth, laborForce}` + `state.{capitalStock, outputGap}`
  (all excluded from approval). The P0 gdpGrowth golden-master parity is **preserved on `sectorGrowth`**, and
  `gdpGrowth` equals the sector signal at cold-start (no jump on deploy). Full TFP basket
  (R&D/education/infra/agglomeration) uses a calibrated baseline for now.
  `src/lib/metricEngine/{capitalStock,potentialGrowth,outputGap,phase}.ts`,
  `src/lib/metricEngine/registry/economic.ts`.

- **Sovereign default reads the smoothed national GDP (P1d-0, §6.1 guardrail).** `applySovereignDebtAdjustment`
  now computes debt-to-GDP off the EMA-smoothed national GDP (`federalBudget.gdpSmoothed`), like the treasury
  and annual-debt paths already did — so now that `state.gdp` moves every turn (P1c), a one-year GDP swing
  can't spuriously trip a sovereign-default threshold. Falls back to raw GDP for cutover safety.
  `src/lib/bonds/sovereign.ts`.

- **Census & reapportionment (P1d of the metrics overhaul).** US House seats + presidential electoral votes
  are now sourced from live `state.houseDistricts` (previously frozen module constants read by ~10 election
  consumers) through a golden-gated apportionment provider — byte-identical to the preset seed until a census
  fires (so the cutover is behavior-preserving). A new **decennial census** turn phase fires in Week 1 of
  years ending in 0 — preset-aware via `gameState.currentYear` (2020/2030 for the 2019 start; 2000/2010 for
  1991), neither start year triggers at bootstrap — recomputes House apportionment from current state
  populations by the **method of equal proportions** (Huntington-Hill, 435 seats), writes the new
  `houseDistricts` so EC + House outcomes follow, stamps `gameState.lastCensusYear`, and posts a National Wire
  census event with the per-state seat deltas. Population drift (P1b/P1c) now feeds reapportionment over a
  long game. `src/lib/elections/apportionment.ts`, `src/lib/turn/census.ts`, election-engine consumers.

- **GDP growth clamped at write time.** `gdpGrowth` is now clamped to its declared `[-10, 15]` range when
  the engine writes it, instead of being written unclamped and corrected a turn later by the policy phase.
  Closes a transient single-turn overshoot above the cap (the runaway the bound was added to prevent, bug
  \#0571). No effect on in-range values.

- **Presidential campaign strength rebalanced.** Vote multiplier soft cap reduced from +300% to +100%
  (`CAMPAIGN_STRENGTH_MAX_BONUS = 1`, `TAU = 50_000`); each campaign turn pulls the strict CS leader back
  toward the race average by up to 175 strength so late-race runaways cannot permanently lock an old lead.
  Wiki election-mechanics page updated. `src/lib/campaigns/campaignStrength.ts`, `src/lib/turn/campaignTurn.ts`,
  `src/lib/seeds/wiki/content/electionMechanics.ts`.

- **Commodity supply/demand rebalance (critical shortages).** Addresses structural S/D mismatch where
  extraction anchor revenue (~$70M) could not cover demand from $4.5B anchors across 1,427 sectors. Demand
  cuts on top consumers: manufacturing iron 0.15→0.10, coal 0.10→0.06, energy 0.20→0.15, natural_gas
  0.08→0.05, timber 0.05→0.03, plastics 0.08→0.06; energy natural_gas 0.12→0.08; chemical oil 0.15→0.10;
  logistics energy 0.20→0.15; construction timber 0.08→0.06; automobiles iron 0.10→0.08, plastics
  0.12→0.10. Supply boosts: broad-mix extraction iron 0.25→0.40, coal 0.22→0.30, natural_gas 0.14→0.24,
  timber 0.12→0.20 (rare_earth/copper trimmed 0.07→0.03 to partially offset); chemical_industries plastics
  co-production 0.15→0.25. `MARKETING_ADVERTISING_DEMAND_RATE` 0.60→0.40 (33% cut on corp marketing →
  advertising demand). Target: move iron, natural_gas, energy, coal, oil, plastics, advertising, and timber
  from CRITICAL (~0.13–0.39 S/D) toward SHORT (~0.40–0.55). `src/lib/constants/commodities.ts`.

### Fixed

- **Approval was scoring 24 lower-is-better metrics INVERTED (P6a audit catch — systemic).** The
  hand-maintained IS_HIGHER_BETTER map had silently omitted ~63 metrics, and approval's `?? true`
  fallback then treated childPoverty, knifeCrimeRate, antiSocialBehaviourRate, floodRisk,
  nhsWaitingTime, housingAffordability, roughSleeping, independenceDesire, matchingFriction and 15
  more as higher-is-better — approval literally rewarded child poverty and knife crime wherever those
  metrics were stored. The metricScoring map is now DERIVED from the metricDefinitions SSOT (omission
  impossible), with a guard test pinning every defined metric's direction. NOTE: this corrects live
  scoring — regions with poor values on the affected metrics will read lower than before (a
  correctness change, deliberately not gated to neutrality).
  **P6d-pre follow-up:** `governmentApproval.computeStateApprovalBase` (the actual approval roll-up
  feeding elections / OPS collapse / NPP behavior) kept a SECOND hand-map that the P6a fix missed —
  it was still scoring the same 23 lower-better metrics (childPoverty, nhsWaitingTime, knifeCrime,
  debtToGdp, housing pressure, …) inverted. That map is now re-exported from the metricScoring SSOT,
  so approval finally penalizes the bad outcomes it had been rewarding. The two maps agreed on all 57
  shared metrics, so consolidation only added the missing directions.

- **housingAffordability was three metrics in a trenchcoat (P3d unit repair, S1 class).** Defined as a
  price-to-income ratio bounded [2,20], but IE seeded index-scale values 18–32 (Dublin was
  CEILING-CLAMPED at 20 live), CN/DE seeded 18–55, and the uniform derivation produced index values with
  inverted polarity. Unified as a 0-100 housing cost PRESSURE index (lower = affordable): defs renamed,
  bounds [0,100], THRESHOLDS [15,70], uniform derivation aligned to the engine anchor; clamped live values
  recover toward baselines via policyEffects decay (no migration). Also caught: uk_housing_planning /
  uk_regional_housing_development / uk_leasehold_reform option ticks were SIGN-INVERTED ("build housing" /
  "protect leaseholders" options RAISED the lower-is-better metric — raw ticks bypass isHigherBetter, the
  AQI-family class); the housing laws now tick the supply root, leasehold flipped to inverted rates.

- **Green cabinet actions WORSENED air quality (P3c audit catch — the lifeExpectancy bug's sibling).**
  Eleven cabinet effect sites across US/CN/DE/JP were authored under a higher-is-better airQuality
  assumption, but it is an AQI (lower = cleaner): CN's Blue Sky Defense / Pollution Crackdown, DE's Clean
  Air Initiative, JP's Air Quality Intervention, and US preservation tiers all RAISED the AQI while
  extraction-style options "improved" it. All flipped; a new sign-coherence guard pins that any cabinet
  effect moving both airQuality and carbonEmissions moves them in the same direction. (Laws were immune —
  weighted contributions respect isHigherBetter; raw cabinet $incs bypass it.)

- **lifeExpectancy rewarded SMOG (P3c audit-class catch).** The P2b first pass treated airQuality as a
  higher-is-better quality index, but it is an AQI (THRESHOLDS best 8 / worst 80 — lower is better): the
  life-expectancy term had the sign inverted. Harmless while the input was a static seed read; fixed
  (with the anchor moved to the AQI midpoint 44) before P3c animates airQuality.

- **Cabinet order/setting modifiers were inert on real-unit metrics (P3b).** The 0.05/turn anti-stacking
  cap is absolute, so per-100k metrics (crimeRate span 9500) could never move — CN's social-credit crime
  order did nothing meaningful. Both the applied modifier and the cap now scale by the metric's THRESHOLDS
  span (the post-S1 realistic-range SSOT); 0-100-convention metrics are byte-identical, metrics without a
  THRESHOLDS entry keep scale 1 (bounds spans are clamp guards, not realistic ranges).

- **Emergency crime-crackdown region gate never worked (P3b audit).** Three breaks in the chain: the
  `regionMetricThreshold` constants compared per-100k crimeRate against 60 (the pre-S1 clamped scale —
  always true on real values); the panel looked the metric up by its bare id while the briefing keys are
  category-qualified (always undefined → zero eligible regions); and the region dropdown silently fell back
  to ALL regions when none were eligible, bypassing the gate entirely. Now: thresholds at 5500 per-100k,
  qualified `publicSafety.crimeRate` keys, and a strict eligible-only dropdown (US Federal Law Enforcement
  Surge ×2, UK and JP justice emergencies). Named: the order route does not re-validate region eligibility
  server-side (pre-existing).

- **Metric value-normalization uses realistic ranges, not safety bounds (S1 follow-up).** Two display/signal
  consumers reused metric `minValue`/`maxValue` (clamp guards) as value-normalization ranges: the corporate
  sector-margin system (`normalizedMetricQuality`) and the cabinet briefing bars (`cabinetMetrics`). With S1
  widening several bounds to headroom ceilings (e.g. `educationSpending` `[0, 10M]`), realistic values pinned
  to an extreme — education spend would have flipped from a margin boost to a drag, and its cabinet bar to
  ~0% fill. Both now normalize over each metric's `THRESHOLDS` `[worst, best]` (its actual realistic span),
  falling back to bounds when a metric has no threshold — so signals are responsive and correctly scaled.

- **Above-100 metrics no longer clamped to 100 (S1 bounds completion).** Every metric now declares explicit
  `minValue`/`maxValue` instead of silently defaulting to `[0,100]` in `processStateMetrics`. Three metrics
  seeded on their own scale above 100 — `educationSpending` (per-pupil, local currency; e.g. JP ¥950k),
  `preventableMortality` (per 100k), and `violentCrimeRate` (per 100k, up to ~2200) — were floored to 100
  every turn; they now hold their true values. Realistic bounds also added to `medianAge`, `physicianRate`,
  `policePerCapita`, `carbonEmissions`, `homelessnessRate`, `smallBusinessFormation` (within range today,
  at-risk once the engine writes derived values). A guard test asserts every metric carries explicit bounds
  so none can regress to the silent default. Completes the §S1 work begun with the negative-floor fixes below.

- **Negative-capable metrics no longer floored at 0 (S1 bounds audit).** `populationGrowth` (`[-3,5]`),
  `migrationRate` (`[-5,5]`), `socialMediaSentiment` (`[-100,100]`), and `budgetBalance` (`[-100,100]`)
  lacked an explicit negative `minValue`, so `processStateMetrics`' default `[0,100]` clamp silently floored
  every negative value at 0. Consequences fixed: restrictive-immigration laws (which push `migrationRate`
  negative) were no-ops; budget **deficits** (a fiscal-cost secondary on ~97 spending laws) and negative
  social-media sentiment could not be represented at all. `src/lib/constants/metricDefinitions.ts`.

- **Index fund NAV decline fixed.** `lastValueAnchor` (cost basis) was being overwritten with
  `totalShares * latestPrice` on every stock purchase instead of accumulating the cost of newly absorbed
  shares. This caused NAV to track market price rather than actual investment cost, producing perpetual
  decline. Now accumulates: `(h.lastValueAnchor ?? 0) + additionalShares * sharePriceAnchor`.
  `src/lib/indexFunds/fundCron.ts`.

- **Index fund public-float absorption 12× faster.** The per-hour absorption cap was
  `Math.floor(totalShares / 1200)` — too slow to deploy fund capital meaningfully. Changed to
  `Math.floor(totalShares / 100)`. A corp with 10M total shares now absorbs 100K shares/hour instead of
  8.3K. `src/lib/indexFunds/publicFloatAbsorption.ts`, `src/lib/indexFunds/publicFloatAbsorption.test.ts`.

- **Index fund subscribe/redeem hardened.** Fund subscribe/redeem are wrapped in the optional-transaction
  fallback and guarded against duplicate-key races. `src/lib/indexFunds/`.

- **Investment fund routes use slug URLs.** Fund detail, subscribe, and redeem APIs and UI migrated from
  legacy `/investment-funds/[id]` ObjectId routes to `/investment-funds/[slug]`; stale `[id]` routes
  removed. Portfolio fund links fall back to slug lookup when needed.
  `src/app/api/investment-funds/[slug]/`, `src/app/stockmarket/[country]/fund/[slug]/`.

- **Ministerial orders could stay active past their 24-turn duration (bug #0761).** Expiry relied on a single
  `{ expiresTurn: { $lte: currentTurn } }` query, which skipped legacy rows with missing or string-typed
  `expiresTurn` values — so orders like CN PBoC **Targeted Credit Easing** could show as active indefinitely.
  Added `expireMinisterialOrders` with a sweep pass, lazy expiry on cabinet briefing load, duplicate-active
  guard on issue, persisted `duration` on new orders, and turn-countdown labels in the cabinet UI.
  `src/lib/cabinet/ministerialOrderLifecycle.ts`, `src/lib/turn/ministerialOrderProcessing.ts`,
  `src/app/api/country/[code]/executive/cabinet/[positionId]/briefing/route.ts`,
  `src/app/api/country/[code]/executive/cabinet/[positionId]/order/route.ts`,
  `src/app/country/[code]/executive/cabinet/[positionId]/office/components/MinisterialOrderPanel.tsx`.

- **China executive endorsements blocked for same-party CCP candidates (bug #0737).** Office
  endorsements compared `character.party` and `candidate.party` with strict string equality.
  Characters store the party sequential id (`"1"`) while some candidate rows still carry legacy
  slugs (`"ccp"`, `"cn_ccp"`), so the Premier's CCP endorsement of another CCP member was
  rejected as cross-party. Added `buildPartyIdResolver` / `partiesMatchInCountry` in
  `src/lib/parties/partyMatch.ts` to canonicalize ids via abbreviation and country-prefixed
  slugs; applied to `createExecutiveEndorsement`, `createEndorsement`, the executive
  endorsements GET payload, and the governor office SSR endorsements list. Player candidates
  now match on current `character.party`, not entry-time `electionCandidates.party`. Disabled
  endorse buttons no longer always show the cross-party tooltip when blocked for other reasons.

- **Cabinet tier settings blocked after regional allocation changes.** Allocation and tier-setting
  cooldowns both wrote `lastChangedTurn`, so updating regional spend split could block tier
  changes for 24 turns (or vice versa). Allocations now track `lastAllocationChangedTurn`
  separately; tier settings keep `lastChangedTurn`. Legacy rows with allocation-only
  `lastChangedTurn` and no `tierSetting` are exempt from the 24-turn lock so ministers can set
  policy after the fix. `src/lib/db/types/cabinetSetting.ts`,
  `src/app/api/country/[code]/executive/cabinet/[positionId]/allocation/route.ts`,
  `src/app/api/country/[code]/executive/cabinet/[positionId]/setting/route.ts`.

- **New cabinet ministers inherited their predecessor's setting cooldown (bug #0754).** The
  `cabinetSettings` document is keyed by position (`{countryId}_{positionId}`), not by holder, and no
  appointment path cleared it — so a freshly appointed minister could be locked out of changing settings
  ("Settings can only be changed once per 24 turns") by the previous holder's recent change. Added
  `resetCabinetSettingCooldowns`, which clears `lastChangedTurn` / `lastAllocationChangedTurn` (preserving
  policy values for continuity) at every holder-seating point: parliamentary appointment, US Senate
  confirmation, US acting appointment, and admin force-confirm. `src/lib/db/collections/cabinetSettings.ts`,
  `src/lib/uk/cabinetApi.ts`, `src/lib/cabinetNominationLifecycle.ts`,
  `src/app/api/whitehouse/cabinet/acting/route.ts`,
  `src/app/api/admin/country/[code]/cabinet-nominations/route.ts`.

- **Politicians list showed US office labels for non-US characters.** `fetchPoliticians` called
  `getOfficeLabel` without `countryId`, so UK/Germany/China/etc. politicians defaulted to US
  titles (e.g. "President of the United States" for foreign heads of state). Pass each character's
  `countryId` into `getOfficeLabel`. `src/app/country/[code]/politicians/fetchPoliticians.ts`.

- **Central bank page overflow on narrow layouts.** Summary strip, chair card, and prime-rate card
  lacked `min-w-0` / `break-words`, causing horizontal overflow and clipped chair titles on mobile.
  `src/app/country/[code]/central-bank/CentralBankClient.tsx`,
  `src/app/country/[code]/central-bank/components/ChairCard.tsx`,
  `src/app/country/[code]/central-bank/components/PrimeRateCard.tsx`,
  `src/app/country/[code]/central-bank/components/CentralBankLoadingState.tsx`.

- **VP achievement not awarded on election win (bug #0767).** `checkOfficeHeldAchievements(...,
"vicePresident")` ran on admin appoint but not when `presidentResolution` seated the winning ticket's
  running mate (or a Senate contingent VP winner). VP winners now receive the **A Heartbeat Away**
  achievement and a win notification. `src/lib/turn/election/presidentResolution.ts`.

- **VP could not resign before a contingent House ballot (bug #0767).** `/api/officials/[id]/resign`
  blocked all resignations while the character had any active candidacy and deleted executive rows outright.
  Executive resign is now allowed during concurrent races; sitting presidents/VPs are excluded from
  contingent chamber voter pools until they resign so a House/Senate seat can cast a party-aligned ballot;
  seating a new House winner no longer clears the VP `electedOfficials` record when vacating prior offices.
  `src/lib/turn/election/loadContingentElectionData.ts`, `src/lib/turn/election/generalResolution.ts`.

- **Sovereign bond issuance skipped non-US/UK/JP countries.** Scheduled bond series only loaded budgets
  for US, UK, and JP. The cron now iterates every configured country budget document.
  `src/lib/bonds/sovereign.ts`.

## [0.3.1] - 2026-06-07

### Added

- **Founder supershares (dual-class share structure) — S#33.** Corporations can adopt a dual-class structure where the founder's shares each carry 2×–10× votes in shareholder votes, letting founders sell a much larger slice of the company for capital without losing governance control. Adoption paths: (1) opt-in at IPO — both the founding-IPO modal and the late go-public card gain a dual-class checkbox + multiplier slider; dual-class IPOs may float up to 75% (vs the standard 49%); (2) post-IPO via a new `adopt_supershares` shareholder vote proposed from the CEO Admin tab, which designates the CEO's current holdings as supershares at the chosen multiplier. Supershares are economically identical to common stock (dividends, dissolution payouts, buyouts unchanged) and never transfer — buyers always receive common stock, and a founder selling below their recorded supershare count erodes it (`min(superShares, shares)` is votable). All shareholder-vote tallying (cast weight, pass thresholds, auto-resolve) now runs on voting power instead of raw share counts, and corporate-control thresholds (subsidiary status, hostile-takeover eligibility) are voting-power based so an economic majority cannot bypass a supervoting founder. Privatization buyout votes intentionally remain one-share-one-vote to protect minority holders from supervoted squeeze-outs. `src/lib/corporations/superShares.ts`, `src/lib/corporations/votes/`, `src/lib/corporations/ipoIssuance.ts`, `src/lib/corporations/commands/capitalOperations/goPublic.ts`, `src/lib/corporations/corporateOwnership.ts`, `src/app/api/corporations/[id]/votes/`, `src/components/corporation/`.

### Fixed

- **German cabinet ministers lose Bundestag office bonus AP.** When a Bundestag member was appointed to the federal cabinet (finance minister, foreign minister, etc.), `currentOffice` was replaced with `{ type: "parliamentaryCabinet" }`, overwriting the `bundestag` office. Since `parliamentaryCabinet` was missing from all three bonus maps (`officeActionBonus` in gameConfig seed, `OFFICE_FUND_BONUS` in fundGeneration, and `OFFICE_NI_BONUS` in actionRefresh), the character received 0 bonus AP/turn, 0 bonus funds/hr, and 0 NI contribution from the office. Same issue affected UK cabinet ministers (`ukCabinet`) and other parliamentary-system cabinets (IE, JP). Added `parliamentaryCabinet: 1` and `ukCabinet: 1` to all three maps; added `bundestag`, `chancellor`, `ministerPresident`, and `landtag` entries to `OFFICE_FUND_BONUS` (previously only had US and UK offices). `src/lib/seeds/reference/gameConfig.ts`, `src/lib/utils/fundGeneration.ts`, `src/lib/turn/actionRefresh.ts`.

### Fixed

- **NPP corporation AI reworked for profitability.** Budgets now scale on revenue (not cash reserves), losing sectors are divested (except the corp's primary type), growth rate adjustments are more aggressive (+2/-2), dividends require 15%+ margin and cash above floor, expansion prioritizes the corp's own sector type first (primary → secondary → any), and expansion is deterministic (no random dice roll). `src/lib/turn/nppCorporationBehavior.ts`, `src/lib/turn/corporation/index.ts`.

- **Cross-country metric scoring distorted by local currency values.** `medianIncome` was stored in local currency per country but scored against USD-calibrated thresholds (best=90000, worst=15000), causing Japan to score 100 instead of 4.4, China 34.9 instead of 0, and the US to score 0. Same distortion affected profit margin calculations where China received a better consumer demand modifier than the US. Added `fxNormalize.ts` with base exchange rates, updated `scoreMetric()` and `normalizedMetricQuality()` to normalize `medianIncome` to USD before scoring/thresholding. `src/lib/utils/fxNormalize.ts`, `src/lib/utils/metricScoring.ts`, `src/lib/corporations/sectorMetricMarginProfiles.ts`.

- **NPP endorsement eligible-race detection broken by missing `countryId` projection.** The direct-action query that populates the NPP profile endorsement picker projected `_id`, `electionType`, `state`, and `senateClass` from the `elections` collection but omitted `countryId`. `nppCanPlausiblyEndorseElection` gates on `election.countryId !== npp.countryId`; with `countryId` undefined the comparison always failed for NPPs that have a `countryId`, so every race was rejected and the UI showed "You have no active eligible campaigns for this NPP to endorse." Added `countryId` to the projection type and field list. `src/lib/npps/queries/directAction.ts`.

- **NPP profile page font unreadable at small sizes.** `NppCapitalPanel.tsx` used hardcoded `text-[10px]`, `text-[11px]`, and `text-[12px]` throughout — below readable thresholds for many system fonts and display scalings. Replaced all hardcoded pixel sizes with Tailwind utilities: `text-xs` (12px) and `text-sm` (13px). `src/app/politicians/npp/[id]/NppCapitalPanel.tsx`.

- **Navbar stale after character creation.** After creating a character, `NavbarWrapper` rendered the no-character skeleton until a hard refresh because `router.refresh()` invalidates server caches but does not update `AuthDataContext`. Both creation entry points (`CreateCharacterPage` and `CharacterCreator`) now call `refetch(true)` from `useAuthMe` after the POST succeeds and before navigation, forcing the auth bootstrap to re-fetch `/api/client-nav` with `hasCharacter: true`. `src/app/create-character/page.tsx`, `src/app/create-character/components/CharacterCreator.tsx`.

- **NPP CEO profile links fixed.** NPP CEOs previously linked to `/character/${sequentialId}` which produced dead pages since NPPs are not in the `characters` collection. Now uses `/npp/${sequentialId}` matching the existing NPP profile route. `src/lib/corporations/queries/corporationDetail.ts`.

- **Duplicate NPP electedOfficials inflate vote tallies in Senate, House, and Cabinet nominations.** If a single NPP held multiple `electedOfficials` records for the same chamber (e.g. stale appointment + race-condition duplicates), the vote loop counted every duplicate as a separate voter, inflating vote counts past the chamber seat total. The fix de-duplicates by `nppId` before voting in `billVoting.ts`, `stateBillVoting.ts`, and `applyWhipVotes.ts`, and applies `seatsHeld` caps per unique NPP. A new regression test verifies duplicate officials produce exactly one vote. `src/lib/turn/npp/billVoting.ts`, `src/lib/turn/npp/stateBillVoting.ts`, `src/lib/congress/applyWhipVotes.ts`, `src/lib/cabinetNominationLifecycle.ts`, `src/lib/turn/npp/billVoting.test.ts`.

### Added

- **National Corporations & the state-ownership lifecycle.** Full nationalize → operate → privatize subsystem in `src/lib/nationalization/`. **Acquisition:** executive path (head-of-government; NPC/unowned + distressed player corps only; discounted/seizure tiers) and legislative path (any corp via a bill provision; fair-value; two-thirds supermajority in free legislatures). Every compensated taking pays a buyout premium above the bare valuation (`NATIONALIZATION_COMPENSATION_PREMIUM` = 5× the going-concern NPV at fair value, 2.5× at the discounted tier, 0 for a seizure) so nationalization is a real fiscal commitment. Granularity is single-sector, whole-corp (absorb sectors + state assumes the corp's bonds + pay shareholders pro-rata in their own currency + dissolve the shell), or industry-wide carve. Eligibility triggers: financial distress (insolvency / defaulted bond / abandoned CEO, behind a grace window), strategic-sector designation, monopoly dominance, supermajority vote. **Multi-NatCorp model:** one primary National Corporation per country plus per-sector-type split-offs; nationalizations route per type via `resolveNationalCorporationForSector`; the finance minister can split-off, merge-back, and appoint/remove NatCorp CEOs (`assertTreasuryAuthority`). **SOE operations** (turn phase): public-service mandate → state-metric contributions, dynamic efficiency (replaces the flat −15%), treasury-backing, employment/price postures, CEO operations (profit-split remittance, treasury draw, R&D modernization momentum). **Privatization:** spin a new corp out of the NatCorp via IPO or auction, golden share, re-nationalization / re-privatization cooldowns, below-reserve re-absorption. **Politics:** per-country investor-confidence index (decays toward baseline; feeds private-corp margins, the sovereign borrowing premium, and new-corp founding cost), approval/legitimacy deltas, ideology gate. **Counterplay:** notice window + cure path for player takings, financial-distress clock + public at-risk badge, facing-nationalization badge, one-time transition revenue haircut + a decaying productivity shock. **Visibility:** public country-wide State Ownership Register (acquisition + political-cost ledger) and privatization-offering notifications to country residents. Whole-corp takings release foreign-held sectors to the unowned market (a country can't hold assets under another jurisdiction). UI: National Corporation page (Overview / Public Mandates / Holdings / Register tabs + official controls), executive wizards, per-country propose-modal provisions, per-country identity seals. `src/lib/nationalization/`, `src/components/national/`, `src/app/api/country/[code]/national-corporation/`, `src/app/api/country/[code]/nationalize/`.

- **National Budget → treasury / finance-ministry redesign + live treasury balance.** New signed `federalBudget.treasuryBalance` is the country's running cash position, accrued each turn by a new `processTreasuryTurn` phase and moved by nationalization compensation, CEO treasury draws, and privatization proceeds; debt + sovereign-health derive from it. The National Budget page is reskinned as a finance-ministry surface: treasury masthead + seal, fiscal stat strip, revenue→treasury→spending sankey, expandable rate/base/laws/grants breakdowns, debt & credit panel with trend sparkline, sovereign-health state machine, and a gated finance-minister lens. One-time backfill seeds the field from existing debt. `src/lib/budget/treasuryBalance.ts`, `src/lib/turn/treasuryTurn.ts`, `src/components/budget/treasury/`, `scripts/migrations/2026-06-03-seed-treasury-balance.ts`.

- **Region seat appointer generalized to all countries.** The region admin seat-appointer was US-shaped (Senate/House/State Senate); it is now config-driven via a `getRegionAppointableSeats` SSOT so every non-UK country exposes its correct appointable seats.

- **Legislation "Dispatch" redesign.** Reskinned the legislation surfaces into an editorial-broadsheet style using the game's existing theme tokens (works in every theme). New shared kit `src/components/legislature/dispatch/`: a vote-colored `VoteSeatingChart` (hemicycle / Westminster benches / new Dáil horseshoe geometry in `src/lib/charts/seatingLayouts.ts`), `dispatchStatusMeta`/`posName` helpers, and broadsheet primitives. Bills lists across US Congress + all six country legislatures + state legislatures now render the shared broadsheet `BillCard` row with a "The Count" tally rail; the card/compact view toggle and `BillCompactRow` are retired in favour of one list rendering. Bill detail (`src/app/congress/bills/[id]/page.tsx`, shared by all countries) gains a live vote-colored seating-chart hero and a country-specific "How it becomes law" panel, both driven by a new per-country legislative-process registry `src/lib/legislature/process.ts`. Ireland's Dáil composition uses a party-colored horseshoe (`src/components/HorseshoeParliamentChart.tsx`). The five near-identical country propose-bill modals (UK/DE/JP/IE/CN) are unified into one config-driven `ProposeLegislationModal`, with subsidy support extended to Germany and Ireland (~2,900 LOC removed). Registered the `--font-display` (Fraunces) theme token. `src/lib/legislature/process.ts`, `src/components/legislature/dispatch/`, `src/components/bills/BillsList.tsx`, `src/app/country/[code]/legislature/ProposeLegislationModal.tsx`.

- **Quorum acceleration for chair elections.** When >50% of party members have cast a vote and the chair seat is vacant, the remaining chair-election timer is halved (fires once per election, tracked by `quorumAcceleratedAtTurn`). `src/lib/nationalPartyElections.ts`.

- **IP/VPN detection for new registrations.** ipapi.co integration checks new signups for VPN, proxy, and Tor usage. Results stored as `ipDetails` on the user document. Master toggle `ipDetectionEnabled` in gameConfig — off by default. Registration, Discord OAuth, and Google OAuth all fire-and-forget the check. Login backfills users missing IP data when the toggle is on. Moderator view shows VPN flag; admin view shows full IP intelligence (geo, ISP, AS, threat score). Admin backfill endpoint `POST /api/admin/ip-backfill` processes 10 users per call with rate limiting. `src/lib/ip/ipteoh.ts`, `src/app/api/admin/ip-backfill/route.ts`.

- **Ireland (IE) promoted from `coming-soon` to playable country.** Full 10-phase parity build-out: Dáil (PR-STV), Seanad (cosmetic composition), Uachtarán (7-year term, 2-term limit), Local Council + Cathaoirleach, Taoiseach/Tánaiste executive hub, Government of Ireland cabinet shell, Oireachtas legislature page, Áras an Uachtaráin page, 41 national + 4 regional legislation types, 11 tax types with USC/CGT/Excise, IE-specific metrics and domain affinities. Wired into election registry, bill lifecycle, per-turn spawners, admin seeder, news, wiki, public API, and Discord webhooks.

- **Germany legislation overhaul (PR1–PR5).** 8 tax types (income, VAT, domestic corporate, foreign corporate, payroll, customs, trade/Gewerbesteuer, solidarity surcharge), all with 11-bracket LARP titles and English-name + German-parenthetical descriptions. 22 new national policy types across economy, labour, infrastructure, housing, environment, agriculture, media, governance, immigration, defence, foreign policy, technology, public safety, health, education, welfare, family, gender equality, research, academic reform, university tuition, education funding, public health, mental health, elder care, health insurance, unemployment welfare, family policy, pension system, constitutional protection, criminal justice, policing, digital governance, robotics/AI, trade agreements, EU integration, cybersecurity, defence posture, Bundeswehr funding. 5 new Land-level state-scoped types. `landtag` chamber added. Solidaritätszuschlag + Gewerbesteuer wired through revenue calc and budget UI. DE-specific metrics baselined per Land.

- **China legislation overhaul (PR1–PR5).** 9 tax types with 11-bracket LARP + English-parenthetical labels (LVAT, UMCT, Stamp Duty wired into revenue calc and budget UI). 21 national policy types across healthcare, education, social policy, defence, security, foreign policy, technology, public safety, media, governance, agriculture, immigration/hukou, environment/energy, infrastructure, economy, environment, provincial. CN-specific metrics (socialCredit, beltAndRoad, commonProsperity) seeded. `peoplesCongress` chamber for provincial bills. CN_DOMAIN_AFFINITIES 8×13 archetype matrix. Provincial resource tax wired into regional budget recompute.

- **One-party state collapse subsystem (China v1).** Slow-buildup regime-legitimacy model: `popularLegitimacy` and `partyConfidence` scalars drift each turn from six drivers (economy, repression, policy alignment, election credibility, intra-party bleed, natural recovery). Four-stage escalation chain (discontent → crisis → internalChallenge → collapse) with dwell windows. Each stage offers the ruling leader a 48-turn decision event (single-slot queue + ignore default). Stage 3 auto-fires a faction split spawning a defected approved party. Five liberalization actions (legalize banned party, reduce vote multipliers, honest by-election, anti-corruption purge, constitutional amendment) with per-action cooldowns and per-turn boost modifiers on `countryState`. Voluntary constitutional convention (announce → draft → ratify) and forced Stage-4 conversion both flip regime via `triggerSystemConversion`, parking a snap-election marker with legacy-seat reservation. UI: public Regime Stability panel, leader-only Regime Health tab, approved-party offers inbox, admin override panel. Wiki country-history badges regime events. E2E sim harness validates 263-turn collapse-from-Stage-1 under sustained mismanagement.

- **CN State Council expansion.** 7 new ministerial seats added (Agriculture, Culture, Ecology, Emergency Management, Human Resources, Justice, Veterans Affairs), bringing total to 16. Agriculture domain scoped to Agriculture minister's bill scope. State Council position count updated on executive hub. CN provincial legislatures and provincial governors live — Provincial People's Congress delegate elections resolve on one-party model, provincial governor elections schedule at bootstrap.

- **Turn-based deadline migration (Phase 1–4).** All time-sensitive game systems migrated from wall-clock deadlines to turn-based counters: parliamentary votes (PM/no-confidence), congressional leadership elections (Speaker, whips, Bundestagspräsident), general election timers (startTurn/primaryEndTurn/endTurn), primary stagger UI, crisis-decision windows, sovereign-default windows, Table-3 deadline holdouts, recruitment cooldowns (state + national), relocation cooldowns. New server/client clock helpers: `formatRemainingTurns`, `projectTurnToDate`, `formatAbsoluteDeadline`, `toAbsoluteWallClock`, `computeElectionPhase`. Game clock catches up to real time when behind. `shiftDeadlinesOnResume` handles stale `isProcessing` locks.

- **Device fingerprinting overhaul (ThumbmarkJS).** Replaced legacy fingerprint with component-level ThumbmarkJS-backed fingerprints. Normalized components sent from register/login forms, persisted on login/register, accepted in auth request bodies, stored on User type. Post-OAuth capture via result-page hook + advisory `record-fingerprint` endpoint. Component-level fuzzy matching (medium severity). Escalation: 3+ shared fingerprints → high with accurate N-way count. Degenerate clusters filtered from high escalation. OAuth cookie fingerprint persisted at registration for gate coverage.

- **Mod tools: deleted-account handling + investigative-trail retention (Admin & Moderator).** Suspicious Activity hides flags for deleted characters/accounts behind a new **Deleted Accounts** filter; High/Medium/Low counts exclude deleted accounts. Deletion preserves investigative trail: `character_deleted` snapshot written to `activityLog` before deletion, gameplay `actionLogs` retained + identity-stamped on deletion instead of purged. Action logs surfaced in Activity Log as `game_action` entry type. Activity Log gains High/Medium/Low **flag-severity filter**. Pure unit-tested helpers extracted (`src/lib/admin/suspiciousList.ts`, `src/lib/admin/activityLog.ts`).

- **Corporation stock ticker symbols.** Required unique ticker symbol on founding (3–5 uppercase letters), validated in schema, unique partial index on `corporations.tickerSymbol`, persisted on Corporation type, passed through stock snapshot to listings. Stock UI: ticker prefix search, ticker sort field, dedicated left column for ticker in stock list, scrolling ticker bar uses symbol as label.

- **Cloudflare R2 upload migration.** All image upload routes (avatar, coat-of-arms, corporation-header/logo, news-image, party-logo, profile-header, wiki-image, screenshot, player-ads, cabinet banner) migrated from Vercel Blob to Cloudflare R2. New `src/lib/r2.ts` S3 client. `@vercel/blob` removed; `@aws-sdk/client-s3` added. `BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN` retained as optional legacy fallback.

- **Character creation improvements.** Starting wealth raised to 1M/2.5M/5M (was 500k/1M/2M). Home-currency preview on creation. Discord linking during character creation (OAuth in new tab, polls `/api/auth/me`, callback redirects back). One-click randomizer for country, name, home state, policies, demographics. China selection shows one-party CCP disclaimer.

- **New-character transfer barrier.** Blocks new characters from sending money for 24 turns. Surfaces cooldown unblock time on election DTOs and disabled Run buttons.

- **Campaign Manager iframe rendering fix.** Same-origin framing allowed so Campaign Manager renders in political operations.

- **Landing page skeleton loading.** Layout-matching skeleton shell replaces centered spinner. Dynamic globe fallback and d3-geo loading state render circular skeleton placeholders.

- **Heap / observability diagnostics.** Function-level leak diagnostics via admin endpoints: async allocation-profile (POST starts, GET retrieves), heap timeline, per-turn deltas, top-constructor diagnostics. RSS-aware heap watchdog thresholds, heap+rss logging at each boot-init step. Pre-OOM heap watchdog. Cron stale-check fall-through for stuck `isProcessing` locks. Sentry `withMonitor` timing + hang/throw guards.

- **Discord webhooks for CN and IE.** Game-event webhooks fire for China and Ireland.

- **AdSense integration.** Sitemap, FAQ, structured data, and ad inventory across public pages. AdSense slots integrated; non-AdSense networks purged from `ads.txt`.

### Changed

- **Sector subsidy margin bonus halved — 15%→7.5% per subsidy.** Each qualifying national or state subsidy now provides +7.5 percentage points of profit margin (down from +15). Stacked federal+state subsidies go from +30pp to +15pp. `src/lib/subsidies/subsidyEffects.ts`, `src/lib/subsidies/subsidyEffects.test.ts`, plus 12 UI text references across legislation modals, subsidy tabs, financials, policy pages, and wiki content.

- **Telecommunications state specializations added.** No state/region previously had telecommunications as a primary or secondary sector specialization, so telecom sectors never received the +2%–4% margin bonus available to other sectors. Added telecom as secondary for New Jersey (US), Georgia (US), Dublin (IE), and Huanan (CN) — all regions with major real-world telecom/data-center infrastructure. `src/lib/admin/seed/seedStateSectorSpecializations.ts`.

- **Influence-weighted national leadership election cards.** When a party uses influence-weighted voting (`leadershipElectionMethod === "influence"`), national election cards now display vote counts rounded to two decimal places (e.g. 42.37 instead of 42) and show an "Influence committed" pill badge with hover tooltip explaining the tally method. `NationalElectionPanel.tsx`, `partyView.ts`, `leadership.ts`, `page.tsx`.

- **Admin/mod player autocomplete now searches by username, character name, and Discord name.** The `/api/characters/search` endpoint now also looks up users by `username` and `discordUsername`, returning characters linked to matched users. The `PlayerSelector` component displays the username (`@`) and Discord name below the character name in dropdown results. The `ModeratorsManagementTab` assign-moderator form now uses `PlayerSelector` instead of a plain text input that fetched all users. `characters/search/route.ts`, `PlayerSelector.tsx`, `ModeratorsManagementTab.tsx`.

- **Mod tools: Users + Activity Log usability (Admin & Moderator).** Duplicate-account groups are collapsible accordions (collapsed by default, Expand all / Collapse all). Users list paginates to 25 rows/page (10 groups/page in Groups view). Activity Log replaces "Load more" with Prev/Next pagination at 25 entries/page.

- **Crisis system rework — planning.** Began two-engine consolidation on `crisis-rework` branch: collapse admin crises, sovereign default, and one-party regime escalation onto a single **Unified Crisis Engine**, plus additive **Player Random Events Engine**. Four-phase plan documented at `docs/plans/events-crisis-audit-plan.md`; autonomous handoff at `docs/plans/crisis-rework-handoff.md`. Documents-only pending design approval.

- **Notifications page auto-clears unread on open.** Opening `/notifications` marks all as read automatically; navbar red dot clears immediately. `IntersectionObserver`-based scroll auto-mark removed. Mail retains manual read flow.

- **Currency display.** Flag emojis/images replaced with currency symbols across forex and UI surfaces.

- **NPP donor base level.** Spawned at level 1 instead of 0.

### Fixed

- **Notification bell badge no longer counts unread mail (#0676).** `NavbarWrapper` added `unreadMailCount` to the bell's `unreadCount`, so a user with unread mail but zero unread notifications saw a badge dot on the bell, yet the Notifications page showed "All caught up" with no "Mark all as read" button. The badge now reflects only unread notifications. The notifications page also refreshes the navbar bootstrap after "Mark all as read" so the badge clears immediately, and shows a hint (e.g. "· 3 unread in Mail") when there are no unread notifications but unread mail exists. `src/components/NavbarWrapper.tsx`, `src/app/notifications/page.tsx`.

- **5G/Infrastructure strategy was strictly worse than Standard.** The telecommunications 5G strategy supplied less network_services (0.35 vs 0.40) and less software (0.15 vs 0.20) than Standard while demanding significantly more inputs (0.74 vs 0.54 total), making it a trap option with no reason to ever pick it. Rebalanced into a network_services specialist: supply raised to 0.55 network_services / 0.10 software (0.65 total, matching Cloud Services), demand reworked toward infrastructure capex (construction_services 0.15, steel 0.08, electronics lowered to 0.12, copper to 0.06, rare_earth removed). Now the three strategies have clear identities — Standard (balanced), 5G (network_services specialist), Cloud (software specialist). `src/lib/constants/sectorStrategies.ts`.

- **Private-corp Market Strength leaked through the region economy API (#0690).** The `/api/country/[code]/region/[id]/economy` endpoint built each sector's `owners[]` straight from the corporation docs and emitted `defenderMarketingStrength` (plus absolute `revenue` and `workers`) for every corp unconditionally — it never consulted the canonical `shouldRedactCorporation` policy that the corp detail/list/bonds/history/portfolio routes already honor. So a private corporation's marketing strength (and revenue/headcount) were readable by any viewer — public scrapers and the Discord bot included — even though the in-game UI restricted that overview to the CEO/admin/mod. The route now derives viewer context (owner, admin, or moderator with `?modView=1`) and nulls `defenderMarketingStrength`/`revenue`/`workers` for private corps to everyone else; it also nulls derived attack cost/capture estimates so logged-in non-owners cannot infer the hidden fields. `marketShare` is preserved so the Sector Targets ranking still works, the state economy UI renders redacted values as undisclosed, and direct sector-detail API requests for private corps now 403 unless the viewer is allowed. `src/app/api/country/[code]/region/[id]/economy/route.ts`, `src/components/state/StateEconomy.tsx`, `src/lib/corporations/queries/sectorDetail.ts`.

- **Universal search now suggests players by Discord handle.** The `/api/search/universal` endpoint now searches the `users` collection by `discordUsername`. When typing a player's Discord handle, their character profile appears in search results (unless already found by name). Duplicate results are suppressed and banned users are filtered out. `src/app/api/search/universal/route.ts`.

- **Corporation spend errors now show the correct currency symbol.** Four sector-operation endpoints hardcoded `$` in error messages regardless of the corporation's actual currency — a Japanese corp (¥), German corp (€), or UK corp (£) would all see dollar signs on insufficient-capital errors. Fixed `expandSector.ts`, `cancelSectorStrategy.ts`, `setSectorStrategy.ts` to use `insufficientCapitalMessage()` which resolves `CURRENCY_SYMBOLS[currencyCode]` and converts anchor amounts to local currency for display.

- **Cross-country party-action exploit closed (#0668).** Party `sequentialId` is unique per country, so a character's party id could collide onto a same-id party in another country — letting (e.g.) a CCP chairman chip the Republican party's Org in Texas via the region politics tab's Contest panel. Added a shared same-country guard (`crossCountryActionGuard`, reusing `isSameCountry`) to every region-scoped party action — Contest, Build Org, GOTV, Suppression, Org Building, NPP Influence (and the Contest/Build-Org previews) — placed before any spender/auth resolution so it applies to admins too. The region politics tab now treats an out-of-country viewer as unaffiliated so the Contest panel / Quick Actions / budget cards no longer surface. `src/lib/api/crossCountryGuard.ts`, `src/app/api/country/[code]/region/[id]/party/[partyId]/{contest,build-org,gotv,suppression,org-building,influence}/`, `src/app/country/[code]/region/[id]/page.tsx`.

- **US presidential vote now factors registration (Reg%).** `accumulatePresidentVoteTurn` built `partyOrgByParty` from Organization but never built/passed `regByParty`, so the legacy distribution engine's `regResistanceMultiplier` always saw `undefined` → neutral `1.0×`. The presidential race ignored Reg% entirely while every down-ballot race (via `tallyManagement.ts`) already applied the entrenchment tilt. Now builds `regByParty` per state from `statePartyOrg.registration` and threads it into the distribution options. `src/lib/presidentialElectionEngine.ts`.

- **Registration Influence card now shows real per-state lean (US presidential).** `GeneralPhaseView` hard-coded `regByState: undefined`, so the card always rendered the "no registration data tracked" placeholder. `_enrichElection` now computes a per-state party-lean breakdown (`statePartyOrg.registration` + `stateRegistrationPool` independent/unregistered buckets), country-scoped, and threads it (plus `partyDisplayById`) onto the presidential response for general and completed races. `src/lib/elections/presidentialRegByState.ts`, `src/lib/elections/enrichElection.ts`.

- **Third parties can now build Reg%.** `planStateRegDriftDecay` filtered out `StatePartyOrg` rows whose `registration` was `undefined`, so an Org-bearing third party with no seeded Reg lane (e.g. US Reform / DSA) was skipped by passive drift forever. Per design §4.2.1/§4.4, drift applies to every party (undefined Reg treated as 0); the filter is removed so Org-bearing parties accrue a registered base toward their Org. `src/lib/turn/partyOrg/regDriftDecay.ts`.

- **Inflation and FX monetary baseline rebalance.** Inflation now evaluates each country against explicit target-inflation and neutral-prime-rate baselines instead of a universal 2% / 3% assumption, so Brazil, Nigeria, China, Japan, and developed markets are judged against fair policy regimes. FX macro targeting and central-bank chair scrutiny use the same monetary baselines; future central-bank defaults were aligned to those neutral rates. 1991 cost-of-living inflation pressure now treats the era-scaled index as neutral, and deep stale deflation recovers faster once current drivers return near target.

- **FX↔inflation deflation feedback loop dampened.** Deflationary forex pass-through attenuated as function of distance below inflation target (downward price stickiness): unchanged at target, halved at 4 pp below, decaying toward zero in deep deflation. Only reinforcing arm touched; inflationary arm unchanged. `src/lib/budget/inflation.ts`.

- **Central bank bootstrap vacancy (#0634).** `processCentralBankChairSelection()` skipped banks where `chairCharacterId === null && chairTermExpiresAtTurn === null` (bootstrap vacancy), so lobbying accumulated but selection never ran. Added `bootstrapVacancy` flag so first-chair selection triggers correctly. One-time DB fix applied to CN (People's Bank of China) in production.

- **Election system — withdrawn candidates filtered from primary polling.**

- **Opposition Leader exclusion.** Plurality party excluded from Opposition Leader when no government formed.

- **Build Org presence check.** Now reads live state instead of cached flag.

- **Party treasury rendering.** Treasury/funds displayed in local currency without FX double-conversion.

- **Corporation dissolution.** Conserves money via salvage NPV + in-kind equity. Charges founders local-currency cost, not anchor currency. Blocked until corp is 24 turns old. Quick dissolve allowed when CEO is sole real shareholder.

- **Public-float market maker.** Now treasury-backed.

- **Cabinet effect-key resolution.** Tier/regionalTarget/advocacy keys resolve to nested metric paths via shared `resolveMetricPath` util.

- **Game-clock parity.** Catches up game time to real time when behind. Stale drift machinery decommissioned.

- **Seed refresh mode.** Hard reset rebuilds unowned market sizes so preset/GDP switches reconstruct correctly.

- **1991 market recompute.** Pure planner scales owned+unowned markets by per-cell ratio (share-preserving). Orphan detection scoped to all live states so `--country` never deletes other countries' markets.

- **Auth nav re-fetch.** Re-fetches after relocation/creation to prevent stale currency context.

- **Regime diagnostics.** Site admins can read regime diagnostics. `isSittingLeader` routed through canonical head-of-government resolver. Runtime-gates CN ops tabs behind `governmentType === "onePartyState"`. Force-stage clears active decision + queue before offering fresh. Admin override `reason` strings no longer leak admin email. `governingPartyId` populated by self-heal.

- **Wiki cache invalidation.** Invalidated after reseed so pages appear immediately.

- **Bond route pause enforcement.** Corporation-actions pause enforced on bond routes and turn engine.

- **Election timer LARP years.** Derived from per-game starting year, honoring active preset.

- **Primary stagger UI.** Driven off turn counter instead of wall-clock.

- **Campaign Manager endorsements.** Allowed for any same-country race, not just presidential.

- **NPP party-line voting.** Default force added to reduce co-partisan abstaining.

- **Non-office NPP campaign priority.** Given priority so they build influence.

- **Auth cookie collision.** Env-prefix on auth-token cookie name prevents sandbox/live collision.

- **Unexpected logout diagnosis.** Auth-cookie clear paths instrumented.

- **Create-character duplicate navbar.** Removed for new players.

- **Tab tooltips on Corporation, Parties, and Stock Market pages.** Hovering over any tab on the corporation detail page, country parties page, or stock market page now shows a descriptive tooltip explaining what that tab contains.

- **Sector split UI streamlined — split size removed, strength selector moved inline.** The Small / Normal / Large split-size dimension has been removed from sector-market-share attacks. Only the Full vs Half strength choice remains. The strength selector is now exposed directly on unowned market cards in the state Economy tab, so players can pick Full or Half strength and see live cost, marketing-strength cost, estimated capture, and capture percentage without leaving the state page. The sector detail page's Attack panel was updated to match. `splitSize` removed from the attack API schema and cost/capture calculations; `splitSizes` removed from `AttackInfo`; `SplitSizeInfo` interface kept for backwards compatibility.

- **1991 national budget calibration.** Pre-2000 absolute policy costs now scale against the active country's historical nominal budget context, while GDP-multiplier and budget-percent costs remain ratio-based. National budget seeding now mirrors runtime enacted-law spending instead of double-counting baseline spending plus default policy costs. This brings 1991 US / UK deficit-to-GDP inputs back into plausible ranges and prevents inflation diagnostics from treating malformed fiscal data as a real macro shock. The admin federal-budget heal action now regenerates default enacted laws from the active game preset instead of silently falling back to `2019-default`.

### Fixed

- **Dividend Yield chart shows actual paid rate, not raw stored rate.** The chart used `dividendRate` from `corporationHistory`, which stored only the CEO-set rate (0 if never explicitly set). For pass-through structures (LLP, LLC, etc.) with a legal minimum, the chart showed 0% despite dividends being paid. Fixed to store the minimum-enforced rate so the chart reflects what shareholders actually receive. `src/lib/turn/corporation/sectorCalculations.ts`.

- **Status bar dividend income includes pass-through legal-minimum payouts.** The `/api/client-status` route filtered corps on `dividendRate > 0`, excluding pass-through corporations where the CEO never explicitly set a rate but a legal minimum (e.g. 25% for LLP) still pays dividends. Removed the filter so all shareholder corps contribute. `src/app/api/client-status/route.ts`.

### Changed

- **NPP corporation AI overhauled with profit-aware decision-making.** NPP corps now analyze sector profitability before making any decision. Losing sectors get reduced growth targets; profitable sectors get increased targets. Budgets scale down when losing money (minimal marketing/logistics, zero R&D) and scale up when profitable. Expansion only triggers when the corp is profitable with >$1M liquid capital, targeting the highest-revenue unowned sector in-country (30% chance per turn). Dividends only paid when profitable. `src/lib/turn/nppCorporationBehavior.ts`, `src/lib/turn/corporation/index.ts`.

- **NPP capital state mapping added.** `NPP_CAPITAL_STATES` constant maps each country to its capital state for NPP corp HQ placement. `batchSpawnNppCorporations` now uses this instead of defaulting to DC. `src/lib/admin/spawnNppCorporation.ts`.

- **Admin spawn-npp-all route.** New `POST /api/admin/corporations/spawn-npp-all` endpoint batch-spawns NPP corporations across all enabled countries in one call, skipping countries that already have NPP corps. `src/app/api/admin/corporations/spawn-npp-all/route.ts`.

### Added (later 0.3.1 testing patches)

- **Player Corporation UI reskin.** Player corporation surfaces realigned to a market-instrument visual identity matching the design prototype — overview, financials, and operational tabs reskinned across the corporation detail page.

- **CEO share buyback & escrow desk.** Per-corporation share buyback mode (Instant vs Escrow) configurable from the CEO Budget tab. The CEO self-acquisition cap is surfaced in the share issuance and purchase modals so a CEO can see how many of their own corp's shares they may still acquire. Bond repayments can draw on a positive escrow balance before defaulting (per-turn and cash-pay), and the escrow balance settles into dissolution / force-liquidate / bond-default / takeover payouts. Escrow fields are redacted for non-CEO viewers of private corps.

- **Corporate escrow ledger + funding warning.** Escrow funding and manual withdrawals now write `corp_escrow_funding` / `corp_escrow_withdrawal` ledger rows, and the CEO Budget tab warns when per-turn escrow funding exceeds the corporation's recent net income.

- **Dual Public Scrutiny pool for dual-role officials.** Officials holding both a state and a national office can choose which Public Scrutiny pool (State or National) a spend draws from.

- **Preset-aware US apportionment.** The Electoral College and House seat apportionment now follow the active preset (1991 vs 2019 census) instead of hardcoded modern figures — presidential EC resolution + electoral map, House race spawn/resolve/snapshot, primary delegate rescale, and the EV-scaled travel-action cost all read from preset apportionment.

- **Interactive Persuasion Drivers card.** The election Persuasion Drivers card gained Focus/Opponent selectors so players can compare any candidate pairing. A Presidential Coattails row and an approval-based Gubernatorial Coattails row were added, and Governor approval now feeds the gubernatorial coattail magnitude in the turn engine.

- **Banner-ad cooldown shown in turns.** Player banner-ad submissions display a turn-based cooldown window in the composer and on `/player-ads`; `canSubmit` respects the window.

- **Unified position-label ruler.** Candidate, party/coalition, and region political-lean labels share one bucket ruler with an exact score shown on hover, and bill-vote / manual-compass position writes snap to a common position grid.

- **Legislation "Dispatch" refinements.** Bill detail gained metric-effect chips on provisions, an all-party Whip Count with a thin dispatch vote bar, a unified vote module with Member Vote History, and a vote-colored seating hero on state bills; government hubs render a country-correct chamber composition chart. The not-in-game IE Presidential referral / Supreme Court step was dropped from the IE process panel.

- **UK agriculture & technology legislation.** Added UK agriculture and technology national legislation types.

- **NPP slate auto-file rule shown.** The election race card now explains the NPP auto-file compliance rule.

- **Bill discussion threads.** Legislator-gated discussion threads on national and regional bill pages, with thumbs up/down reactions, admin soft-hide, a slur filter, and 10-per-page pagination. Eligibility and national bill actions resolve the chamber key to the correct office type (CN `npc` → `npcDelegate`), and the whip panel is guarded against cross-country `sequentialId` collisions.

- **China NPCSC & CPPCC chairs.** Two new Chinese offices — NPCSC Chairman (any seated delegate) and CPPCC Chairman (largest party) — each with a UI panel on the NPC page, a read-only executive-hub card, election resolution, and turn hooks.

- **Germany Bundestag fills all 630 seats.** Landeslisten are auto-generated at Bundestag spawn (within the chair edit window) and an aggregate-native AMS resolver fills the list-tier seats to 630; list-seat holders are named so member/composition counts include them. Guarded per cycle via `gameState.lastBundestagReconciledCycle`.

- **US presidential veto webhook.** US presidential vetoes are announced on the game-events webhook.

- **Logout tracking.** Logout now records a timestamp to parity with login, surfaced in the admin Last Logout column.

- **Turn-based countdowns surfaced.** Election and vote countdowns now show the remaining turns.

### Changed (later 0.3.1 testing patches)

- **CEO salary capped at 1.25× revenue.** CEO salary is now capped at 1.25× the corporation's revenue, and a founding cooldown was added.

- **Sector trades logged accurately.** Buying or selling a sector market share now logs as a Sector Purchase / Sector Sale ledger entry instead of "Founding Capital."

- **24-turn party-tenure gate on leadership.** Members must hold party membership for 24 turns before they can run in or vote in national, state, or caucus leadership elections.

- **One Party State cabinet — any player eligible.** One Party States can appoint any non-banned player (not only sitting legislators) to cabinet; the head-of-government seat is auto-assigned from the sitting PM and rendered read-only in the cabinet UI.

- **IE/CN default spending laws + 1991 budget reconciliation.** Ireland and China now seed default spending laws so expenditure populates on both presets, 1991 spending and revenue are reconciled to era-realistic deficits, and a one-time migration reset bug-driven federal debt back to 1991 levels.

- **Subsidy/tariff sector lists complete.** Subsidy and tariff legislation list every sector with proper labels, including newly added sectors.

- **Country-aware GOTV / suppression / turnout.** GOTV and suppression targeting, and the region turnout API, work for every archetype country instead of the US set only.

- **Country-aware voter archetypes.** Poll and ad voter-archetype lists are country-aware.

- **Status bar freshness.** Actions and campaign funds refresh on turn, focus, and poll, and campaign-fund amounts render in local currency rather than the anchor currency.

- **EUR grants credit DE & IE equally.** EUR cash and campaign grants credit German and Irish players on the same anchor rate.

- **Region-economy actions charged in local currency.** Corporation region-economy spends are charged and displayed in the corp's local currency.

- **Party treasury revenue SSOT.** Live party-revenue surfaces and per-turn fund generation route through shared projection helpers; NPP fund generation no longer runs through FX.

- **China PRC leadership terminology + head of state.** Chinese PRC titles are localized (e.g. General Secretary, Secretariat, CMC Chairman) and the head-of-state line now shows the office-based President of the PRC.

- **Governor's Office — NPP party-officer delegation.** Party officers can now manage NPP-held regional offices (orders, legislation, devolution, bill assent); institutional tabs and bill assent are gated on manage-access, with an office link surfaced to NPP-office party officers.

- **Game clock converges to wall-clock.** In normal mode the game clock now converges to real time rather than only ratcheting forward.

- **Inflation breakdown shows monetary stance.** The central-bank inflation breakdown tooltip surfaces the monetary-stance driver, and cabinet policy inflation pressure is threaded into the per-turn recompute, the Governor strip, and admin diagnostics.

- **Legislation Dispatch refinements (cont.).** Bill detail shows archetype approvals under the metric provisions, the cross-pressure prediction member list paginates (10/page), and the Current → Proposed comparison stacks on mobile.

### Fixed (later 0.3.1 testing patches)

- **Corporation errors on CEO actions.** Widespread corporation server errors on found / take-CEO paths were traced to a positional `ceoHistory` array update on an absent field; the update is now guarded. Campaign-song thumbnail loading was also fixed.

- **CEO/ex-CEO bond self-dealing blocked.** CEOs, pending CEOs, and recent former CEOs can no longer buy their own corporation's bonds, and a CEO holding the corp's bonds is skipped for nomination and blocked from accepting the seat.

- **Hostile-takeover bond payoff on standalone DB.** "Pay off bonds" no longer fails on standalone (non-replica-set) MongoDB; a money-safe sequential fallback with a compensation stack replaces the transaction.

- **Foreign votes leaking into bill tallies (#0699).** Foreign NPP legislators no longer leak into another country's bill vote tally where two countries share an office type.

- **CN Premier vote eligibility.** Fixed a chamber-key vs office-type mismatch affecting China's Premier appointment and no-confidence vote eligibility.

- **Party membership & leadership integrity (#0701).** Party `memberCount` is reconciled every turn; founders who split to a new party vacate their old party's leadership; departed members are removed from the old party's leadership races and their votes deleted; leadership-election quorum counts player voters, not NPPs.

- **Party merge integrity.** Merges cap absorbed NPPs at the recruitment limit, wipe the absorbed party's leftover org/registration, and transfer seats plus the coalition slot to the surviving party.

- **Purge rejoin scope.** A purge blocks rejoining only the purging party (24 turns) and clears the global party-switch cooldown so the purged player can join other parties immediately.

- **Candidates with no party org no longer zeroed (#0671).** A personal-reach floor keeps a candidate's effective organization from collapsing to zero when they have personal pull but no seeded org.

- **Region lean scale + foreign pollution (#0659).** Region political lean renders on the −5…+5 scale and ignores unweighted foreign voter-group categories.

- **Election closing-surge keyed to turns.** The end-of-race closing surge keys off turns rather than the drifting game clock.

- **NPP slate filing dead-zone.** The NPP slate resolver decides on compliance (binary) rather than a removed "considering" relationship lookup, fixing NPPs that accepted a slate but never filed.

- **Hard whips bind NPPs.** Non-player legislators obey hard leadership / PM / cabinet whips.

- **General-phase upgrade surcharge in cost preview.** Campaign upgrade cost previews include the general-phase surcharge, removing the "Insufficient funds" mismatch on an affordable-looking button.

- **Change Policy Positions targeting.** The admin/moderator Change Policy Positions tool resolves the target by `characterId` instead of treating the display name as a username.

- **statePartyOrg healing.** Rows missing `countryId` are healed and prevented, and parties with a presence but no seeded org row can Build Org and Contest.

- **Country-aware party roster office labels.** Party member rosters show the correct office label per country.

- **Tariff origin-country selector.** The tariff origin-country picker parses the `/api/game/countries` response shape correctly.

- **Archived campaigns locked.** Primary-loser campaigns are hidden from the elections list and locked read-only against direct-URL access.

- **Discord bot fixes.** Avatar/logo upload URLs are absolutised for embed thumbnails, `/predict` is data-driven so IE/BR/CN/NG work, and the IE Dáil per-region seat magnitude is corrected.

- **Share-buyback escrow decoupled from share price.** Share-buyback escrow no longer feeds into share-price valuation, removing a crash and price-distortion path.

- **Bill projected-effect direction is per-metric.** Bill pages show the projected-effect direction per metric (delta versus current law) instead of one option-level direction painted across every metric chip.

- **China bill chamber-key fixes.** CN bills store their chamber as the key `npc` (not the office type), the propose modal no longer crashes on agriculture/technology and hides empty categories, and CN whip / bill-action paths resolve the chamber key to the right office type.

- **Cabinet regional breakdown sorting.** Regional breakdown metric columns now sort by their composite key instead of being a no-op.

- **US region turnout populates.** The Demographics & Turnout tab's US region turnout reads through a shared helper so legacy region documents without a country tag no longer show blanks.

- **NPP economy defaults stamped on all creation paths.** Every NPP creation path now stamps economy defaults (donor base level and the like).

### Content (later 0.3.1 testing patches)

- **Ireland hero art.** Added region hero banner images for Ireland and a Dáil chamber interior for the legislature hero header.

- **1991/2019 demographics census parity.** Per-region census profiles added so the Demographics & Turnout tab populates for all archetype countries across both presets.

## [0.3.0] - 2026-05-24

### Added

- **Ireland (IE) promoted from `coming-soon` to playable country.** Closes a 10-phase parity build-out against the UK / DE / JP / CN parliamentary subsystems. New office types: `tanaiste` (deputy executive), `uachtaran` (President of Ireland, 7-year term, 2-term limit), `localCouncil` member, `cathaoirleach` (regional executive). New election types: `dail` (PR-STV, multi-seat, 10% threshold), `seanad` (cosmetic composition for now), `uachtaran` (nationwide IRV, 48h cycle), `localCouncil` (5-year IE cycle). NUTS-III regions renamed to county-based codes; region GeoJSON migrated. New surfaces: Áras an Uachtaráin page (sitting Uachtarán + Council of State ex-officio members), Government of Ireland parliamentary cabinet shell, IE Oireachtas page with Seanad chamber switcher, `IESeanadCompositionPanel` client component, `GET /api/country/[code]/legislature/seanad/composition` endpoint, `deriveSeanadComposition` helper, IE_DAIL_2020 historical seat fixture for 2019-default preset, IE `statePartyOrg` seeded from per-preset vote-share tables, `statePartyOrgMin` readiness raised to 40 (8 regions × 5 parties). `headOfStateTitle` set to "Uachtarán na hÉireann", national address renamed "Address to the Oireachtas". IE wired into per-turn election registry, admin `CharacterStateManager` + `UniversalSeeder`, `ElectionFilterBar` dropdowns, election headers, news, region elections layout, politicians filter, career history, endorsements, wiki layouts, publicApi, government legislation-types country filter, `electionToLarpYear` LARP labels, and admin discord. Pre-existing audit gaps closed in Pass 2.

- **`isCountryEnabledForPlayers` country-access helper.** Replaces ~15 hardcoded country allowlists across admin / mod / API / layout sites with a single boolean derived from `countryConfig.status === "active"`. New countries flip on simply by changing their config status; no scattered code edits required. Sweep covers admin route params, country metadata layouts, mod tooling, and the API allowlist registry.

- **`countryState` runtime collection.** Promotes per-country mutable runtime state (regime stage, popular legitimacy / party confidence scalars, reform cooldowns, popular-boost modifiers, convention state, post-conversion markers) out of the static `COUNTRY_CONFIGS` constant and into a persisted `countryState` MongoDB collection. New `getCountryState` / `updateCountryState` helpers, request-scoped cache, seed-from-config helper, seed-manifest classification, wipe-on-reset hook, and `useRuntimeCountryConfig` client hook + `GET /api/country/[code]/runtime-config` endpoint so client components read live runtime fields instead of compile-time snapshots. Migration script (`scripts/migrations/2026-05-28-promote-country-state.ts`) backfills existing databases.

- **Country-specific NPP name pools for all 8 countries.** `src/lib/npp/nameGenerator.ts` ships hand-curated first/last name lists for US, UK, JP, DE, CN, IE, BR, NG. Replaces a fall-through Anglo pool that produced obviously wrong names for non-Anglo countries. Sample script `scripts/debug/sample-npp-names.ts` and unit-test coverage included.

- **Preset-aware seeding for the 1991 alternate game start.** Three new layers thread the active preset through core seed phases: state baselines with 1991 era floor adjustments (`stateBaselines1991.ts`), demographics with 1991 cohort-share adjustments (`stateDemographics1991.ts`), and statePolicies with 1991 budget policy defaults (`basePolicies1991.ts`, 696 lines). Bundle-loading silent failures in `runCoreSeed.ts` resolved by threading preset into `seedBudgets` and `seedStateMetrics` calls.

- **Admin: auto-enable maintenance mode after every game reset.** `resetGameWorld` now writes `MaintenanceStatus { enabled: true }` as the final step so the world is gated behind the maintenance page while the new bootstrap settles, instead of letting players hit a half-seeded world. Mods flip maintenance back off manually when the reset is verified. `src/lib/maintenanceStatus.ts`.

- **Admin override panel on the CN executive hub.** New red "Admin" tab (after Regime Health) at `/country/cn/executive` lets admins force-stage / set-scalar / force-conversion without leaving the executive hub. Force-stage offers a fresh decision after clearing any active decision + queue.

- **Stage banner + colored severity on the Regime Health diagnostic tab.** Stage transitions get a banner with severity-colored chip; reform descriptions are expandable and show a 48-turn scalar projection so the leader can see where the system is heading before spending a reform.

- **Selective-concession reform now requires a banned-party picker.** Picks which banned party to legalize at action time rather than choosing the lowest-loyalty option automatically.

- **DE legislation overhaul (PR1): 8 tax types.** Rewrites `de_income_tax_rate`, `de_vat_rate`, `de_domestic_corporate_tax_rate` to English law titles with original-language German italic parenthetical in descriptions, expands each to 11 brackets. Adds 4 new tax types: `de_foreign_corp_tax_rate`, `de_payroll_tax_rate`, `de_customs_tax_rate`, `de_trade_tax_rate` (Gewerbesteuer). Adds `de_solidarity_surcharge` (Solidaritätszuschlag) as a separate 11-bracket law. New `tradeTax` + `solidaritySurcharge` enums on the taxType union. `landtag` added to the chamber union. Solidaritätszuschlag + Gewerbesteuer wired through `deriveTaxRates` at seed time, `revenue.ts` calc, and the budget UI line items. 8 new DE-specific `StateMetrics` fields added with baseline values per Land. Preset-year fixes: deLandtag preset anchor wired into `electionToLarpYear`, DE 1991 + 2020 preset `taxPolicyIds` repaired (stale IDs + missing tariffs/foreignCorp).

- **Bundestagspräsident office.** Auto-opens after general elections + vacates on government transition. Webhook fires for speaker / pt / bundestagspräsident leadership changes. Stale developer comments and wiki copy referencing "majority bloc" swept in `RoleEligibilityPolicy` rollout. Cabinet, demographics, and wiki parity fixes alongside.

- **One-party states wiki page.** Adds `onePartyStatesContent` covering the three regime tiers (Ruling / Approved / Banned), what each can/cannot do, why new player parties default to banned, and the confidence model. Surfaces at `/wiki/one-party-states`.

- **Persuasion drivers card on non-presidential generals.** General-election driver breakdown surfaces for Senate / Governor / House / State Senate races, not just president.

- **Election panels surface run-button cooldown countdowns.** Disabled Run button shows the new-character cooldown unblock time on election DTOs. Mid-flight election duration extensions retroactively apply to in-flight elections.

- **Function-level leak diagnostics via admin endpoints.** Async allocation-profile endpoint (POST starts, GET retrieves) + timeout + logging for inspector calls. Heap timeline, per-turn deltas, top-constructor diagnostics, RSS-aware heap watchdog thresholds, heap+rss logging at each boot-init step.

- **One-party state collapse subsystem.** Adds a slow-buildup regime-legitimacy model on top of one-party countries (CN is the first beneficiary; future OPS countries inherit automatically). Two coupled scalars — `popularLegitimacy` (public mood) and `partyConfidence` (intra-party stability) — drift each turn from six drivers (economy, repression, policy alignment, election credibility, intra-party bleed, natural recovery). A four-stage escalation chain (discontent → crisis → internalChallenge → collapse) gates entry on dwell windows so a single bad turn never collapses a regime. Each stage offers the ruling leader a decision event (with a single-slot 48-turn queue + ignore default), and Stage 3 auto-fires a faction split that spawns a defected approved party. A five-action liberalization menu (legalize a banned party, reduce vote multipliers, hold an honest by-election, anti-corruption purge, constitutional amendment) lets the leader trade intra-party confidence for popular legitimacy, with per-action cooldowns and per-turn boost modifiers persisted on `countryState`. A voluntary constitutional convention (announce → draft → ratify) and a forced Stage-4 conversion path both flip the regime to a target government type via the shared `triggerSystemConversion` driver, which parks a snap-election marker with legacy-seat reservation for the former ruling party. UI surfaces: a public Regime Stability panel on the country overview (band label only), a leader-only Regime Health tab on the CN executive hub (raw scalars + action buttons), an approved-party offers inbox, and an admin override panel at `/admin/country/[code]/regime`. Wiki country-history widget now badges regime events distinctly; CN was added to that widget's allowlist. Validated by an E2E simulation harness (`src/lib/turn/__sim__/`) measuring 263-turn collapse-from-Stage-1 time under sustained mismanagement.

- **Character creation UI overhaul.** Added a new "World State" card (section 1) before Politician Details, displaying the in-game date and preset-specific flavor text explaining the world state (1991 vs. 2019 starts). Country buttons now use CDN flag images instead of emoji flags via `next/image`. Each country button shows the count of registered player characters. China selection triggers a disclaimer about the one-party CCP system. `/api/game/countries` expanded to return `gameDate`, `flavorText`, `preset`, and per-country `playerCount`. (`src/app/api/game/countries/route.ts`, `src/app/create-character/page.tsx`, `src/app/register/components/PoliticianSection.tsx`, `src/app/register/components/registerTypes.ts`)

- **Discord link section on character creation.** New `DiscordLinkSection` component lets users link their Discord during character creation. Opens OAuth in a new tab so form progress is preserved, then polls `/api/auth/me` to detect completion. `/api/auth/discord` now accepts `?returnUrl` and stores it in a cookie so the callback redirects back to create-character. Explainer blurb notes Discord is recommended for party/nation coordination. (`src/app/create-character/DiscordLinkSection.tsx`, `src/app/create-character/page.tsx`, `src/app/api/auth/discord/route.ts`, `src/app/api/auth/discord/callback/route.ts`)

- **Randomize button for character creation.** One-click randomizer picks a random country, character name, home state, policy positions, and demographics, letting players quickly generate starting points. (`src/app/create-character/page.tsx`)

- **Landing page skeleton loading.** Replaced the centered spinner in `src/app/loading.tsx` with a layout-matching skeleton shell (navbar strip, hero text blocks, globe placeholder, content cards). The dynamic-import globe fallback (`LandingGlobeLoader.tsx`) and the internal d3-geo loading state (`LandingGlobe.tsx`) both now render circular skeleton placeholders instead of text spinners.

- **Beta 2 launch landing refresh.** Hero headline updated to the 5/29/26 Beta 2 start date with a client-side countdown. Closing CTA simplified. `BetaCountdown` component extracted to `src/components/landing/LandingClientBits.tsx`.

- **Corp sector split strength option.** Sector-market-share attacks now support a `splitStrength` parameter (`full` | `half`). Half-strength splits cost 50% less liquid capital and 50% less marketing strength (min 1 MS), but capture proportionally less market share. UI surfaced in `AttackPanel` with dynamic cost tooltips. `SPLIT_STRENGTH_MULTIPLIERS` and `calculateSplitMsCostForStrength` added to `marketActionCosts.ts`.

- **Cloudflare R2 upload migration.** All image upload routes (avatar, coat-of-arms, corporation-header, corporation-logo, news-image, party-logo, profile-header, wiki-image, screenshot, player-ads, cabinet banner) migrated from Vercel Blob to Cloudflare R2 (S3-compatible). New `src/lib/r2.ts` client wraps `@aws-sdk/client-s3`. Environment schema expanded with `CLOUDFLARE_R2_*` variables; `BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN` retained as optional legacy fallback. `@vercel/blob` removed from dependencies; `@aws-sdk/client-s3` added.

### Fixed

- **Regime audit pass 1 + 2 follow-ups.** Pass 1: per-turn driver self-heals missing leader-state row before reading scalars (was silently leaking back to seed defaults each tick); scalar leak through `adjust*` paths plugged; admin cleanup tightened on stage transition. Pass 2: runtime-gates CN ops tabs behind `governmentType === "onePartyState"` so a forced conversion immediately hides them; dead `transition` field on EscalationState dropped. Force-stage clears any active decision + queue before offering a fresh decision so admins can't end up with two pinned decisions at once. Admin override `reason` strings no longer leak the admin's email. `set-scalar` and `force-stage` now route through the canonical head-of-government resolver. `governingPartyId` populated by the regime self-heal so the public API can resolve the ruling party even on stale DBs. Convention escalation doc self-heals when missing; panel description expanded.

- **Auth: `isSittingLeader` routed through the canonical head-of-government resolver.** Was using a stale lookup that didn't recognize acting heads or the conversion-target seat assignment, so the leader-only Regime Health tab could 403 the actual leader on conversion DBs.

- **Natural-recovery rate halved + clearer projection label.** Slows passive bounceback so reform actions remain meaningfully expensive; projection card now labels the next 48-turn band rather than the absolute scalar.

- **Decision card shows label + option buttons (register-then-read).** Decision-event registry was being read before the per-turn registration tick fired, so the card briefly rendered with no buttons.

- **Taiwan removed from both CN macro-region maps.** Was previously drawn as part of the HD (East) region; now omitted from both `cn-regions.json` and `cn-regions-1991.json`. Independent of the era-aware HK/Macau handover logic.

- **Parliament chart scales for chambers as large as the CN NPC (2980 seats).** `parliamentSizing.ts` chooses row count + seat radius based on total seats so the NPC renders without overlap; previous fixed sizing assumed UK/JP-scale chambers and produced unreadable layouts for the NPC. `ChamberChart` and `parliamentChart` route through the new sizer.

- **JP_GOVERNORS_1990 renamed to JP_GOVERNORS_1991 for preset alignment.** Aligns the constant name with the 1991-default preset's actual game-start year; same historical roster, just the symbol name.

- **CN NPP seed names use Chinese first/last name pools** instead of the Anglo fall-through. NPP names for CN, DE, IE, BR, JP all draw from country-appropriate pools now.

- **Stage-2 and Stage-3 regime decision option descriptions tightened** for clarity on the trade-off each option represents.

- **`countryState` reset on game reset + classified in seed manifest.** Without this, post-reset `countryState` rows survived the wipe and carried regime stage / scalar state across resets.

- **Resource capacity now seeded after all regions load** so per-region capacity rows aren't missing for late-loading countries (#1799).

- **Bundestagspräsident viewer identity + `myVote` key.** Page used the wrong viewer-identity resolver, breaking the my-vote display for Bundestagspräsident bills. PM-favorability default-force tuning reduces co-partisan abstaining in NPP bill voting (#1767).

- **Non-office NPPs get a campaign priority so they build influence (#1763).** Without an explicit priority, NPPs holding no office sat idle on per-turn campaigning.

- **NPP endorsements: Campaign Manager endorsements allowed for any same-country race.** Was overly restricted to the CM's own race-family.

- **Bill UI shift-based metric preview.** Previews now apply `isHigherBetter` direction so an option that lowers a metric reads as red/green correctly. `effectDirection` convention fixed for JP / DE / IE / CN.

- **Minimum-wage types no longer mistakenly include `unemploymentRate`** as a metric effect.

- **Cron / observability hardening.** Stuck `isProcessing` locks now recover via stale-check fall-through. Sentry monitor check-in wired on turn cron; check-in time + hang/throw guard. Sentry monitor slug derived from `RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME` rather than `ENVIRONMENT_NAME`. Sentry alerting preserved when `Sentry.withMonitor` is itself the failure mode. unhandledRejection + uncaughtException now flush to Sentry before exit. `sentry.client.config.ts` added; Railway env detection falls back to `RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME`. Pre-OOM heap watchdog wired (later: heap snapshot dropped so trip completes). Output:standalone disabled so Next.js `instrumentation` runs on Railway. `railway.toml` startCommand reverted from standalone server to `next start`. `auth-token` cookie name env-prefixed to prevent sandbox/live collision.

- **`verifyAuth` only returns null on token-bad JOSE errors** instead of swallowing all throws; layout guards a transient throw so the page doesn't 500. `activityLog` insert errors handled; login redirect restricted to 401/403 to avoid bouncing on transient API errors.

- **CN provincial people's congress elections + provincial governor elections live.** Provincial NPC delegate elections resolve on a one-party model from provincial representatives up to the national NPC. Provincial governor elections are now scheduled at bootstrap. CN seed tightened to 7 CCP NPPs per region with real Reg + region-strength Org.

- **CN government type display + `onePartyState` enum.** Country overview surfaces the regime type explicitly; runtime code reads from the `governmentType` enum rather than a hardcoded check.

- **Pending treasury approval slots no longer corrupt on save.** Undefined approval slots were persisted as BSON null; slots are now omitted when not yet filled.

- **Charters link in mobile hamburger menu** + clickable invite notification + Nation-dropdown entry correctly hidden when not applicable.

- **`heal-sandbox-cron-stall.mjs` script** to catch up the game clock after a verified cron silence event.

- **Coalition chair sync** when the lead party's chair changes through any path (committee-vote removal, retirement, ban cascade, admin reassignment) — not just regular party-leadership elections.

- **Charter race against `expireCharters` sweep** guarded by status precondition on `replaceFounder`.

- **Charter founder identity moved from `userId` to `characterId`** so multi-character users can hold founder slots through different characters.

- **Bond issuance: 2× equity check false positive on non-anchor corps.** Form was sending raw local-currency amount where server expects ₳ (#1699). Min-validity guard also corrected.

- **Diminishing-returns curve re-enabled for primary NPI reach** (had been accidentally disabled). Reg-entrenchment curves softened for tier-3 calibration.

- **Runtime Mongo database name respected** when configured (#1697).

- **LandingClientBits `SERIF` token.** Added missing `SERIF` constant to `LandingClientBits.tsx` (was referenced by `BetaCountdown` but never declared).

- **CN map era-aware rendering.** The China regional map now drops Hong Kong and Macau from the SOUTH region for game time before the HK handover (July 1, 1997), correcting the 1991 preset's anachronistic display of those territories as part of the PRC. New `public/cn-regions-1991.json` generated by `scripts/build-country-geojson.ts`; `UnifiedMapWithModes` fetches `/api/game/turn/status` and the CN renderer in `countryMapConfigs.tsx` picks the era-appropriate GeoJSON via the new `isPreHKHandover()` helper. Taiwan is intentionally untouched (separate scope). Macau is shown as part of the PRC from 1997-07 onward — a 2.5-year visual inaccuracy vs. its actual 1999-12 handover, accepted to avoid a third GeoJSON file. (`docs/plans/2026-05-28-cn-region-map-1991.md`)

- **CN macro-region IDs renamed to Pinyin codes.** `NORTHEAST → DB`, `NORTH → HB`, `EAST → HD`, `CENTRAL → HZ`, `SOUTH → HN`, `SOUTHWEST → XN`, `NORTHWEST → XB`. URLs, internal lookups, seeds, tests, and historical seat tables all migrated. Reset required for existing CN games — old `homeState`, `state`, and `stateId` references won't match the new IDs.

- **DE Bremen `_id` renamed `HB → BRE` to resolve a primary-key collision with CN Huabei.** MongoDB requires `_id` to be unique within a collection; both states previously seeded to `_id: "HB"` and the second seed silently overwrote the first across `states`, `stateBudgets`, `stateMetrics`, `stateDemographics`, `stateDemographicTurnout`, `stateBaselines`, `regionalBudgets`, and `statePartyOrg`. Bremen now uses the 3-letter `BRE` everywhere internally; the map label and ISO 3166-2:DE-HB association are preserved. Reset required for existing DE games — characters with `homeState: "HB"` lose their Bremen reference until reseeded. Affects 17 DE-specific files (seeds, maps, constants, tests).

- **Country-aware state-lookup scoping.** 80+ `states.findOne` call sites and ~10 lookups across `stateBudgets`, `regionalBudgets`, `stateMetrics`, `stateDemographics`, `stateDemographicTurnout`, and `statePartyOrg` now filter by both `_id`/`stateId` AND `countryId` to prevent cross-country state-ID collisions from silently returning the wrong row. `createCharacterSchema`, `adminPartyOrgPatchSchema`, `adminDemographicsPatchSchema`, and the extraction-contract `grantSchema` now require `countryId`; `relocateBodySchema` accepts optional `targetCountryId` for cross-country moves. 8 internal helpers (`calculateStateRevenue`, `calculateStateSpending`, `validateStateBudgetImpact`, `computeStateTickRates`, `validateStatePartyElectionAccess`, `processStateFiscalYear`, `getRegionState`, `getSeatData`) gained a `countryId` parameter, and 5 UI consumers (`CharacterCreator`, `DemographicsManager`, `PartyOrgManager`, `CeoAdminSubtab`, `GrantContractModal`) updated to pass it.

- **`STATE_DISPLAY_NAMES`, `STATE_SECTOR_WEIGHT_OVERRIDES`, and `STATE_RESOURCE_CAPACITY` restructured to be countryId-keyed.** Previously single-namespace `Record<string, ...>` maps; collisions across countries (e.g. DE HB / CN HB) silently masked one entry. Now nested by country or use `${countryId}:${stateId}` compound keys. `getStateDisplayName(countryId, stateId)` is the new lookup signature.

## [0.3.0] - 2026-05-24

This release closes out the multi-month "political system rework" branch (264 commits, 550 files, ~52K net lines) plus the upstream cron-platform migration. The political-system rework introduces the Reg/Org/Support data model, Political Strength reserves and pressure ladder, the §7.3.2 swing-flow general-election formula, primary-tier expansion to every chamber, Party Charters, the State Overview / State Politics / Battleground / Persuasion / Registration UI shell, UK Devolution, country-aware regional executives, the 2019 game-start migration and 1991 alternate preset, and a comprehensive Committee Proposal / Treasury / Priority Region governance pass on top of the rework.

### Added (political system rework — phases 0-7)

- **CN region banner images.** All 7 Chinese macro-regions now have representative landmark photos on their region pages: Dongbei (Saint Sophia Cathedral, Harbin), Huabei (Great Wall), Huadong (Oriental Pearl Tower, Shanghai), Huazhong (Yellow Crane Tower, Wuhan), Huanan (Li River, Guilin), Xinan (Leshan Giant Buddha), Xibei (Terracotta Army, Xi'an). `src/lib/constants/cn.ts`, `src/lib/constants/regionBanner.ts`.

- **Reg / Org / Support data model.** New normalized per-state factors that feed `voteDistribution` and the §7.3.2 general-election formula. Registration is a per-party slow drift; Organization is per-state party presence; Support is sticky cycle-to-cycle voter loyalty. `orgRegLedger` provides an append-only audit trail. `src/lib/electionEngine/electionFormulaFactors.ts`, `src/lib/db/types/orgRegLedger.ts`.

- **State Overview tab.** New first tab on every state page: KPI strip, all-party Org pie, Political / Economy / Primary / Watchlist cards, hot-race classifier, country-aware regional-exec chip. Server-side aggregator + pure view-model builder.

- **State Politics tab.** Reworked panel with per-state party rows, partyBudget plumbing, hot-race surfacing, empty/disabled-state copy.

- **Political Strength (PS) reserve.** `actionPool → politicalStrength` rename with new collection. PS reserve generation with soft-cap slowdown bands, per-geography pressure ladder, shared `spendPoliticalStrength` command. Drift / decay validator + priority-region scaffold. National Agenda banner + Party Hub PS reserve card. `src/lib/parties/commands/spendPoliticalStrength.ts`, `src/lib/turn/politicalStrength/`.

- **Primary election tier surfaces.** TierSelector chip strip (Pres / Sen / Gov / House / SState), PrimaryCalendar timeline, CarveUpPanel demographic carve-up. `src/components/elections/primary/`.

- **Election formula rewrite (§7.3.2 swing-flow).** Pairwise-peel general-election model. Drivers activated: share-weighted incumbency, money, coattails, support, tribal-voter directional bonus. Per-state median voter for state races; EV-weighted national median for US president. Diff-test harness keeps margins within ±10pt of legacy. `src/lib/electionEngine/voteDistributionSwingFlow.ts`, `src/lib/electionEngine/persuasionDrivers.ts`.

- **Battleground / Persuasion / Registration cards.** BattlegroundMap with per-candidate hover cards, PersuasionDrivers driver-breakdown card, RegistrationInfluenceCard, generalViewModel server builder. `src/components/elections/general/`.

- **Non-presidential Campaign Manager.** CM eligibility extended to US Senate / Governor / House / State Senate with race-family-aware copy and strength gates. Per-race-family budget scalars on income / upgrades / maintenance: Pres 1.0× / Gov·Sen·Commons 0.5× / Landtag·MP 0.4× / House·Regional 0.3× / State Senate 0.2×. `src/lib/campaigns/upgradeCosts.ts`.

- **Party Charter system.** 3-of-3 founder cosign required for new parties. `PartyCharter` schema, lifecycle commands (`draftCharter`, `signCharter`, `rejectCharter`, `replaceFounder`, `ratifyCharter`, `expireCharters`), API routes, wizard UI at `/charters`. Migration script (`migrateExistingParties`) synthesizes ratified charters for existing 3+-human parties; under-staffed migrations get cleanup-immunity until founder slots fill. Preset-gated default-party names reserved at draft time. Ban cascade on charter founders. `src/lib/charters/`, `src/app/charters/`.

- **F4 founding cohort.** 3 NPPs spawn at ratification: 1 in chair's home state with charter positions + 2 player-picked via `FoundingCohortPicker` (adjacency-validated, same-state double-up allowed). True-believer personality profile (loyalty 75-95, ambition 40-55). Favorability tracks chair ± 5. `src/lib/charters/ratifyCharter.ts`.

- **Charter founder → role mapping.** Founder 1 → Chair, Founder 2 → Vice Chair, Founder 3 → Treasurer at ratification. Labels surfaced in draft form, signing page, ratification notifications. `replaceFounder` preserves slot positions so the mapping survives founder swaps.

- **`STATE_ADJACENCY` consolidated to one source of truth.** Per-country adjacency map (US 51 + DC w/ AK↔WA + MI↔WI; UK 12 w/ NIR↔SCO + NIR↔NWE ferries; DE 16; JP 8 w/ Tsugaru / Awaji / Seto / Kanmon sea bridges; CN 7 macro-regions). `adjacentStates(country, id)` API. `src/lib/constants/stateAdjacency.ts`.

- **Committee Proposal overhaul.** 60%-of-filled-roles threshold with abstain-as-nay at expiry. 4-axis positionShift (economic / social / foreignPolicy / culture) with 336-turn per-axis cooldown. Type cooldowns of 96 turns for rename / merge / electionMethod / electionDuration / removeOfficeHolder / transactionApprovalMode. Chair OR VC OR committee proposer eligibility. Duplicate-open guard. `src/lib/parties/proposals.ts`, `src/lib/parties/proposalConstants.ts`.

- **Remove Office Holder proposal.** Vote to vacate chair / VC / committee member seats. Target excluded from voting on own removal. Race-protection filter on resolver. `removeOfficeHolder` payload on `CommitteeProposal`. `src/lib/parties/proposals.ts:applyRemoveOfficeHolderEffect`.

- **Vice-chair acts as chair when chair vacant.** `canActAsChair`, `getActingChairId`, `isViceChairActing` helpers. VC inherits Chair Office access, parliamentary PM proposal, coalition actions, etc. when `chairId === null`. "Chair Office (acting)" tab label + acting banner. Sweep of all chair-only auth gates. `src/lib/parties/actingChair.ts`.

- **Treasury two-person approval.** Send-to-Member + Transfer-to-State require Treasurer + Chair/VC co-sign in "double" mode (default). New `pendingTreasuryTransactions` collection. `transactionApprovalMode` CommitteeProposal toggles single ↔ double (96-turn cooldown). Pending Transactions card above the audit log with Approve/Cancel actions. Per-turn expiry sweep (48 turns). Admin bypass preserved. Default-party seeds + charter ratification both default to "double". `src/lib/parties/pendingTreasuryTransactions.ts`.

- **Member-initiated Request Funds.** Any party member can request campaign funds from the party treasury. Single mode → 1 of {Treasurer, Chair, VC} approves; double mode → Treasurer + (Chair OR VC). Requester always excluded from approving own request, even if they hold an officer seat. `approvalModeAtPropose` recorded on the row so mid-flight mode toggles don't change the rules.

- **Per-role Treasury management gating.** Treasury Plan visible to all officers but only editable by Treasurer + admin (Chair/VC see read-only). Tax visible to all officers but only editable by Chair/VC + admin (Treasurer sees read-only). GOTV / Suppression / PS Investment hidden from Treasurer entirely (chair-level controls). `canManageTax` / `canManageBudgets` flags threaded through `TreasuryPanel`.

- **Priority Region cluster.** Chair-tunable 2-3 (or 4 with Governor anchor) connected adjacent states. 168-turn lockout once set. +25% effect bonus on Build Org + Contest in cluster states. Multi-country state-executive lookup powers the Governor anchor (US governor / DE Land Minister-President / JP prefectural governor / UK devolved First Minister / Mayor of London). Adjacency-aware picker auto-dims non-adjacent options as picks accumulate; "Gov" badges on anchor-eligible states. `src/lib/parties/priorityRegion.ts`.

- **Unified PS-investment lever.** Single chair-tunable `psInvestmentBudget` (explicit cash spend, expensive rate US national $250K/+1 PS, cap +4 PS/turn) + fixed 5% baseline trickle (cheap rate $75K/+1 PS, doesn't debit). Replaces the prior two-control system.

- **UK Devolution.** 5-phase system: `independenceDesire` + `devolutionPolicy` schema/constants, per-turn drift engine, Devolution tab + policy-change API, soft electoral hook (stepped pro-indy election bonus at desire ≥ 60). Reform UK gated to 2019-default; UUP gated to 1991-default. `src/lib/uk/devolution/`.

- **UK / DE / JP regional executives.** Country-aware `getRegionalExecutive(db, country, state)` resolves per-country office mapping (US governor / DE ministerPresident / JP governor / UK devolved-region governor with FM / Mayor labels). Perpetual elections for UK First Ministers, Mayor of London, JP prefectural governors, DE Minister-Presidents synced to Landtag. State-bills routed through the country-aware exec for assent. State-overview chips + banner images. `src/lib/states/regionalExecutive.ts`.

- **2019 game start.** Migrated from 2020 to Jan 2019. Preset-aware year everywhere — clock, elections, market, turn engine, larpDate. Cycle anchors corrected (1991 reset elects in 1992; 2019 reset elects in 2020). 2020-preset renamed to 2019-default.

- **1991-default preset.** Alternative game start with historical seat data. UUP / PDS / JSP / DSP as 1991-only default parties; Reform UK / AfD / Linke / CDP / Ishin / DPFP as 2019-only. Preset-aware regional Org seeding for UK / JP / DE. Sub-national 1991 NPPs seeded. Historical seat slugs corrected for DE / JP / UK 1990-1992.

- **DE_LANDTAG_2020 dataset.** 201 seat-holdings across all 16 Bundesländer with realistic post-2019 partisan splits. Local lists (FW Bayern, SSW, BVB) folded into `de_independent`. Hamburg uses 2015 pre-game-start data. `src/lib/constants/historicalSeats.ts`.

- **Org/Reg model cleanup.** Defender-conditional Org floor (undefended parties chip to 0); previous tier-scaled floor removed. Passive Org decay halved (0.25 → 0.125 pp/turn). Per-party Org cap clamp dropped — state-wide pool-sum is the only ceiling. Momentum field + cap-barrier UI retired everywhere. Reg-drift lag below Org by country-specific gap.

- **Auto-pause on cron silence.** Replaces the prior game-clock-offset detection (which conflated DST and tab-throttling with cron silence). The simulation now auto-pauses when the cron service goes silent for too long, surfacing the reason to moderators. PR #1688. `src/lib/turn/turnLogQueries.ts`, `src/lib/turnSystem.ts`, `src/lib/cron/backupFireGuard.ts`.

- **Cron platform migrated from Vercel to Railway.** `vercel.json` removed. Self-hosted `node-cron` schedule via `src/lib/cron.ts`. New `backupFireGuard` catches double-fires from concurrent cron drivers.

- **Visual polish (Phase 7a).** PS-spend animation keyframes + halo glow on Build Org + Contest tiles. Pre-click cost+gain preview routes. Per-candidate hover cards on BattlegroundMap. Political Strength stat in state + national party headers. PoliticalSummaryCard buttons converted to inline actions.

- **Nation dropdown entry to `/charters`.** New "Party Charters" link in the Nation dropdown next to "Political Parties".

### Changed (political system rework — phases 0-7)

- **`positionShift` axes expanded from economic / social to all four (economic / social / foreignPolicy / culture).** Each axis carries its own 336-turn cooldown. Legacy 2-axis party rows treat absent fields as neutral 0.

- **`Party.priorityRegion` schema field now wired end-to-end.** Previously declared but unused; this release adds the setter route, lockout enforcement, action-handler integration, anchor lookup, and Chair Office UI.

- **`STATE_ADJACENCY` is now the single source of truth for state neighbor lookups.** Previous duplicate `src/lib/states/adjacency.ts` retired. AK↔WA action cost changed from DISTANT to NEIGHBORING by design.

- **`spendPoliticalStrength` is the canonical PS-debit + pressure-ladder helper.** All direct-spend routes (`/build-org`, `/contest`) call it; pressure is escalated per-(party, state) and the `partyPoliticalStrengthLedger` audit row is written atomically.

- **PartyCharterAmendment scaffolding retired** in favor of CommitteeProposal-based amendments. `AMENDMENT_MAX_DELTA_PER_AXIS` + `validateAmendmentDeltas` removed.

- **Charter founder identity moved from `userId` to `characterId`** so multi-character users can hold multiple founder slots through different characters.

- **Default-party seeds + charter ratification both default to `transactionApprovalMode: "double"`.** Legacy rows without the field read as "double" at the route layer too.

- **Per-state median voter feeds through to PersuasionDrivers UI.** State median for state races; EV-weighted national median for US president.

### Fixed (political system rework — phases 0-7)

- **NaN-recovery in `applyPositionShiftEffect`.** Pre-redesign code could persist NaN when a position field was absent (`clampPosition(undefined, 1) = NaN`). Subsequent shifts re-read NaN, re-wrote NaN — the proposal "passed" but the position never moved. `Number.isFinite` guard now recovers stuck rows. Regression test added.

- **`ensureDefaultParties` skips seed when a non-default party already holds the name.** Prevents duplicate-name rows when a player creates a custom party under one preset that later becomes a preset-gated default on switch.

- **Charter draft now rejects preset-reserved default-party names + abbreviations.** Even when the gated default isn't currently seeded for the active preset.

- **Bulk discord-bot sync-roles route null-guards shareholder lookup** when a discord-linked character holds no party (post-purge or just-joined state).

- **`processNationalCommitteeElections` runs both `expireOpenProposals` and `expirePendingTransactions`** alongside the existing committee-election processing in the per-turn cadence.

- **Primary-calendar "In X turns" relative to current turn,** not the wave-table T-X offset.

- **Tier-primary**: stable per-state colors + district hover names + meaningful state-leader table. Rank candidates by projected `primaryScore` when no votes cast yet.

- **Tier-primary-pages distinguish "no contest" / "pre-primary" / "leader resolved".**

- **Election-detail surfaces primary-map pills for every tier + when no one has filed.**

- **`useGameTurnStatus` hoisted above early returns to satisfy Rules of Hooks.**

- **State-bills route through country-aware regional executive for assent** (was hardcoded to US governor).

- **UK region**: FM/Mayor chip + Office button in hero header.

- **UK devolution**: backfill `independenceDesire` on stale DBs.

- **Reset**: `party_<country>` counter collision when adding preset-only party.

- **Reset**: seed preset-only parties before historical NPP seeding.

- **Charter race against `expireCharters` sweep** is guarded by status precondition on the `replaceFounder` update.

- **Cross-country founder check** added at charter draft time (F5 closeout).

- **President primary renders empty state instead of 404 when no candidates filed.**

- **Tier-primary house**: silenced React key warning + force drilldown remount on state change.

- **Tier-primary back button uses generic `BackButton`** instead of hardcoded "back to presidential primary".

- **State-overview**: regional-exec chip uses party abbreviation; party-id mapping + state-id casing corrected.

- **Year-display**: preset-aware year everywhere (clock, elections, market, turn engine).

- **Admin clock**: `startingYear` passed to `turnToLarpDate`.

- **Metric projection**: surface devolution drift as `tickRate` for chart.

- **Bond issuance: 2× equity check false-positive for non-anchor corps.** The bond-issuance form was sending the raw typed amount in the corp's local currency, but the server expects ₳. For corps whose local currency has a rate > 1 (weaker than ₳), the local amount exceeds the ₳ equivalent, causing the debt-ceiling check to fire even when the intended issuance was within the allowed limit. The submit handler now converts to ₳ via `toInternalFrom` before posting. The minimum-validity guard (for the projected-impact preview) was also corrected to check the ₳ threshold. Bug #1698. `src/components/corporation/BondsTab.tsx`.

- **Region hero**: country-aware banner image on state/region pages.

- **Wiki-scenarios**: party-display map updated for 1991/2019 era slugs.

- **Chinese region display names**: `STATE_DISPLAY_NAMES` now uses Pinyin names (Dongbei, Huabei, Huadong, Huazhong, Huanan, Xinan, Xibei) instead of generic English labels (Northeast, North, East, Central, South, Southwest, Northwest), consistent with the seed data in `cnRegions.ts`.

### Added (whips / close-corps / economics)

- **House & Senate Majority/Minority Whip elections.** Whip roles (`majority_whip`, `minority_whip`) are now electable positions in both chambers, resolved automatically on the same schedule as their corresponding leader elections. Whips are authorized to issue party whips in their respective chambers. `src/lib/db/types/leadership.ts`, `src/app/api/congress/house-leadership/route.ts`, `src/app/api/congress/senate-leadership/route.ts`, `src/lib/congress/leadershipElections.ts`, `src/lib/partyWhips/constraints.ts`.

- **Close-corporation private share invitations.** CEOs of corporations with shareholder-capped legal structures (e.g. US S-Corp, capped at 100) can invite specific characters to purchase private shares at a set price per share. New `CorporationShareInvite` type, `/api/corporation/[id]/private-invites` routes, and `ManagePrivateShareholdersPanel` UI. `src/lib/db/types/corporationShareInvite.ts`, `src/app/api/corporation/[id]/private-invites/route.ts`, `src/components/corporation/ManagePrivateShareholdersPanel.tsx`.

- **`maxShareholders` field on `LegalStructure`** to model close-corporation caps. `src/lib/constants/legalStructures.ts`.

- **Dynamic `wageGrowth` recalculation.** Inflation turn processing now computes a `wageGrowth` target from unemployment vs NAIRU and GDP growth, smoothing toward it each turn instead of leaving it frozen at seed values. `src/lib/turn/inflationRecalc.ts`.

- **`minValue: -10`, `maxValue: 15` bounds on `gdpGrowth` metric definition.** `src/lib/metrics/metricDefinitions.ts`.

### Changed (whips / close-corps / economics)

- **Inflation inertia increased to 0.35** (was 0.2). Added mean-reversion toward 2% target (`MEAN_REVERSION_COEFF = 0.08`). Single-turn delta clamped to ±1.5pp to prevent cliffs. `src/lib/budget/inflation.ts`.

- **GDP growth `prevSectorBaseline` initialization fixed** from `prevGdpGrowth` to `rawGdpGrowth` so the first policy delta is computed correctly instead of zeroed out. Policy delta capped at ±8 to prevent permanent lock-in from pre-metric-clamp cabinet orders. `src/lib/turn/gdpGrowth.ts`.

- **Ministerial order effects capped at ±0.05pp per metric per turn** to prevent fully-staffed cabinets from stacking unlimited modifiers and producing 26%+ runaway growth. `src/lib/turn/ministerialOrderProcessing.ts`.

- **Committee election term durations now respect `party.customElectionDurationTurns`**, matching national leadership elections. `src/lib/nationalCommitteeElections.ts`.

- **Committee / party election entry routes now return user-friendly messages** — "voting has ended, refresh for next cycle" instead of generic 400 errors.

### Fixed (whips / close-corps / economics)

- **Missing `share_invite_*` notification types** added to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES`.
- **Missing `corpHeldShares` field** added to `Corporation` interface.
- **`getLegalStructureForCorp` parameter type mismatch** resolved.

### Removed

- **`vercel.json`** — Vercel cron declaration replaced by Railway `node-cron`.

- **`/api/country/[code]/parties/[id]/ps-investment-pct`** + state-scope sibling — the dual-control PS-investment system was unified into a single chair-tunable lever.

- **`PartyCharterAmendment` type + `AMENDMENT_MAX_DELTA_PER_AXIS` + `validateAmendmentDeltas`** — replaced by CommitteeProposal-based amendments.

- **Momentum field + cap-barrier UI** — retired per the Org/Reg rework.

- **Per-party Org cap clamp** — state-wide pool-sum is the only ceiling.

- **Tier-scaled Org floor on Contest** — defender-conditional floor only; undefended parties chip to 0.

### Ops

- **264 commits / 550 files / +58,398 / -5,570 from the political-system-update branch.**

- **6708 tests pass** (up from 6335 at start of branch).

- **Architecture audit clean** (0 blocking findings).

- **Scope docs landed** under `docs/superpowers/plans/`: treasury two-person approval, amendments-via-CommitteeProposal, F4 founding cohort redesign, VC-acting-chair + remove office holder, things-left walkthrough continuation, subnational 1991 NPP seeding, swing-flow implementation + driver activation + driver tuning + per-state median voter + tribal recalibration + cross-race ticket splitting (future), CN regional budget. Deferrals doc covers #5 non-US CM scope and #12 Address × Reg drift for the next branch.

### Added (post-cutover)

- **CN Provincial People's Congress elections.** One-party-model election resolves NPC delegates from provincial representatives. `oneparty_primaryWinners` groundwork and NPC + provincial congress wired through all election-type consumers. `src/lib/elections/cn/`. (`484e494`, `c917152`)

- **CN governor elections + bootstrap scheduling.** Provincial governor elections created at bootstrap. `ca55f4a`.

- **CN seed tightening.** 7 CCP NPPs per region, realistic registration values, region-strength org seeding. (`ea488da`, `192b12f`)

- **Government type display + `onePartyState` enum.** `governmentType` field displayed in country UI. `onePartyState` variant added to the `GovernmentType` enum to gate CN-specific election resolution. `b760468`.

- **Persuasion drivers card on non-presidential general elections.** Extended from presidential-only to Senate, Governor, House, and State Senate generals. `c861ea3`. `src/components/elections/general/PersuasionDriversCard.tsx`.

- **Observability.** Pre-OOM heap watchdog (`3d5af97`). `unhandledRejection` / `uncaughtException` forwarded to Sentry before process exit (`83b77cd`). Sentry monitor check-in wired on turn cron (`a77b909`). Heal-sandbox-cron-stall script to manually catch the game clock up after cron silence (`5c234ea`).

### Changed (post-cutover)

- **`governmentType` drives 8 regime-specific country fields.** `isParliamentary`, `hasPrimeMinister`, `hasPresident`, `primaryWinnerLogic`, etc. are now derived from `governmentType` instead of being set per-country. `refactor(country): derive 8 regime-driven fields from governmentType` (`e97bf0a`). `src/lib/constants/countryHelpers.ts`.

- **Primary winner routing split on `governmentType`.** Parliamentary vs. presidential primary resolution now branches on the enum value instead of per-country hardcoding. `refactor(country): drive primary winners off governmentType; split parliamentary` (`4278d20`).

### Fixed (post-cutover)

- **1991-default full audit.** Silent failures in bundle loading, election scheduling, and party bootstrapping resolved. (#1720, `6fd8e8f`). `src/lib/seed/presets/`.

- **Primary NPI reach diminishing-returns curve re-enabled.** Had been accidentally disabled; `calcNpiReach` now applies the curve. `fix(elections): re-enable diminishing-returns curve for primary npi reach` (`5410d34`).

- **Registration entrenchment curves in primaries softened.** T3 calibration coefficients over-tuned; reduced so registration shares respond more naturally. `fix(elections): soften reg entrenchment curves (t3 calibration)` (`030e53e`).

- **Treasury approval slots no longer persisted as BSON null.** Undefined slots stored as `null` in MongoDB broke approval-check comparisons; they are now omitted. `fix(treasury): stop persisting undefined approval slots as BSON null` (`f371b9e`).

- **Charter mobile hamburger menu link.** "Party Charters" was absent from the mobile nav. `fix(charters): show charter link in mobile hamburger menu` (`0c2dbf1`).

- **Charter invite notification now clickable + dropdown entry gated.** Invite notifications now link to the correct charter detail page; Nation dropdown entry correctly suppressed when inactive. `fix(charters): clickable invite notification + gate dropdown entry` (`f0cc164`).

- **Auth: activityLog insert errors handled; login redirects restricted to 401/403.** (`b7b97ca`)

- **Auth: JOSE error boundary.** `verifyAuth` now returns `null` only on token-bad JOSE errors; other throws propagate. Layout guard added so transient auth throws don't 500 the page. (`eea9f72`, `c3c3132`)

- **Auth cookie env-prefixed.** Cookie name is now prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_ENVIRONMENT` to prevent sandbox/live session collision. `fix(auth): env-prefix the auth-token cookie name` (`6ba5121`).

### Ops (post-cutover)

- **Cron stuck-lock recovery.** Stale `isProcessing: true` locks cleared by stale-check fall-through on next fire. `fix(cron): recover from stuck isProcessing locks` (`e695692`).

- **Deploy: `output: standalone` dropped** so Railway instrumentation hooks run. Switched `railway.toml` `startCommand` from standalone binary to `next start`. (`5f63f01`, `45b48ad`)

- **Sentry monitor slug derived from `RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME`** to avoid environment-name collisions. (`692f125`)

- **258 more files / +12,948 / -1,166 shipped post-cutover** (whips, close-corps, economics, CN system, government-type refactor, 1991 audit, observability, auth hardening, deploy fixes).

